{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_011UHcXiwe19KXTuTPZJdehQ","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t// Package orchestrate decides which agent mg-jdi should run next for a job,\n2\t// or whether it should stop — the agent-sequence orchestration logic.\n3\t//\n4\t// Next is a pure function of state already visible on disk/in git: a job's\n5\t// job.Stage(), how many verdict commits exist on its branch\n6\t// (git.CountVerdictCommits), and whether the branch tip is itself one of\n7\t// those verdict commits (git.LatestCommitIsVerdict). No new state file is\n8\t// needed, and mg-jdi never has to trust in-memory state across loop\n9\t// iterations — every decision re-derives cleanly from what's already\n10\t// committed, so re-running mg-jdi against the same job after it was killed\n11\t// mid-loop is always safe.\n12\tpackage orchestrate\n13\t\n14\timport (\n15\t\t\"github.com/lmuskalla/manigot/internal/agents\"\n16\t\t\"github.com/lmuskalla/manigot/internal/job\"\n17\t)\n18\t\n19\t// Kind is what mg-jdi should do next.\n20\ttype Kind int\n21\t\n22\tconst (\n23\t\t// RunAgent means launch the agent named in Decision.Agent next.\n24\t\tRunAgent Kind = iota\n25\t\t// StopFinished means the job's verdict is APPROVED — mg-jdi's work is\n26\t\t// done (it does not auto-merge; see the brief's completion criteria).\n27\t\tStopFinished\n28\t\t// StopNeedsHuman means mg-jdi cannot safely continue unattended and\n29\t\t// must hand control back to a human.\n30\t\tStopNeedsHuman\n31\t)\n32\t\n33\tfunc (k Kind) String() string {\n34\t\tswitch k {\n35\t\tcase RunAgent:\n36\t\t\treturn \"run-agent\"\n37\t\tcase StopFinished:\n38\t\t\treturn \"stop-finished\"\n39\t\tcase StopNeedsHuman:\n40\t\t\treturn \"stop-needs-human\"\n41\t\tdefault:\n42\t\t\treturn \"unknown\"\n43\t\t}\n44\t}\n45\t\n46\t// Decision is Next's result.\n47\ttype Decision struct {\n48\t\t// Kind is what to do.\n49\t\tKind Kind\n50\t\t// Agent is set only when Kind == RunAgent: \"analyst\", \"developer\", or\n51\t\t// \"reviewer\" — see Sequence.\n52\t\tAgent string\n53\t\t// Reason is a short, human-readable explanation, always set — surfaced\n54\t\t// in mg-jdi's own log/status output.\n55\t\tReason string\n56\t}\n57\t\n58\t// Sequence is the fixed, uniform mg-jdi agent order: the same for every job.Type — feature, fix, and chore all drive\n59\t// through analyst → developer → reviewer. Not branched on Job.Type anywhere\n60\t// in this package. Keyed off the agents package constants so a rename breaks\n61\t// the build.\n62\tvar Sequence = []string{agents.Analyst, agents.Developer, agents.Reviewer}\n63\t\n64\t// AgentTargetFile maps each driven agent (Sequence) to the job file it's\n65\t// expected to have written by the time its invocation returns — mg-jdi's\n66\t// output-dedup check reads this file fresh off disk (via j.Dir) to compare\n67\t// against the agent's own response text. Kept next to Sequence so the two\n68\t// can't drift.\n69\tvar AgentTargetFile = map[string]string{\n70\t\tagents.Analyst:   \"tasks.md\",\n71\t\tagents.Developer: \"implementation.md\",\n72\t\tagents.Reviewer:  \"verdict.md\",\n73\t}\n74\t\n75\t// Next decides what mg-jdi should do next for a job.\n76\t//\n77\t// verdictRounds is git.CountVerdictCommits(root, branch, jobID) — how many\n78\t// \"[ID] verdict: …\" commits exist on the job's branch. latestCommitIsVerdict\n79\t// is git.LatestCommitIsVerdict(root, branch, jobID) — whether the branch tip\n80\t// is itself one of those commits, which is what lets StageImplement's two\n81\t// very different situations be told apart: a rejection @developer hasn't\n82\t// responded to yet (tip is the verdict commit) versus one it already has\n83\t// (something newer sits on top of it).\n84\tfunc Next(stage job.Stage, verdictRounds int, latestCommitIsVerdict bool) Decision {\n85\t\tswitch stage {\n86\t\tcase job.StageDefine:\n87\t\t\t// brief.md itself isn't written — writing it is not any of the three\n88\t\t\t// driven agents' job (a human fills in brief.md; @analyst starts\n89\t\t\t// from it, per agents/analyst.md).\n90\t\t\treturn Decision{Kind: StopNeedsHuman, Reason: \"brief.md is not written yet\"}\n91\t\n92\t\tcase job.StagePlan:\n93\t\t\treturn Decision{Kind: RunAgent, Agent: agents.Analyst, Reason: \"brief.md written, tasks.md not written yet\"}\n94\t\n95\t\tcase job.StageReview:\n96\t\t\treturn Decision{Kind: RunAgent, Agent: agents.Reviewer, Reason: \"implementation.md written, verdict.md not written yet\"}\n97\t\n98\t\tcase job.StageFinished:\n99\t\t\treturn Decision{Kind: StopFinished, Reason: \"verdict.md's Overall verdict is APPROVED\"}\n100\t\n101\t\tcase job.StageImplement:\n102\t\t\tswitch {\n103\t\t\tcase verdictRounds == 0:\n104\t\t\t\t// Either the very first pass (tasks.md written, implementation.md\n105\t\t\t\t// not yet), or a job that started implementation but hasn't been\n106\t\t\t\t// reviewed at all yet — either way, it's @developer's turn.\n107\t\t\t\treturn Decision{Kind: RunAgent, Agent: agents.Developer, Reason: \"tasks.md written, no verdict yet\"}\n108\t\t\tcase verdictRounds >= 2:\n109\t\t\t\t// Two rejections already happened (a third could only exist if\n110\t\t\t\t// job.Stage() were still StageImplement after a second rejection,\n111\t\t\t\t// which is exactly this case) — the one-bounce retry budget is\n112\t\t\t\t// exhausted regardless of what's on the tip. The Reason leads\n113\t\t\t\t// with \"needs human: review cycle\" so this deliberate\n114\t\t\t\t// developer/reviewer back-and-forth stop reads distinctly from a\n115\t\t\t\t// crash.\n116\t\t\t\treturn Decision{Kind: StopNeedsHuman, Reason: \"needs human: review cycle — retry budget exhausted: 2 verdict commits already, still not approved\"}\n117\t\t\tcase latestCommitIsVerdict:\n118\t\t\t\t// Exactly 1 verdict commit, and it's still the tip: @reviewer\n119\t\t\t\t// rejected once and @developer hasn't responded yet. This is the\n120\t\t\t\t// one allowed bounce.\n121\t\t\t\treturn Decision{Kind: RunAgent, Agent: agents.Developer, Reason: \"reviewer rejected once, bouncing back to developer (1 of 1 allowed retries)\"}\n122\t\t\tdefault:\n123\t\t\t\t// Exactly 1 verdict commit, but something newer sits on top of\n124\t\t\t\t// it: @developer already committed a fix since that verdict —\n125\t\t\t\t// time to re-review, not another developer bounce.\n126\t\t\t\treturn Decision{Kind: RunAgent, Agent: agents.Reviewer, Reason: \"developer has committed since the last verdict, re-review\"}\n127\t\t\t}\n128\t\n129\t\tdefault:\n130\t\t\treturn Decision{Kind: StopNeedsHuman, Reason: \"unknown stage: \" + string(stage)}\n131\t\t}\n132\t}\n133\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"5af88135-9c7b-453b-b119-c245e62a60fb","uuid":"fc04f9ad-353c-418e-a102-47e8b0928c35","timestamp":"2026-08-28T20:16:35.819Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/workspace/src/internal/orchestrate/orchestrate.go","content":"// Package orchestrate decides which agent mg-jdi should run next for a job,\n// or whether it should stop — the agent-sequence orchestration logic.\n//\n// Next is a pure function of state already visible on disk/in git: a job's\n// job.Stage(), how many verdict commits exist on its branch\n// (git.CountVerdictCommits), and whether the branch tip is itself one of\n// those verdict commits (git.LatestCommitIsVerdict). No new state file is\n// needed, and mg-jdi never has to trust in-memory state across loop\n// iterations — every decision re-derives cleanly from what's already\n// committed, so re-running mg-jdi against the same job after it was killed\n// mid-loop is always safe.\npackage orchestrate\n\nimport (\n\t\"github.com/lmuskalla/manigot/internal/agents\"\n\t\"github.com/lmuskalla/manigot/internal/job\"\n)\n\n// Kind is what mg-jdi should do next.\ntype Kind int\n\nconst (\n\t// RunAgent means launch the agent named in Decision.Agent next.\n\tRunAgent Kind = iota\n\t// StopFinished means the job's verdict is APPROVED — mg-jdi's work is\n\t// done (it does not auto-merge; see the brief's completion criteria).\n\tStopFinished\n\t// StopNeedsHuman means mg-jdi cannot safely continue unattended and\n\t// must hand control back to a human.\n\tStopNeedsHuman\n)\n\nfunc (k Kind) String() string {\n\tswitch k {\n\tcase RunAgent:\n\t\treturn \"run-agent\"\n\tcase StopFinished:\n\t\treturn \"stop-finished\"\n\tcase StopNeedsHuman:\n\t\treturn \"stop-needs-human\"\n\tdefault:\n\t\treturn \"unknown\"\n\t}\n}\n\n// Decision is Next's result.\ntype Decision struct {\n\t// Kind is what to do.\n\tKind Kind\n\t// Agent is set only when Kind == RunAgent: \"analyst\", \"developer\", or\n\t// \"reviewer\" — see Sequence.\n\tAgent string\n\t// Reason is a short, human-readable explanation, always set — surfaced\n\t// in mg-jdi's own log/status output.\n\tReason string\n}\n\n// Sequence is the fixed, uniform mg-jdi agent order: the same for every job.Type — feature, fix, and chore all drive\n// through analyst → developer → reviewer. Not branched on Job.Type anywhere\n// in this package. Keyed off the agents package constants so a rename breaks\n// the build.\nvar Sequence = []string{agents.Analyst, agents.Developer, agents.Reviewer}\n\n// AgentTargetFile maps each driven agent (Sequence) to the job file it's\n// expected to have written by the time its invocation returns — mg-jdi's\n// output-dedup check reads this file fresh off disk (via j.Dir) to compare\n// against the agent's own response text. Kept next to Sequence so the two\n// can't drift.\nvar AgentTargetFile = map[string]string{\n\tagents.Analyst:   \"tasks.md\",\n\tagents.Developer: \"implementation.md\",\n\tagents.Reviewer:  \"verdict.md\",\n}\n\n// Next decides what mg-jdi should do next for a job.\n//\n// verdictRounds is git.CountVerdictCommits(root, branch, jobID) — how many\n// \"[ID] verdict: …\" commits exist on the job's branch. latestCommitIsVerdict\n// is git.LatestCommitIsVerdict(root, branch, jobID) — whether the branch tip\n// is itself one of those commits, which is what lets StageImplement's two\n// very different situations be told apart: a rejection @developer hasn't\n// responded to yet (tip is the verdict commit) versus one it already has\n// (something newer sits on top of it).\nfunc Next(stage job.Stage, verdictRounds int, latestCommitIsVerdict bool) Decision {\n\tswitch stage {\n\tcase job.StageDefine:\n\t\t// brief.md itself isn't written — writing it is not any of the three\n\t\t// driven agents' job (a human fills in brief.md; @analyst starts\n\t\t// from it, per agents/analyst.md).\n\t\treturn Decision{Kind: StopNeedsHuman, Reason: \"brief.md is not written yet\"}\n\n\tcase job.StagePlan:\n\t\treturn Decision{Kind: RunAgent, Agent: agents.Analyst, Reason: \"brief.md written, tasks.md not written yet\"}\n\n\tcase job.StageReview:\n\t\treturn Decision{Kind: RunAgent, Agent: agents.Reviewer, Reason: \"implementation.md written, verdict.md not written yet\"}\n\n\tcase job.StageFinished:\n\t\treturn Decision{Kind: StopFinished, Reason: \"verdict.md's Overall verdict is APPROVED\"}\n\n\tcase job.StageImplement:\n\t\tswitch {\n\t\tcase verdictRounds == 0:\n\t\t\t// Either the very first pass (tasks.md written, implementation.md\n\t\t\t// not yet), or a job that started implementation but hasn't been\n\t\t\t// reviewed at all yet — either way, it's @developer's turn.\n\t\t\treturn Decision{Kind: RunAgent, Agent: agents.Developer, Reason: \"tasks.md written, no verdict yet\"}\n\t\tcase verdictRounds >= 2:\n\t\t\t// Two rejections already happened (a third could only exist if\n\t\t\t// job.Stage() were still StageImplement after a second rejection,\n\t\t\t// which is exactly this case) — the one-bounce retry budget is\n\t\t\t// exhausted regardless of what's on the tip. The Reason leads\n\t\t\t// with \"needs human: review cycle\" so this deliberate\n\t\t\t// developer/reviewer back-and-forth stop reads distinctly from a\n\t\t\t// crash.\n\t\t\treturn Decision{Kind: StopNeedsHuman, Reason: \"needs human: review cycle — retry budget exhausted: 2 verdict commits already, still not approved\"}\n\t\tcase latestCommitIsVerdict:\n\t\t\t// Exactly 1 verdict commit, and it's still the tip: @reviewer\n\t\t\t// rejected once and @developer hasn't responded yet. This is the\n\t\t\t// one allowed bounce.\n\t\t\treturn Decision{Kind: RunAgent, Agent: agents.Developer, Reason: \"reviewer rejected once, bouncing back to developer (1 of 1 allowed retries)\"}\n\t\tdefault:\n\t\t\t// Exactly 1 verdict commit, but something newer sits on top of\n\t\t\t// it: @developer already committed a fix since that verdict —\n\t\t\t// time to re-review, not another developer bounce.\n\t\t\treturn Decision{Kind: RunAgent, Agent: agents.Reviewer, Reason: \"developer has committed since the last verdict, re-review\"}\n\t\t}\n\n\tdefault:\n\t\treturn Decision{Kind: StopNeedsHuman, Reason: \"unknown stage: \" + string(stage)}\n\t}\n}\n","numLines":133,"startLine":1,"totalLines":133}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01QxgxQNmvmpuADeHnMudr7d","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t// Package launch spawns manigot agent sessions in a new terminal window or\n2\t// pane so the TUI can keep running while the agent works.\n3\t//\n4\t// Spawn order (macOS + Linux; no Windows v1):\n5\t//  1. Inside tmux — when the TUI is itself running inside tmux (detected via\n6\t//     the $TMUX env var, with a tmux binary on PATH), this works the same on\n7\t//     every platform. The session is opened as a split pane in the TUI's\n8\t//     current window (`tmux split-window), replacing the pane manigot opened\n9\t//     last so at most one manigot pane exists at a time (the replace policy).\n10\t//     Inside tmux this branch always wins — even when\n11\t//     config.Settings.Terminal is set — so a session launched from a TUI\n12\t//     that is itself inside tmux lands in a tmux pane, never in a separate\n13\t//     window.\n14\t//  2. config.Settings.Terminal, when set: the explicit user-chosen terminal\n15\t//     (e.g. \"kitty\") is invoked directly — see buildOverrideCmd. It applies\n16\t//     only when the TUI is NOT inside tmux (or tmux is not available there);\n17\t//     inside tmux the split pane above wins.\n18\t//  3. macOS Terminal.app via osascript.\n19\t//  4. A Linux terminal emulator, tried in order: gnome-terminal, ptyxis\n20\t//     (Fedora's default GNOME terminal since Fedora 41), x-terminal-emulator\n21\t//     (Debian), konsole, xterm.\n22\t//\n23\t// The command opened is always `<mg-binary> --agent <agent> --job <jobID>` run\n24\t// from the project root, matching the session launcher's invocation contract.\n25\t// <mg-binary> is the running binary's own path (os.Executable) — the one Go\n26\t// binary IS the command now — so an install under a different name still\n27\t// works.\n28\tpackage launch\n29\t\n30\timport (\n31\t\t\"errors\"\n32\t\t\"fmt\"\n33\t\t\"io\"\n34\t\t\"os\"\n35\t\t\"os/exec\"\n36\t\t\"runtime\"\n37\t\t\"strings\"\n38\t\t\"sync\"\n39\t\n40\t\t\"github.com/lmuskalla/manigot/internal/config\"\n41\t)\n42\t\n43\t// Agent opens a new terminal — inside tmux, a split pane in the TUI's current\n44\t// window (see launchTmuxPane) — that runs manigot with `--profile <profile>\n45\t// --agent <agent> --job <jobID>` in projectRoot. profile is one of the\n46\t// subscription profiles in config.Profiles; an empty value defaults to\n47\t// config.ProfileClaudePro, matching the session launcher's own default. terminal is\n48\t// config.Settings.Terminal: when non-empty and the TUI is NOT inside tmux, that\n49\t// terminal is invoked directly instead of the auto-detect chain below — see\n50\t// buildOverrideCmd; inside tmux the split pane wins regardless of this setting.\n51\t// Empty falls back to the configured override itself (TerminalSetting), so\n52\t// callers that don't have the setting loaded — CLI paths like `mg done`'s\n53\t// git-solver handoff — still honor it; only when the setting is also unset does\n54\t// the auto-detect chain apply. It returns a short\n55\t// human description of where it opened (e.g. \"tmux pane\", \"Terminal.app\",\n56\t// \"gnome-terminal\", or the override's own binary name) so the caller can surface\n57\t// it in a status line.\n58\t//\n59\t// The launcher runs from projectRoot (the main worktree) even though the job\n60\t// lives in its own worktree: the session launcher\n61\t// (session.ResolveRootFrom) re-derives the effective mount root itself from\n62\t// the --job it is given (branch-match + worktree lookup, hard error on a\n63\t// worktree-less job), so launching from projectRoot is not just sufficient\n64\t// but required — it locates the project from $PWD and only then resolves the\n65\t// job's worktree. The same reasoning keeps Quick/AgentQuick (no --job at all, mount\n66\t// PROJECT_ROOT directly) and Jdi (mg-jdi, which re-derives per invocation)\n67\t// on projectRoot.\n68\t//\n69\t// The launcher process is detached: its stdio is discarded so it cannot\n70\t// corrupt the TUI's alt screen, and it is reaped asynchronously. The one\n71\t// exception is the tmux path, where the split-window client is run\n72\t// synchronously (see launchTmuxPane) so its pane-id output can be captured\n73\t// for the replace policy — split-window itself returns in milliseconds, it\n74\t// does not wait for the agent session.\n75\t//\n76\t// cmd.Start() failing (the launcher binary itself couldn't be\n77\t// spawned — e.g. permission denied) is already surfaced: it's returned as an\n78\t// error here and shown by the caller (App.updateDetail via cmdErrorText). The\n79\t// remaining gap is a launcher that starts successfully but then fails on its\n80\t// own after Agent has already returned \"success\" to the caller — e.g.\n81\t// gnome-terminal/konsole exiting non-zero because there's no display server.\n82\t// Surfacing that would need either blocking on cmd.Wait() here or wiring the\n83\t// reaping goroutine back into a tea.Msg, and neither is safe to add: xterm\n84\t// (unlike gnome-terminal/konsole/tmux/osascript, which detach quickly) *is*\n85\t// the window process, so it doesn't return until the window closes — for it,\n86\t// Wait()-ing here would block Update() for the whole agent session, which is\n87\t// strictly worse than the bug being fixed. holdOnFailure\n88\t// already covers the failure mode the brief actually reports (the inner `sc\n89\t// --agent` command failing fast); a launcher-binary-itself failure is a\n90\t// narrower, rarer case left uncovered rather than risk reintroducing UI lag.\n91\tfunc Agent(agent, jobID, projectRoot, profile, terminal string) (string, error) {\n92\t\texe, err := ExeOverride()\n93\t\tif err != nil {\n94\t\t\treturn \"\", fmt.Errorf(\"locate mg binary: %w\", err)\n95\t\t}\n96\t\tinner := shellCommand(exe, agent, jobID, projectRoot, profile)\n97\t\treturn launchDetached(inner, terminal)\n98\t}\n99\t\n100\t// Quick opens a new terminal — inside tmux, a split pane in the TUI's current\n101\t// window, exactly like Agent, including the replace policy — that runs manigot\n102\t// with just `--profile <profile>` (no --agent, no --job) in projectRoot: a\n103\t// bare session for an ad-hoc change that doesn't belong to any specific\n104\t// job/agent workflow. profile is one of the subscription profiles in\n105\t// config.Profiles; an empty value defaults to config.ProfileClaudePro,\n106\t// matching Agent. terminal is config.Settings.Terminal, threaded through to\n107\t// launchDetached exactly like Agent's own terminal parameter — see Agent's\n108\t// doc for the override semantics. It returns the same short human description\n109\t// of where it opened so the caller can surface it in a status line.\n110\t//\n111\t// The session launcher treats --agent and --job as optional, so a bare `sc\n112\t// --profile <profile>` simply runs claude/opencode against the current project\n113\t// with no agent flag and no job prompt.\n114\tfunc Quick(projectRoot, profile, terminal string) (string, error) {\n115\t\texe, err := ExeOverride()\n116\t\tif err != nil {\n117\t\t\treturn \"\", fmt.Errorf(\"locate mg binary: %w\", err)\n118\t\t}\n119\t\tinner := quickShellCommand(exe, projectRoot, profile)\n120\t\treturn launchDetached(inner, terminal)\n121\t}\n122\t\n123\t// AgentQuick opens a new terminal — inside tmux, a split pane in the TUI's\n124\t// current window, exactly like Agent/Quick, including the replace policy —\n125\t// that runs manigot with `--profile <profile> --agent <agent>` (no --job) in\n126\t// projectRoot: launching a specific agent for an ad-hoc session that doesn't\n127\t// belong to any particular job. profile is one of the subscription profiles\n128\t// in config.Profiles; an empty value defaults to config.ProfileClaudePro,\n129\t// matching Agent/Quick. terminal is config.Settings.Terminal, threaded\n130\t// through to launchDetached exactly like Agent/Quick's own terminal\n131\t// parameter — see Agent's doc for the override semantics. It returns the\n132\t// same short human description of where it opened so the caller can surface\n133\t// it in a status line.\n134\t//\n135\t// The session launcher treats --job as optional (see Quick's own doc for the\n136\t// --agent/--job-less case), so `sc --profile <profile> --agent <agent>`\n137\t// simply runs that agent against the current project with no job prompt.\n138\tfunc AgentQuick(agent, projectRoot, profile, terminal string) (string, error) {\n139\t\texe, err := ExeOverride()\n140\t\tif err != nil {\n141\t\t\treturn \"\", fmt.Errorf(\"locate mg binary: %w\", err)\n142\t\t}\n143\t\tinner := agentQuickShellCommand(exe, agent, projectRoot, profile)\n144\t\treturn launchDetached(inner, terminal)\n145\t}\n146\t\n147\t// HostAgent opens a new terminal — inside tmux, a split pane in the TUI's\n148\t// current window, exactly like Agent/Quick/AgentQuick, including the replace\n149\t// policy — that runs `mg host --profile <profile> --agent <agent> --prompt\n150\t// '<prompt>'` in projectRoot: a host-mode agent session (the agent CLI runs\n151\t// directly on the host, unisolated — see `mg host`) with an explicit prompt.\n152\t// prompt is the free-form instruction the agent starts with (the session\n153\t// launcher passes it through verbatim); it is what `mg done` uses to hand a\n154\t// failed squash merge to @git-solver, telling it what broke and what cleanup\n155\t// remains. profile is one of the subscription profiles in config.Profiles;\n156\t// an empty value defaults to config.ProfileClaudePro, matching Agent/Quick.\n157\t// terminal is config.Settings.Terminal, threaded through to launchDetached\n158\t// exactly like the other launch paths' own terminal parameter — see Agent's\n159\t// doc for the override semantics. It returns the same short human description\n160\t// of where it opened so the caller can surface it in a status line.\n161\t//\n162\t// Unlike Agent/AgentQuick there is no --job: host-mode sessions run against\n163\t// the project root itself — the main worktree, where a failed `mg done`\n164\t// squash merge leaves its conflicted state — never a job worktree.\n165\tfunc HostAgent(agent, prompt, projectRoot, profile, terminal string) (string, error) {\n166\t\texe, err := ExeOverride()\n167\t\tif err != nil {\n168\t\t\treturn \"\", fmt.Errorf(\"locate mg binary: %w\", err)\n169\t\t}\n170\t\tinner := hostAgentShellCommand(exe, agent, prompt, projectRoot, profile)\n171\t\treturn launchDetached(inner, terminal)\n172\t}\n173\t\n174\t// Tig opens a new terminal — inside tmux, a split pane in the TUI's current\n175\t// window, exactly like Agent/Quick/AgentQuick, including the replace policy —\n176\t// that runs `mg diff '<jobName>' --tig` in projectRoot: the host-side tig TUI\n177\t// browsing what the job's branch changed (the CLI path already exists —\n178\t// cmd/mg/diff.go runTig resolves the job's branch + the project's base\n179\t// branch, builds the three-dot range <base>...<branch>, runs `tig <range>`,\n180\t// and errors clearly when tig is not installed). terminal is\n181\t// config.Settings.Terminal, threaded through to launchDetached exactly like\n182\t// the other launch paths' own terminal parameter — see Agent's doc for the\n183\t// override semantics. It returns the same short human description of where it\n184\t// opened so the caller can surface it in a status line.\n185\t//\n186\t// Unlike Agent/Quick there is no profile flag: `mg diff` is a plain host git\n187\t// command, not a session launch, so it takes no --profile (and no --agent or\n188\t// --job either).\n189\t//\n190\t// The job is passed by its Name (id_slug), not its ID: `mg diff` resolves the\n191\t// job's branch via an exact match on the branch's id_slug tail segment first,\n192\t// then a unique prefix (cmd/mg/diff.go resolveJobBranch) — and job.Name\n193\t// equals that tail segment exactly, so resolution is unambiguous.\n194\t//\n195\t// Tig re-checks availability itself (TigAvailable) before spawning: the\n196\t// TUI's cached availability gate (detailView.tigAvailable) is only as fresh\n197\t// as the last job open, so a stale cached \"available\" must surface a\n198\t// synchronous error here rather than opening a doomed pane that dies on the\n199\t// CLI's own tig-missing check.\n200\tfunc Tig(jobID, projectRoot, terminal string) (string, error) {\n201\t\tif !TigAvailable() {\n202\t\t\treturn \"\", errors.New(\"tig is not installed on the host — install it, or use the diff tab\")\n203\t\t}\n204\t\texe, err := ExeOverride()\n205\t\tif err != nil {\n206\t\t\treturn \"\", fmt.Errorf(\"locate mg binary: %w\", err)\n207\t\t}\n208\t\tinner := tigShellCommand(exe, jobID, projectRoot)\n209\t\treturn launchDetached(inner, terminal)\n210\t}\n211\t\n212\t// Tail opens a new terminal — inside tmux, a split pane in the TUI's current\n213\t// window, exactly like Agent/Quick/Tig, including the replace policy — that\n214\t// runs `tail -f '<logPath>'`: a live tail of the given absolute log file (the\n215\t// mg-jdi run.log sidecar, job.JDIRunLogPath) so a user can watch the currently\n216\t// running agent's output grow from inside the TUI. When jq resolves on the\n217\t// host (JqAvailable), the tail is piped through it (jqTailShellCommand) so\n218\t// the JSONL raw stream both agent CLIs write is pretty-printed as it scrolls\n219\t// by, with the mg-jdi section headers and blank lines passed through\n220\t// unchanged; when jq is missing, this silently falls back to the plain\n221\t// tail -f (tailShellCommand) — strictly no worse than before jq support\n222\t// existed. terminal is config.Settings.Terminal, threaded through to\n223\t// launchDetached exactly like the other launch paths' own terminal\n224\t// parameter — see Agent's doc for the override semantics. It returns the\n225\t// same short human description of where it opened so the caller can surface\n226\t// it in a status line.\n227\t//\n228\t// Unlike Agent/Quick/Tig there is no mg-binary hop at all: `tail -f` (piped\n229\t// through jq or not) is a plain host command, so there is no ExeOverride\n230\t// resolution and no profile flag. The inner command is deliberately NOT\n231\t// wrapped in holdOnFailure (the one launch path that deviates): a user ends\n232\t// a tail pane with Ctrl+C (exit 130, non-zero), so holding on a non-zero\n233\t// exit would leave a \"press enter to close\" prompt every single time — the\n234\t// pane should just close when the user is done watching.\n235\t//\n236\t// tail -f idles harmlessly once the underlying run ends (the file simply\n237\t// stops growing), so there is no liveness check here; the caller decides when\n238\t// the key and footer hint are reachable (e.g. gated on the log file existing).\n239\tfunc Tail(logPath, terminal string) (string, error) {\n240\t\tinner := tailShellCommand(logPath)\n241\t\tif JqAvailable() {\n242\t\t\tinner = jqTailShellCommand(logPath)\n243\t\t}\n244\t\treturn launchDetached(inner, terminal)\n245\t}\n246\t\n247\t// ExeOverride returns the path of the binary implementing the mg session\n248\t// subcommand — the running binary itself. An exported package-level variable\n249\t// so tests (in this package and internal/ui) can point Agent/Quick/AgentQuick\n250\t// at a stub or a resolution failure instead of the real os.Executable().\n251\tvar ExeOverride = func() (string, error) { return os.Executable() }\n252\t\n253\t// TerminalSetting returns the user's configured terminal override — the\n254\t// `terminal` field of config/tui-settings.json — or \"\" when unset or\n255\t// unreadable. It is the default every terminal-launch path falls back to when\n256\t// the caller passes an empty terminal (see launchDetached), so the setting is\n257\t// honored from the TUI — which passes it explicitly — and from CLI paths like\n258\t// `mg done`'s git-solver handoff alike, instead of silently degrading to\n259\t// auto-detect. An exported package-level variable, mirroring ExeOverride, so\n260\t// tests can stub it.\n261\tvar TerminalSetting = func() string {\n262\t\ts, err := config.Load()\n263\t\tif err != nil {\n264\t\t\treturn \"\"\n265\t\t}\n266\t\treturn s.Terminal\n267\t}\n268\t\n269\t// JdiExe returns the path of the binary implementing the `mg jdi` subcommand —\n270\t// the running binary itself. An exported package-level variable, mirroring\n271\t// ExeOverride, so tests (in this package and internal/ui) can point it at a\n272\t// stub or a resolution failure.\n273\tvar JdiExe = func() (string, error) { return os.Executable() }\n274\t\n275\t// TigLookPath resolves the tig binary on the host, mirroring `mg diff --tig`'s\n276\t// own check (cmd/mg/diff.go's tigLookPath). An exported package-level\n277\t// variable, mirroring ExeOverride/JdiExe, so tests (in this package and\n278\t// internal/ui) can point it at a stub or a tig-less PATH instead of requiring\n279\t// tig on the test machine.\n280\tvar TigLookPath = exec.LookPath\n281\t\n282\t// TigAvailable reports whether tig resolves on the host — the \"if available\"\n283\t// gate the TUI's `t` key and footer hint key off (see Tig's own doc for why\n284\t// the launch path re-checks this itself). It is deliberately a lightweight\n285\t// LookPath probe with no version check or config scan: `mg diff --tig` is the\n286\t// authoritative backstop and errors clearly on its own when tig is missing.\n287\tfunc TigAvailable() bool {\n288\t\t_, err := TigLookPath(\"tig\")\n289\t\treturn err == nil\n290\t}\n291\t\n292\t// JqLookPath resolves the jq binary on the host, mirroring TigLookPath's own\n293\t// tig-availability seam. An exported package-level variable, mirroring\n294\t// ExeOverride/JdiExe/TigLookPath, so tests (in this package and internal/ui)\n295\t// can point it at a stub or a jq-less PATH instead of requiring jq on the\n296\t// test machine.\n297\tvar JqLookPath = exec.LookPath\n298\t\n299\t// JqAvailable reports whether jq resolves on the host — the \"if available\"\n300\t// gate Tail keys its JSONL-pretty-printing pipe on (see Tail's own doc for\n301\t// why it falls back to a plain tail -f rather than erroring when jq is\n302\t// missing). Deliberately a lightweight LookPath probe with no version check,\n303\t// mirroring TigAvailable.\n304\tfunc JqAvailable() bool {\n305\t\t_, err := JqLookPath(\"jq\")\n306\t\treturn err == nil\n307\t}\n308\t\n309\t// Jdi starts `mg jdi --job <jobID> --profile <profile>` detached in the\n310\t// background — no spawned terminal window at all, unlike Agent/Quick.\n311\t// mg-jdi drives a fixed,\n312\t// non-interactive agent sequence with no TTY at any point in its own\n313\t// container invocations (the session launcher's --print flag) and needs no\n314\t// terminal for a human or a subprocess to attach to — spawning one anyway\n315\t// would be pure overhead and would reintroduce exactly the per-agent-window\n316\t// cost the backlog's \"in-TUI agent terminal\" idea is about removing, not\n317\t// adding to. Visibility into a TUI-launched run is the list-row status\n318\t// badge and the detail-view log tab, not a window; see those for how a\n319\t// human watches it run.\n320\t//\n321\t// profile is one of the subscription profiles in config.Profiles, matching\n322\t// Agent/Quick — an empty value defaults to config.ProfileClaudePro, mg-jdi's\n323\t// own default (see cmd/mg/jdi.go).\n324\t//\n325\t// cmd.Dir stays on projectRoot (the main worktree) even though the job lives\n326\t// in its own worktree: mg-jdi — like every other\n327\t// `--job` invocation — re-derives the effective worktree root itself from the\n328\t// job id it is given, per invocation, so it must be launched from the project\n329\t// root that find_project_root expects, never from a job worktree directly.\n330\t// See Agent's doc for the full reasoning, shared by all four launch paths.\n331\t//\n332\t// Unlike Agent/Quick there is no \"where it opened\" description to return —\n333\t// there is no terminal/pane to describe — so this returns only an error (nil\n334\t// on a successful start). Like launchDetached, the started process's stdio is\n335\t// discarded and it is reaped asynchronously so it cannot corrupt the TUI's\n336\t// alt screen or zombie; mg-jdi may run for a long time (several full agent\n337\t// sessions), so this must not block waiting for it.\n338\tfunc Jdi(jobID, projectRoot, profile string) error {\n339\t\tif profile == \"\" {\n340\t\t\tprofile = config.ProfileClaudePro\n341\t\t}\n342\t\texe, err := JdiExe()\n343\t\tif err != nil {\n344\t\t\treturn fmt.Errorf(\"locate mg binary: %w\", err)\n345\t\t}\n346\t\tcmd := exec.Command(exe, \"jdi\", \"--job\", jobID, \"--profile\", profile)\n347\t\tcmd.Dir = projectRoot\n348\t\tcmd.Stdin = nil\n349\t\tcmd.Stdout = io.Discard\n350\t\tcmd.Stderr = io.Discard\n351\t\tif err := cmd.Start(); err != nil {\n352\t\t\treturn fmt.Errorf(\"start mg jdi: %w\", err)\n353\t\t}\n354\t\tif cmd.Process != nil {\n355\t\t\tgo func() { _ = cmd.Wait() }()\n356\t\t}\n357\t\treturn nil\n358\t}\n359\t\n360\t// tmuxPaneTag is the pane title manigot tags the split panes it opens with\n361\t// (`tmux select-pane -t <id> -T`, run by launchTmuxPane after the split —\n362\t// split-window itself has no -T on any released tmux). It is how a later\n363\t// launch finds the pane manigot opened last so it can be replaced — see\n364\t// killPreviousTmuxPane. It lives in the pane title (in tmux, not in memory)\n365\t// so it survives a TUI restart.\n366\tconst tmuxPaneTag = \"manigot\"\n367\t\n368\t// tmuxMu serializes the find-kill-split sequence in launchTmuxPane so two\n369\t// concurrent launches (Agent/Quick can be invoked from Bubble Tea command\n370\t// goroutines) can never interleave: without it, both could list the same\n371\t// previous pane, both split, and the \"at most one manigot pane\" invariant\n372\t// would break. Also guards tmuxLastPaneID.\n373\tvar tmuxMu sync.Mutex\n374\t\n375\t// tmuxLastPaneID is the pane_id (e.g. \"%12\") of the most recent pane manigot\n376\t// opened via launchTmuxPane, or \"\" if none is known yet (e.g. the TUI was\n377\t// restarted after the pane was opened, or no launch has happened). Guarded by\n378\t// tmuxMu.\n379\tvar tmuxLastPaneID string\n380\t\n381\t// launchTmuxPane opens the manigot session as a split pane in the TUI's\n382\t// current tmux window and implements the \"replace\" reuse policy:\n383\t// before splitting, the pane manigot opened last is killed so at most one\n384\t// manigot pane exists at a time. It is the tmux-specific launch path — unlike\n385\t// the generic launchDetached tail it runs the split-window client\n386\t// synchronously because it needs its stdout (the new pane's id, via\n387\t// -P -F '#{pane_id}') to tag the pane with tmuxPaneTag (select-pane -T, since\n388\t// split-window has no -T on released tmux) and to record it for the next\n389\t// replace; split-window itself returns as soon as the pane exists, the inner\n390\t// command runs inside the pane, not in this client, so blocking on it is\n391\t// milliseconds and Output() captures stdout+stderr so nothing leaks onto the\n392\t// TUI's screen.\n393\tfunc launchTmuxPane(inner string) (string, error) {\n394\t\ttmuxMu.Lock()\n395\t\tdefer tmuxMu.Unlock()\n396\t\n397\t\t// Replace, then split. The replace is best-effort: a failure to find the\n398\t\t// previous pane must not prevent the new launch — if tmux is in a state\n399\t\t// where list-panes fails, the split-window below will fail too and its\n400\t\t// error is the accurate one to surface. The user having already closed\n401\t\t// the previous pane is not an error at all (tolerated inside).\n402\t\t_ = killPreviousTmuxPane()\n403\t\n404\t\tcmd, desc, err := buildCmd(inner, \"\")\n405\t\tif err != nil {\n406\t\t\treturn \"\", err\n407\t\t}\n408\t\tout, err := cmd.Output()\n409\t\tif err != nil {\n410\t\t\treturn \"\", fmt.Errorf(\"launch %s: %w\", desc, err)\n411\t\t}\n412\t\tpaneID := strings.TrimSpace(string(out))\n413\t\tif paneID == \"\" {\n414\t\t\treturn \"\", fmt.Errorf(\"launch %s: tmux split-window printed no pane id\", desc)\n415\t\t}\n416\t\n417\t\t// Tag the new pane so a later launch can find it even after a TUI restart\n418\t\t// (which loses tmuxLastPaneID). select-pane -T is supported by every\n419\t\t// released tmux, unlike split-window -T. Best-effort: the pane is already\n420\t\t// live and the in-memory id covers replacement within this TUI process, so\n421\t\t// a tagging failure only narrows the restart-surviving lookup — it must\n422\t\t// not abort a launch that already succeeded.\n423\t\t_ = exec.Command(\"tmux\", \"select-pane\", \"-t\", paneID, \"-T\", tmuxPaneTag).Run()\n424\t\n425\t\ttmuxLastPaneID = paneID\n426\t\treturn desc, nil\n427\t}\n428\t\n429\t// killPreviousTmuxPane implements the \"replace\" reuse policy: it kills\n430\t// the split pane manigot opened last so that at most one manigot pane exists\n431\t// at a time. The pane is identified two ways, both safe against ever killing\n432\t// a pane manigot did not itself open:\n433\t//\n434\t//   - tmuxLastPaneID — the pane_id learned from the previous split-window's\n435\t//     -P output; reliable while this TUI process lives.\n436\t//   - any pane in the current session whose title is tmuxPaneTag — set via\n437\t//     select-pane -T after the split (see launchTmuxPane); survives TUI\n438\t//     restarts because it lives in tmux, not in memory (a restart loses\n439\t//     tmuxLastPaneID).\n440\t//\n441\t// list-panes is scoped to the current session (-s): the manigot pane is split\n442\t// from the TUI's own pane, so it lives in the TUI's session (a tmux client\n443\t// run as a subprocess of the TUI resolves \"current session\" from $TMUX_PANE),\n444\t// and -s will also find it if the user moved it to another window of that\n445\t// session — without reaching into a different session where a different\n446\t// manigot TUI might legitimately have its own pane. A pane the user already\n447\t// closed in the meantime is not an error: that kill-pane failure is ignored.\n448\t// The kill is unconditional with respect to the pane's state — a pane mid-\n449\t// hold-on-failure is replaced exactly like a live session.\n450\t// Called with tmuxMu held.\n451\tfunc killPreviousTmuxPane() error {\n452\t\tcandidates := map[string]struct{}{}\n453\t\tif tmuxLastPaneID != \"\" {\n454\t\t\tcandidates[tmuxLastPaneID] = struct{}{}\n455\t\t}\n456\t\tout, err := exec.Command(\"tmux\", \"list-panes\", \"-s\", \"-F\", \"#{pane_id} #{pane_title}\").Output()\n457\t\tif err != nil {\n458\t\t\treturn fmt.Errorf(\"tmux list-panes: %w\", err)\n459\t\t}\n460\t\tfor _, line := range strings.Split(strings.TrimSpace(string(out)), \"\\n\") {\n461\t\t\tfields := strings.SplitN(line, \" \", 2)\n462\t\t\tif len(fields) == 2 && fields[1] == tmuxPaneTag {\n463\t\t\t\tcandidates[fields[0]] = struct{}{}\n464\t\t\t}\n465\t\t}\n466\t\tfor id := range candidates {\n467\t\t\t// Already closed by the user (or its window closed with it) → the\n468\t\t\t// replace is a no-op, which is the tolerated case.\n469\t\t\t_ = exec.Command(\"tmux\", \"kill-pane\", \"-t\", id).Run()\n470\t\t}\n471\t\treturn nil\n472\t}\n473\t\n474\t// launchDetached is the shared spawn/reap tail used by both launch paths: it\n475\t// builds the *exec.Cmd for inner, discards its stdio so the launcher cannot\n476\t// corrupt the TUI's alt screen, starts it, and reaps the launcher\n477\t// asynchronously so it doesn't zombie (the actual terminal/pane outlives it).\n478\t// It returns the short \"where it opened\" description buildCmd produces.\n479\t//\n480\t// terminal is config.Settings.Terminal: when non-empty it is used in place of\n481\t// the macOS/Linux auto-detect chain (branch 3/4 below) — but only when the TUI\n482\t// is NOT inside tmux. Inside tmux (with a tmux binary on PATH) the launch is\n483\t// handled by launchTmuxPane instead, regardless of the override: a user who\n484\t// runs the TUI inside tmux always gets the split-pane behavior, and the\n485\t// override applies only outside tmux.\n486\t//\n487\t// An empty terminal falls back to TerminalSetting — the user's configured\n488\t// override itself — before auto-detect, so every launch path honors the same\n489\t// setting even when the caller doesn't have it loaded (the TUI passes it\n490\t// explicitly; CLI paths like `mg done`'s git-solver handoff pass \"\" and get\n491\t// the setting here). Only when the setting is also unset does the auto-detect\n492\t// chain below apply. Inside tmux the terminal value is never consulted, so\n493\t// the resolution is harmless there — the tmux split pane wins regardless.\n494\t//\n495\t// Inside tmux, the launch is handled by launchTmuxPane: it implements the\n496\t// replace policy (killing the previously manigot-opened pane before splitting\n497\t// a new one) and needs the split-window client's stdout (the new pane's id),\n498\t// so it runs synchronously rather than through the detached tail below. The\n499\t// same tmux detection ($TMUX set and a tmux binary on PATH) is used here and\n500\t// in buildCmd.\n501\t//\n502\t// See Agent's doc comment for why a launcher that starts successfully but then\n503\t// fails on its own (e.g. no display server) is not surfaced here — holdOnFailure\n504\t// covers the inner command's own fast failures instead.\n505\tfunc launchDetached(inner, terminal string) (string, error) {\n506\t\t// The caller's explicit terminal wins; an empty one falls back to the\n507\t\t// user's configured override (TerminalSetting) so the setting is honored\n508\t\t// from every launch path, TUI and CLI alike. Auto-detect applies only when\n509\t\t// neither is set. Inside tmux the resolved value is never consulted — the\n510\t\t// tmux split pane below wins regardless.\n511\t\tif terminal == \"\" {\n512\t\t\tterminal = TerminalSetting()\n513\t\t}\n514\t\n515\t\t// Inside tmux with a tmux binary on PATH, the tmux split pane always wins —\n516\t\t// even when an override is set. The override applies only outside tmux.\n517\t\tif os.Getenv(\"TMUX\") != \"\" {\n518\t\t\tif _, err := exec.LookPath(\"tmux\"); err == nil {\n519\t\t\t\treturn launchTmuxPane(inner)\n520\t\t\t}\n521\t\t}\n522\t\n523\t\tcmd, desc, err := buildCmd(inner, terminal)\n524\t\tif err != nil {\n525\t\t\treturn \"\", err\n526\t\t}\n527\t\tcmd.Stdin = nil\n528\t\tcmd.Stdout = io.Discard\n529\t\tcmd.Stderr = io.Discard\n530\t\tif err := cmd.Start(); err != nil {\n531\t\t\treturn \"\", fmt.Errorf(\"launch %s: %w\", desc, err)\n532\t\t}\n533\t\t// Reap the launcher to avoid a zombie; the actual terminal/pane outlives it.\n534\t\tif cmd.Process != nil {\n535\t\t\tgo func() { _ = cmd.Wait() }()\n536\t\t}\n537\t\treturn desc, nil\n538\t}\n539\t\n540\t// shellCommand builds the shell string executed inside the new terminal:\n541\t//\n542\t//\tcd '<projectRoot>' && '<manigot>' --profile '<profile>' --agent '<agent>' --job '<jobID>'; ec=$?; ...\n543\t//\n544\t// cd-ing first matters because manigot finds the project root from $PWD (the\n545\t// same walk-up job.FindProjectRoot uses). manigotPath is the absolute path from the\n546\t// resolve package; it is quoted like every other value, so a checkout in a\n547\t// directory with spaces survives both osascript and `bash -lc`. Arguments are\n548\t// single-quoted and embedded single quotes are escaped, so no value can break\n549\t// out of its quotes. An empty profile defaults to config.ProfileClaudePro so the\n550\t// flag is always passed explicitly, regardless of what the session\n551\t// launcher's own default happens to be.\n552\t//\n553\t// None of buildCmd's five spawn paths keep the\n554\t// window/pane open once the inner command exits, success or failure — tmux\n555\t// destroys a new window as soon as its command exits (no remain-on-exit),\n556\t// Terminal.app's `do script` types the command but the window otherwise\n557\t// behaves normally, and gnome-terminal/x-terminal-emulator/konsole/xterm all\n558\t// close on exit by default. A fast failure (docker not running, resolve\n559\t// failure inside the container, an auth error) is therefore invisible: the\n560\t// window flashes and disappears before its output can be read — this is the\n561\t// brief's \"a window appears and it immediately closes again\". The result is\n562\t// wrapped in holdOnFailure so a non-zero exit pauses instead.\n563\tfunc shellCommand(manigotPath, agent, jobID, projectRoot, profile string) string {\n564\t\tif profile == \"\" {\n565\t\t\tprofile = config.ProfileClaudePro\n566\t\t}\n567\t\tinner := fmt.Sprintf(\"cd %s && %s --profile %s --agent %s --job %s\",\n568\t\t\tshellQuote(projectRoot), shellQuote(manigotPath), shellQuote(profile), shellQuote(agent), shellQuote(jobID))\n569\t\treturn holdOnFailure(inner)\n570\t}\n571\t\n572\t// quickShellCommand builds the shell string for a bare manigot session (no\n573\t// --agent, no --job), executed inside the new terminal:\n574\t//\n575\t//\tcd '<projectRoot>' && '<manigot>' --profile '<profile>'; ec=$?; ...\n576\t//\n577\t// It is the --agent/--job-less counterpart to shellCommand: same cd-first,\n578\t// shellQuote-everything, holdOnFailure-wrap behavior (so a fast failure of the\n579\t// inner command still holds the window open), just without the\n580\t// agent/job flags. An empty profile defaults to config.ProfileClaudePro for the\n581\t// same reason shellCommand does. It is deliberately a separate function rather\n582\t// than a generalization of shellCommand so the latter's exact-format tests stay\n583\t// unchanged.\n584\tfunc quickShellCommand(manigotPath, projectRoot, profile string) string {\n585\t\tif profile == \"\" {\n586\t\t\tprofile = config.ProfileClaudePro\n587\t\t}\n588\t\tinner := fmt.Sprintf(\"cd %s && %s --profile %s\",\n589\t\t\tshellQuote(projectRoot), shellQuote(manigotPath), shellQuote(profile))\n590\t\treturn holdOnFailure(inner)\n591\t}\n592\t\n593\t// agentQuickShellCommand builds the shell string for AgentQuick's jobless\n594\t// agent session (--agent, no --job), executed inside the new terminal:\n595\t//\n596\t//\tcd '<projectRoot>' && '<manigot>' --profile '<profile>' --agent '<agent>'; ec=$?; ...\n597\t//\n598\t// It sits between shellCommand (agent + job) and quickShellCommand (neither):\n599\t// same cd-first, shellQuote-everything, holdOnFailure-wrap behavior, just\n600\t// with --agent and no --job. An empty profile defaults to\n601\t// config.ProfileClaudePro for the same reason the other two do. Deliberately\n602\t// its own function rather than a generalization of either existing one, per\n603\t// the file's \"deliberately a separate function\" convention — keeps\n604\t// shellCommand/quickShellCommand's exact-format tests stable.\n605\tfunc agentQuickShellCommand(manigotPath, agent, projectRoot, profile string) string {\n606\t\tif profile == \"\" {\n607\t\t\tprofile = config.ProfileClaudePro\n608\t\t}\n609\t\tinner := fmt.Sprintf(\"cd %s && %s --profile %s --agent %s\",\n610\t\t\tshellQuote(projectRoot), shellQuote(manigotPath), shellQuote(profile), shellQuote(agent))\n611\t\treturn holdOnFailure(inner)\n612\t}\n613\t\n614\t// hostAgentShellCommand builds the shell string for HostAgent's host-mode\n615\t// agent session (--agent, --prompt, no --job), executed inside the new\n616\t// terminal:\n617\t//\n618\t//\tcd '<projectRoot>' && '<manigot>' host --profile '<profile>' --agent '<agent>' --prompt '<prompt>'; ec=$?; ...\n619\t//\n620\t// Same cd-first, shellQuote-everything, holdOnFailure-wrap behavior as the\n621\t// other launch paths, with the `host` subcommand inserted and the prompt\n622\t// passed via --prompt — the one launch path that carries a prompt. The prompt\n623\t// is a free-form multi-sentence instruction, so shellQuote's embedded-quote\n624\t// escaping matters here more than anywhere (see TestHostAgentShellCommandQuotesPrompt).\n625\t// An empty profile defaults to config.ProfileClaudePro for the same reason the\n626\t// other builders do. Deliberately its own function rather than a\n627\t// generalization of the other shell-command builders, per the file's\n628\t// \"deliberately separate function per launch path\" convention.\n629\tfunc hostAgentShellCommand(manigotPath, agent, prompt, projectRoot, profile string) string {\n630\t\tif profile == \"\" {\n631\t\t\tprofile = config.ProfileClaudePro\n632\t\t}\n633\t\tinner := fmt.Sprintf(\"cd %s && %s host --profile %s --agent %s --prompt %s\",\n634\t\t\tshellQuote(projectRoot), shellQuote(manigotPath), shellQuote(profile), shellQuote(agent), shellQuote(prompt))\n635\t\treturn holdOnFailure(inner)\n636\t}\n637\t\n638\t// tigShellCommand builds the shell string executed inside the new terminal:\n639\t//\n640\t//\tcd '<projectRoot>' && '<manigot>' diff '<jobID>' --tig; ec=$?; ...\n641\t//\n642\t// Same cd-first, shellQuote-everything, holdOnFailure-wrap behavior as the\n643\t// other launch paths — the job's files are resolved by `mg diff` from $PWD,\n644\t// so cd-ing first matters exactly as it does for Agent/Quick. No --profile\n645\t// flag: `mg diff` takes none (it is a host git command, not a session\n646\t// launch). Deliberately its own function rather than a generalization of the\n647\t// other shell-command builders, per the file's \"separate function per launch\n648\t// path\" convention — keeps their exact-format tests unchanged.\n649\tfunc tigShellCommand(manigotPath, jobID, projectRoot string) string {\n650\t\tinner := fmt.Sprintf(\"cd %s && %s diff %s --tig\",\n651\t\t\tshellQuote(projectRoot), shellQuote(manigotPath), shellQuote(jobID))\n652\t\treturn holdOnFailure(inner)\n653\t}\n654\t\n655\t// tailShellCommand builds the shell string executed inside the new terminal:\n656\t//\n657\t//\ttail -f '<logPath>'\n658\t//\n659\t// The log path is absolute (the caller passes job.JDIRunLogPath's output), so\n660\t// there is no cd-first like the other launch paths — and the inner command is\n661\t// deliberately NOT wrapped in holdOnFailure, the one deviation from the\n662\t// agent/quick/tig convention: a user ends `tail -f` with Ctrl+C (exit 130,\n663\t// non-zero), so holding on a non-zero exit would leave a \"press enter to\n664\t// close\" prompt every single time instead of just closing the pane. Same\n665\t// shellQuote-everything behavior as the other builders — a path with spaces\n666\t// or embedded quotes survives osascript and `bash -lc` as one word.\n667\t// Deliberately its own function rather than a generalization of the other\n668\t// shell-command builders, per the file's \"separate function per launch path\"\n669\t// convention.\n670\tfunc tailShellCommand(logPath string) string {\n671\t\treturn \"tail -f \" + shellQuote(logPath)\n672\t}\n673\t\n674\t// jqTailShellCommand builds the shell string executed inside the new\n675\t// terminal when jq is available on the host (see Tail's own doc):\n676\t//\n677\t//\ttail -f '<logPath>' | jq -R -r 'fromjson? // .'\n678\t//\n679\t// session.log is not pure JSON: mg-jdi writes `=== <RFC3339> <agent> (attempt\n680\t// N) ===` section headers and blank lines around each invocation's raw\n681\t// stream, and the raw stream itself is JSONL for both agent CLIs (opencode\n682\t// --format json; Claude --output-format stream-json). The filter reads each\n683\t// line raw (-R), tries to parse it as JSON (fromjson?, jq's \"try\" form —\n684\t// null/error on failure rather than aborting the whole pipeline) and falls\n685\t// back to the raw line (// .) when parsing fails, so the headers and blank\n686\t// lines pass through untouched while every JSONL line is pretty-printed\n687\t// (jq's default output format) as it scrolls by. -r prints the fallback raw\n688\t// lines without JSON-string quoting.\n689\t//\n690\t// Same shellQuote-everything behavior as tailShellCommand — a log path with\n691\t// spaces or embedded quotes survives osascript and `bash -lc` as one word.\n692\t// The jq filter itself is a fixed literal (no host input embedded in it), so\n693\t// it needs no quoting beyond the single-quoted shell argument it already is.\n694\tfunc jqTailShellCommand(logPath string) string {\n695\t\treturn \"tail -f \" + shellQuote(logPath) + ` | jq -R -r 'fromjson? // .'`\n696\t}\n697\t\n698\t// holdOnFailure wraps a shell command so that, if it exits non-zero, the\n699\t// window/pane it is running in stays open with the failure message visible\n700\t// instead of closing immediately. A clean exit (status 0) is left\n701\t// alone — the launched agent session usually runs interactively until the\n702\t// user ends it themselves, so closing right away once that happens is the\n703\t// expected behavior; only a fast, invisible failure needs holding open.\n704\t//\n705\t// Wrapping the shared inner command string (rather than reaching for a\n706\t// per-terminal mechanism like tmux's remain-on-exit or `xterm -hold`) keeps\n707\t// the fix uniform across all five spawn paths in buildCmd and lets it be\n708\t// exercised by ordinary string-based tests.\n709\t//\n710\t// The wrap behaves identically inside a tmux split pane as in a\n711\t// window — tmux runs the pane's shell-command in a pty and destroys the pane\n712\t// when the command exits, so the `read -r _ignored` blocking on a non-zero\n713\t// exit keeps the pane open with the failure message visible exactly as it\n714\t// held a new window open (the message may wrap in the narrower 35% pane, but\n715\t// stays fully visible). And because the replace policy treats every\n716\t// manigot pane alike, a pane mid-hold-on-failure is killed by a subsequent\n717\t// launch just like a live session is — deliberate and unconditional (the\n718\t// \"skip or warn?\" alternative was rejected: skipping would break\n719\t// the at-most-one-pane invariant and needs unfeasible mid-hold detection from\n720\t// outside the pane, and warning needs new TUI plumbing that is out of scope\n721\t// for this job). A user who launches something new has moved on from the\n722\t// failure they were present for when they launched it.\n723\tfunc holdOnFailure(cmd string) string {\n724\t\treturn cmd + `; ec=$?; if [ \"$ec\" -ne 0 ]; then echo; echo \"--- manigot: exited with status $ec ---\"; printf 'press enter to close... '; read -r _ignored; fi; exit \"$ec\"`\n725\t}\n726\t\n727\t// shellQuote wraps s in single quotes, escaping any embedded single quote via\n728\t// the standard '\\” idiom.\n729\tfunc shellQuote(s string) string {\n730\t\treturn \"'\" + strings.ReplaceAll(s, \"'\", `'\\''`) + \"'\"\n731\t}\n732\t\n733\t// terminalCandidates is the Linux terminal-emulator candidate table used both\n734\t// by buildCmd's auto-detect chain (branch 4) and, by name lookup, by\n735\t// buildOverrideCmd's known-name-vs-\"-e\"-fallback flag-convention choice:\n736\t// an override whose binary matches one of these names\n737\t// (case-insensitively) reuses that name's own flag convention instead of the\n738\t// \"-e\" default.\n739\tvar terminalCandidates = []struct {\n740\t\tname string\n741\t\tpre  []string // args between the emulator name and the shell command\n742\t}{\n743\t\t{\"gnome-terminal\", []string{\"--\"}},\n744\t\t{\"ptyxis\", []string{\"--\"}},\n745\t\t{\"x-terminal-emulator\", []string{\"-e\"}},\n746\t\t{\"konsole\", []string{\"-e\"}},\n747\t\t{\"xterm\", []string{\"-e\"}},\n748\t}\n749\t\n750\t// buildOverrideCmd constructs the *exec.Cmd for an explicit user-chosen\n751\t// terminal override (config.Settings.Terminal): it takes over the auto-detect\n752\t// spawn order — the tmux branch is only reachable inside tmux, where\n753\t// launchDetached/buildCmd prefer the tmux pane, so an override is invoked when\n754\t// the TUI is NOT inside tmux (or tmux is unavailable there), replacing the\n755\t// macOS/Linux auto-detect chain (see launchDetached).\n756\t//\n757\t// terminal's first whitespace-separated token is the binary to run; any\n758\t// remaining tokens are passed as leading arguments before the flag that hands\n759\t// off the inner shell command — mirroring how config.Settings.Editor (and\n760\t// $VISUAL/$EDITOR) already allow trailing args, e.g. \"code --wait\". The\n761\t// binary is looked up via exec.LookPath exactly like the auto-detect\n762\t// candidates in terminalCandidates, so a typo'd or missing binary surfaces a\n763\t// clear \"launch <name>: exec: ... file not found\" error here instead of\n764\t// silently falling back to auto-detect.\n765\t//\n766\t// The flag used to hand off the inner command is chosen the same way as the\n767\t// existing candidates: if the binary case-insensitively matches one of\n768\t// terminalCandidates' own names, that name's convention (\"--\" or \"-e\") is\n769\t// reused; otherwise \"-e\" is used, since it is the more common convention\n770\t// among terminals not already in that list (kitty, alacritty, wezterm, rxvt,\n771\t// terminator all accept it). This is a best-effort convention, not a\n772\t// guarantee for every possible terminal emulator — a known limitation, not\n773\t// solved generally, since a fully general solution would need a per-terminal\n774\t// flag field the brief did not ask for.\n775\tfunc buildOverrideCmd(inner, terminal string) (*exec.Cmd, string, error) {\n776\t\tfields := strings.Fields(terminal)\n777\t\tif len(fields) == 0 {\n778\t\t\treturn nil, \"\", fmt.Errorf(\"terminal override is blank\")\n779\t\t}\n780\t\tname := fields[0]\n781\t\tleadingArgs := fields[1:]\n782\t\n783\t\tif _, err := exec.LookPath(name); err != nil {\n784\t\t\treturn nil, \"\", fmt.Errorf(\"launch %s: %w\", name, err)\n785\t\t}\n786\t\n787\t\tflag := \"-e\"\n788\t\tfor _, c := range terminalCandidates {\n789\t\t\tif strings.EqualFold(c.name, name) {\n790\t\t\t\tflag = c.pre[0]\n791\t\t\t\tbreak\n792\t\t\t}\n793\t\t}\n794\t\n795\t\targs := append(append([]string{}, leadingArgs...), flag, \"bash\", \"-lc\", inner)\n796\t\treturn exec.Command(name, args...), name, nil\n797\t}\n798\t\n799\t// buildCmd constructs the *exec.Cmd for the detected environment, or — when\n800\t// terminal (config.Settings.Terminal) is non-empty and the TUI is not inside\n801\t// tmux — for the explicit override instead (see buildOverrideCmd). The tmux\n802\t// branch (1) is checked first and always wins when the TUI is inside tmux and\n803\t// a tmux binary is on PATH, even over an override; branches 2–4 (override,\n804\t// macOS Terminal.app, Linux emulators) run detached via launchDetached's\n805\t// spawn/reap tail. The tmux branch is also the command launchTmuxPane runs\n806\t// synchronously.\n807\tfunc buildCmd(inner, terminal string) (*exec.Cmd, string, error) {\n808\t\t// 1. Inside tmux (works on every OS that has tmux): split a pane off the\n809\t\t// TUI's current window, replacing the previously manigot-opened\n810\t\t// pane. Checked before the override so a session launched from a TUI\n811\t\t// inside tmux always lands in a tmux pane, regardless of\n812\t\t// config.Settings.Terminal.\n813\t\tif os.Getenv(\"TMUX\") != \"\" {\n814\t\t\tif _, err := exec.LookPath(\"tmux\"); err == nil {\n815\t\t\t\t// -h splits side by side (vertical divider); -l 35% sizes the new\n816\t\t\t\t// pane to 35% of the window width so the TUI's own pane (run\n817\t\t\t\t// with no explicit -t, so this targets the TUI's own currently\n818\t\t\t\t// active pane) keeps the majority share.\n819\t\t\t\t// -l (not the older -p) is required: tmux deprecated -p's\n820\t\t\t\t// percentage sizing in 3.1 in favor of -l with a \"%\" suffix, and\n821\t\t\t\t// tmux 3.4 rejects -p outright with \"size missing\" (surfaced to\n822\t\t\t\t// the user as the split-window client exiting 1) instead of\n823\t\t\t\t// splitting.\n824\t\t\t\t// -P -F '#{pane_id}' makes the split-window client print the new\n825\t\t\t\t// pane's id to its stdout so launchTmuxPane can tag it with\n826\t\t\t\t// tmuxPaneTag (via select-pane -T — split-window has no -T on any\n827\t\t\t\t// released tmux) and record it for the replace policy.\n828\t\t\t\t//\n829\t\t\t\t// The trailing \"bash\", \"-lc\", inner (three separate argv elements,\n830\t\t\t\t// not one combined string) matters: given a single shell-command\n831\t\t\t\t// argument, tmux runs it via the pane's default-shell in -c mode,\n832\t\t\t\t// which is non-login and non-interactive — .zshrc/.bash_profile are\n833\t\t\t\t// never sourced, so any PATH customization living there (docker,\n834\t\t\t\t// a manigot install dir, nvm, homebrew, ...) is invisible to it and\n835\t\t\t\t// the inner command fails fast (caught by holdOnFailure, but the\n836\t\t\t\t// session never actually starts). Passing multiple argv elements\n837\t\t\t\t// instead makes tmux exec them directly with no shell in between,\n838\t\t\t\t// so this becomes an ordinary `bash -lc inner` invocation — the\n839\t\t\t\t// exact login-shell wrapping every other spawn path below already\n840\t\t\t\t// uses (see shellArgs), just constructed inline since tmux's\n841\t\t\t\t// argv-vs-single-string parsing is what selects shell-vs-direct-exec\n842\t\t\t\t// here, unlike a plain exec.Command call.\n843\t\t\t\treturn exec.Command(\"tmux\", \"split-window\", \"-h\", \"-l\", \"35%\",\n844\t\t\t\t\t\"-P\", \"-F\", \"#{pane_id}\", \"bash\", \"-lc\", inner), \"tmux pane\", nil\n845\t\t\t}\n846\t\t}\n847\t\n848\t\t// 2. The explicit terminal override (config.Settings.Terminal), when the\n849\t\t// TUI is not inside tmux (or tmux is unavailable there).\n850\t\tif terminal != \"\" {\n851\t\t\treturn buildOverrideCmd(inner, terminal)\n852\t\t}\n853\t\n854\t\t// 3. macOS Terminal.app.\n855\t\tif runtime.GOOS == \"darwin\" {\n856\t\t\tscript := fmt.Sprintf(`tell application \"Terminal\" to do script %q`, inner)\n857\t\t\treturn exec.Command(\"osascript\", \"-e\", script), \"Terminal.app\", nil\n858\t\t}\n859\t\n860\t\t// 4. Linux terminal emulators, in order of preference.\n861\t\tshellArgs := []string{\"bash\", \"-lc\", inner}\n862\t\tfor _, c := range terminalCandidates {\n863\t\t\tif _, err := exec.LookPath(c.name); err == nil {\n864\t\t\t\targs := append(append([]string{}, c.pre...), shellArgs...)\n865\t\t\t\treturn exec.Command(c.name, args...), c.name, nil\n866\t\t\t}\n867\t\t}\n868\t\n869\t\treturn nil, \"\", fmt.Errorf(\"no supported terminal launcher found (looked for: tmux, Terminal.app, gnome-terminal, ptyxis, x-terminal-emulator, konsole, xterm)\")\n870\t}\n871\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"5af88135-9c7b-453b-b119-c245e62a60fb","uuid":"f4d6f1e8-0200-4c0d-99e1-a4f2efc90787","timestamp":"2026-08-28T20:16:35.834Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/workspace/src/internal/launch/launch.go","content":"// Package launch spawns manigot agent sessions in a new terminal window or\n// pane so the TUI can keep running while the agent works.\n//\n// Spawn order (macOS + Linux; no Windows v1):\n//  1. Inside tmux — when the TUI is itself running inside tmux (detected via\n//     the $TMUX env var, with a tmux binary on PATH), this works the same on\n//     every platform. The session is opened as a split pane in the TUI's\n//     current window (`tmux split-window), replacing the pane manigot opened\n//     last so at most one manigot pane exists at a time (the replace policy).\n//     Inside tmux this branch always wins — even when\n//     config.Settings.Terminal is set — so a session launched from a TUI\n//     that is itself inside tmux lands in a tmux pane, never in a separate\n//     window.\n//  2. config.Settings.Terminal, when set: the explicit user-chosen terminal\n//     (e.g. \"kitty\") is invoked directly — see buildOverrideCmd. It applies\n//     only when the TUI is NOT inside tmux (or tmux is not available there);\n//     inside tmux the split pane above wins.\n//  3. macOS Terminal.app via osascript.\n//  4. A Linux terminal emulator, tried in order: gnome-terminal, ptyxis\n//     (Fedora's default GNOME terminal since Fedora 41), x-terminal-emulator\n//     (Debian), konsole, xterm.\n//\n// The command opened is always `<mg-binary> --agent <agent> --job <jobID>` run\n// from the project root, matching the session launcher's invocation contract.\n// <mg-binary> is the running binary's own path (os.Executable) — the one Go\n// binary IS the command now — so an install under a different name still\n// works.\npackage launch\n\nimport (\n\t\"errors\"\n\t\"fmt\"\n\t\"io\"\n\t\"os\"\n\t\"os/exec\"\n\t\"runtime\"\n\t\"strings\"\n\t\"sync\"\n\n\t\"github.com/lmuskalla/manigot/internal/config\"\n)\n\n// Agent opens a new terminal — inside tmux, a split pane in the TUI's current\n// window (see launchTmuxPane) — that runs manigot with `--profile <profile>\n// --agent <agent> --job <jobID>` in projectRoot. profile is one of the\n// subscription profiles in config.Profiles; an empty value defaults to\n// config.ProfileClaudePro, matching the session launcher's own default. terminal is\n// config.Settings.Terminal: when non-empty and the TUI is NOT inside tmux, that\n// terminal is invoked directly instead of the auto-detect chain below — see\n// buildOverrideCmd; inside tmux the split pane wins regardless of this setting.\n// Empty falls back to the configured override itself (TerminalSetting), so\n// callers that don't have the setting loaded — CLI paths like `mg done`'s\n// git-solver handoff — still honor it; only when the setting is also unset does\n// the auto-detect chain apply. It returns a short\n// human description of where it opened (e.g. \"tmux pane\", \"Terminal.app\",\n// \"gnome-terminal\", or the override's own binary name) so the caller can surface\n// it in a status line.\n//\n// The launcher runs from projectRoot (the main worktree) even though the job\n// lives in its own worktree: the session launcher\n// (session.ResolveRootFrom) re-derives the effective mount root itself from\n// the --job it is given (branch-match + worktree lookup, hard error on a\n// worktree-less job), so launching from projectRoot is not just sufficient\n// but required — it locates the project from $PWD and only then resolves the\n// job's worktree. The same reasoning keeps Quick/AgentQuick (no --job at all, mount\n// PROJECT_ROOT directly) and Jdi (mg-jdi, which re-derives per invocation)\n// on projectRoot.\n//\n// The launcher process is detached: its stdio is discarded so it cannot\n// corrupt the TUI's alt screen, and it is reaped asynchronously. The one\n// exception is the tmux path, where the split-window client is run\n// synchronously (see launchTmuxPane) so its pane-id output can be captured\n// for the replace policy — split-window itself returns in milliseconds, it\n// does not wait for the agent session.\n//\n// cmd.Start() failing (the launcher binary itself couldn't be\n// spawned — e.g. permission denied) is already surfaced: it's returned as an\n// error here and shown by the caller (App.updateDetail via cmdErrorText). The\n// remaining gap is a launcher that starts successfully but then fails on its\n// own after Agent has already returned \"success\" to the caller — e.g.\n// gnome-terminal/konsole exiting non-zero because there's no display server.\n// Surfacing that would need either blocking on cmd.Wait() here or wiring the\n// reaping goroutine back into a tea.Msg, and neither is safe to add: xterm\n// (unlike gnome-terminal/konsole/tmux/osascript, which detach quickly) *is*\n// the window process, so it doesn't return until the window closes — for it,\n// Wait()-ing here would block Update() for the whole agent session, which is\n// strictly worse than the bug being fixed. holdOnFailure\n// already covers the failure mode the brief actually reports (the inner `sc\n// --agent` command failing fast); a launcher-binary-itself failure is a\n// narrower, rarer case left uncovered rather than risk reintroducing UI lag.\nfunc Agent(agent, jobID, projectRoot, profile, terminal string) (string, error) {\n\texe, err := ExeOverride()\n\tif err != nil {\n\t\treturn \"\", fmt.Errorf(\"locate mg binary: %w\", err)\n\t}\n\tinner := shellCommand(exe, agent, jobID, projectRoot, profile)\n\treturn launchDetached(inner, terminal)\n}\n\n// Quick opens a new terminal — inside tmux, a split pane in the TUI's current\n// window, exactly like Agent, including the replace policy — that runs manigot\n// with just `--profile <profile>` (no --agent, no --job) in projectRoot: a\n// bare session for an ad-hoc change that doesn't belong to any specific\n// job/agent workflow. profile is one of the subscription profiles in\n// config.Profiles; an empty value defaults to config.ProfileClaudePro,\n// matching Agent. terminal is config.Settings.Terminal, threaded through to\n// launchDetached exactly like Agent's own terminal parameter — see Agent's\n// doc for the override semantics. It returns the same short human description\n// of where it opened so the caller can surface it in a status line.\n//\n// The session launcher treats --agent and --job as optional, so a bare `sc\n// --profile <profile>` simply runs claude/opencode against the current project\n// with no agent flag and no job prompt.\nfunc Quick(projectRoot, profile, terminal string) (string, error) {\n\texe, err := ExeOverride()\n\tif err != nil {\n\t\treturn \"\", fmt.Errorf(\"locate mg binary: %w\", err)\n\t}\n\tinner := quickShellCommand(exe, projectRoot, profile)\n\treturn launchDetached(inner, terminal)\n}\n\n// AgentQuick opens a new terminal — inside tmux, a split pane in the TUI's\n// current window, exactly like Agent/Quick, including the replace policy —\n// that runs manigot with `--profile <profile> --agent <agent>` (no --job) in\n// projectRoot: launching a specific agent for an ad-hoc session that doesn't\n// belong to any particular job. profile is one of the subscription profiles\n// in config.Profiles; an empty value defaults to config.ProfileClaudePro,\n// matching Agent/Quick. terminal is config.Settings.Terminal, threaded\n// through to launchDetached exactly like Agent/Quick's own terminal\n// parameter — see Agent's doc for the override semantics. It returns the\n// same short human description of where it opened so the caller can surface\n// it in a status line.\n//\n// The session launcher treats --job as optional (see Quick's own doc for the\n// --agent/--job-less case), so `sc --profile <profile> --agent <agent>`\n// simply runs that agent against the current project with no job prompt.\nfunc AgentQuick(agent, projectRoot, profile, terminal string) (string, error) {\n\texe, err := ExeOverride()\n\tif err != nil {\n\t\treturn \"\", fmt.Errorf(\"locate mg binary: %w\", err)\n\t}\n\tinner := agentQuickShellCommand(exe, agent, projectRoot, profile)\n\treturn launchDetached(inner, terminal)\n}\n\n// HostAgent opens a new terminal — inside tmux, a split pane in the TUI's\n// current window, exactly like Agent/Quick/AgentQuick, including the replace\n// policy — that runs `mg host --profile <profile> --agent <agent> --prompt\n// '<prompt>'` in projectRoot: a host-mode agent session (the agent CLI runs\n// directly on the host, unisolated — see `mg host`) with an explicit prompt.\n// prompt is the free-form instruction the agent starts with (the session\n// launcher passes it through verbatim); it is what `mg done` uses to hand a\n// failed squash merge to @git-solver, telling it what broke and what cleanup\n// remains. profile is one of the subscription profiles in config.Profiles;\n// an empty value defaults to config.ProfileClaudePro, matching Agent/Quick.\n// terminal is config.Settings.Terminal, threaded through to launchDetached\n// exactly like the other launch paths' own terminal parameter — see Agent's\n// doc for the override semantics. It returns the same short human description\n// of where it opened so the caller can surface it in a status line.\n//\n// Unlike Agent/AgentQuick there is no --job: host-mode sessions run against\n// the project root itself — the main worktree, where a failed `mg done`\n// squash merge leaves its conflicted state — never a job worktree.\nfunc HostAgent(agent, prompt, projectRoot, profile, terminal string) (string, error) {\n\texe, err := ExeOverride()\n\tif err != nil {\n\t\treturn \"\", fmt.Errorf(\"locate mg binary: %w\", err)\n\t}\n\tinner := hostAgentShellCommand(exe, agent, prompt, projectRoot, profile)\n\treturn launchDetached(inner, terminal)\n}\n\n// Tig opens a new terminal — inside tmux, a split pane in the TUI's current\n// window, exactly like Agent/Quick/AgentQuick, including the replace policy —\n// that runs `mg diff '<jobName>' --tig` in projectRoot: the host-side tig TUI\n// browsing what the job's branch changed (the CLI path already exists —\n// cmd/mg/diff.go runTig resolves the job's branch + the project's base\n// branch, builds the three-dot range <base>...<branch>, runs `tig <range>`,\n// and errors clearly when tig is not installed). terminal is\n// config.Settings.Terminal, threaded through to launchDetached exactly like\n// the other launch paths' own terminal parameter — see Agent's doc for the\n// override semantics. It returns the same short human description of where it\n// opened so the caller can surface it in a status line.\n//\n// Unlike Agent/Quick there is no profile flag: `mg diff` is a plain host git\n// command, not a session launch, so it takes no --profile (and no --agent or\n// --job either).\n//\n// The job is passed by its Name (id_slug), not its ID: `mg diff` resolves the\n// job's branch via an exact match on the branch's id_slug tail segment first,\n// then a unique prefix (cmd/mg/diff.go resolveJobBranch) — and job.Name\n// equals that tail segment exactly, so resolution is unambiguous.\n//\n// Tig re-checks availability itself (TigAvailable) before spawning: the\n// TUI's cached availability gate (detailView.tigAvailable) is only as fresh\n// as the last job open, so a stale cached \"available\" must surface a\n// synchronous error here rather than opening a doomed pane that dies on the\n// CLI's own tig-missing check.\nfunc Tig(jobID, projectRoot, terminal string) (string, error) {\n\tif !TigAvailable() {\n\t\treturn \"\", errors.New(\"tig is not installed on the host — install it, or use the diff tab\")\n\t}\n\texe, err := ExeOverride()\n\tif err != nil {\n\t\treturn \"\", fmt.Errorf(\"locate mg binary: %w\", err)\n\t}\n\tinner := tigShellCommand(exe, jobID, projectRoot)\n\treturn launchDetached(inner, terminal)\n}\n\n// Tail opens a new terminal — inside tmux, a split pane in the TUI's current\n// window, exactly like Agent/Quick/Tig, including the replace policy — that\n// runs `tail -f '<logPath>'`: a live tail of the given absolute log file (the\n// mg-jdi run.log sidecar, job.JDIRunLogPath) so a user can watch the currently\n// running agent's output grow from inside the TUI. When jq resolves on the\n// host (JqAvailable), the tail is piped through it (jqTailShellCommand) so\n// the JSONL raw stream both agent CLIs write is pretty-printed as it scrolls\n// by, with the mg-jdi section headers and blank lines passed through\n// unchanged; when jq is missing, this silently falls back to the plain\n// tail -f (tailShellCommand) — strictly no worse than before jq support\n// existed. terminal is config.Settings.Terminal, threaded through to\n// launchDetached exactly like the other launch paths' own terminal\n// parameter — see Agent's doc for the override semantics. It returns the\n// same short human description of where it opened so the caller can surface\n// it in a status line.\n//\n// Unlike Agent/Quick/Tig there is no mg-binary hop at all: `tail -f` (piped\n// through jq or not) is a plain host command, so there is no ExeOverride\n// resolution and no profile flag. The inner command is deliberately NOT\n// wrapped in holdOnFailure (the one launch path that deviates): a user ends\n// a tail pane with Ctrl+C (exit 130, non-zero), so holding on a non-zero\n// exit would leave a \"press enter to close\" prompt every single time — the\n// pane should just close when the user is done watching.\n//\n// tail -f idles harmlessly once the underlying run ends (the file simply\n// stops growing), so there is no liveness check here; the caller decides when\n// the key and footer hint are reachable (e.g. gated on the log file existing).\nfunc Tail(logPath, terminal string) (string, error) {\n\tinner := tailShellCommand(logPath)\n\tif JqAvailable() {\n\t\tinner = jqTailShellCommand(logPath)\n\t}\n\treturn launchDetached(inner, terminal)\n}\n\n// ExeOverride returns the path of the binary implementing the mg session\n// subcommand — the running binary itself. An exported package-level variable\n// so tests (in this package and internal/ui) can point Agent/Quick/AgentQuick\n// at a stub or a resolution failure instead of the real os.Executable().\nvar ExeOverride = func() (string, error) { return os.Executable() }\n\n// TerminalSetting returns the user's configured terminal override — the\n// `terminal` field of config/tui-settings.json — or \"\" when unset or\n// unreadable. It is the default every terminal-launch path falls back to when\n// the caller passes an empty terminal (see launchDetached), so the setting is\n// honored from the TUI — which passes it explicitly — and from CLI paths like\n// `mg done`'s git-solver handoff alike, instead of silently degrading to\n// auto-detect. An exported package-level variable, mirroring ExeOverride, so\n// tests can stub it.\nvar TerminalSetting = func() string {\n\ts, err := config.Load()\n\tif err != nil {\n\t\treturn \"\"\n\t}\n\treturn s.Terminal\n}\n\n// JdiExe returns the path of the binary implementing the `mg jdi` subcommand —\n// the running binary itself. An exported package-level variable, mirroring\n// ExeOverride, so tests (in this package and internal/ui) can point it at a\n// stub or a resolution failure.\nvar JdiExe = func() (string, error) { return os.Executable() }\n\n// TigLookPath resolves the tig binary on the host, mirroring `mg diff --tig`'s\n// own check (cmd/mg/diff.go's tigLookPath). An exported package-level\n// variable, mirroring ExeOverride/JdiExe, so tests (in this package and\n// internal/ui) can point it at a stub or a tig-less PATH instead of requiring\n// tig on the test machine.\nvar TigLookPath = exec.LookPath\n\n// TigAvailable reports whether tig resolves on the host — the \"if available\"\n// gate the TUI's `t` key and footer hint key off (see Tig's own doc for why\n// the launch path re-checks this itself). It is deliberately a lightweight\n// LookPath probe with no version check or config scan: `mg diff --tig` is the\n// authoritative backstop and errors clearly on its own when tig is missing.\nfunc TigAvailable() bool {\n\t_, err := TigLookPath(\"tig\")\n\treturn err == nil\n}\n\n// JqLookPath resolves the jq binary on the host, mirroring TigLookPath's own\n// tig-availability seam. An exported package-level variable, mirroring\n// ExeOverride/JdiExe/TigLookPath, so tests (in this package and internal/ui)\n// can point it at a stub or a jq-less PATH instead of requiring jq on the\n// test machine.\nvar JqLookPath = exec.LookPath\n\n// JqAvailable reports whether jq resolves on the host — the \"if available\"\n// gate Tail keys its JSONL-pretty-printing pipe on (see Tail's own doc for\n// why it falls back to a plain tail -f rather than erroring when jq is\n// missing). Deliberately a lightweight LookPath probe with no version check,\n// mirroring TigAvailable.\nfunc JqAvailable() bool {\n\t_, err := JqLookPath(\"jq\")\n\treturn err == nil\n}\n\n// Jdi starts `mg jdi --job <jobID> --profile <profile>` detached in the\n// background — no spawned terminal window at all, unlike Agent/Quick.\n// mg-jdi drives a fixed,\n// non-interactive agent sequence with no TTY at any point in its own\n// container invocations (the session launcher's --print flag) and needs no\n// terminal for a human or a subprocess to attach to — spawning one anyway\n// would be pure overhead and would reintroduce exactly the per-agent-window\n// cost the backlog's \"in-TUI agent terminal\" idea is about removing, not\n// adding to. Visibility into a TUI-launched run is the list-row status\n// badge and the detail-view log tab, not a window; see those for how a\n// human watches it run.\n//\n// profile is one of the subscription profiles in config.Profiles, matching\n// Agent/Quick — an empty value defaults to config.ProfileClaudePro, mg-jdi's\n// own default (see cmd/mg/jdi.go).\n//\n// cmd.Dir stays on projectRoot (the main worktree) even though the job lives\n// in its own worktree: mg-jdi — like every other\n// `--job` invocation — re-derives the effective worktree root itself from the\n// job id it is given, per invocation, so it must be launched from the project\n// root that find_project_root expects, never from a job worktree directly.\n// See Agent's doc for the full reasoning, shared by all four launch paths.\n//\n// Unlike Agent/Quick there is no \"where it opened\" description to return —\n// there is no terminal/pane to describe — so this returns only an error (nil\n// on a successful start). Like launchDetached, the started process's stdio is\n// discarded and it is reaped asynchronously so it cannot corrupt the TUI's\n// alt screen or zombie; mg-jdi may run for a long time (several full agent\n// sessions), so this must not block waiting for it.\nfunc Jdi(jobID, projectRoot, profile string) error {\n\tif profile == \"\" {\n\t\tprofile = config.ProfileClaudePro\n\t}\n\texe, err := JdiExe()\n\tif err != nil {\n\t\treturn fmt.Errorf(\"locate mg binary: %w\", err)\n\t}\n\tcmd := exec.Command(exe, \"jdi\", \"--job\", jobID, \"--profile\", profile)\n\tcmd.Dir = projectRoot\n\tcmd.Stdin = nil\n\tcmd.Stdout = io.Discard\n\tcmd.Stderr = io.Discard\n\tif err := cmd.Start(); err != nil {\n\t\treturn fmt.Errorf(\"start mg jdi: %w\", err)\n\t}\n\tif cmd.Process != nil {\n\t\tgo func() { _ = cmd.Wait() }()\n\t}\n\treturn nil\n}\n\n// tmuxPaneTag is the pane title manigot tags the split panes it opens with\n// (`tmux select-pane -t <id> -T`, run by launchTmuxPane after the split —\n// split-window itself has no -T on any released tmux). It is how a later\n// launch finds the pane manigot opened last so it can be replaced — see\n// killPreviousTmuxPane. It lives in the pane title (in tmux, not in memory)\n// so it survives a TUI restart.\nconst tmuxPaneTag = \"manigot\"\n\n// tmuxMu serializes the find-kill-split sequence in launchTmuxPane so two\n// concurrent launches (Agent/Quick can be invoked from Bubble Tea command\n// goroutines) can never interleave: without it, both could list the same\n// previous pane, both split, and the \"at most one manigot pane\" invariant\n// would break. Also guards tmuxLastPaneID.\nvar tmuxMu sync.Mutex\n\n// tmuxLastPaneID is the pane_id (e.g. \"%12\") of the most recent pane manigot\n// opened via launchTmuxPane, or \"\" if none is known yet (e.g. the TUI was\n// restarted after the pane was opened, or no launch has happened). Guarded by\n// tmuxMu.\nvar tmuxLastPaneID string\n\n// launchTmuxPane opens the manigot session as a split pane in the TUI's\n// current tmux window and implements the \"replace\" reuse policy:\n// before splitting, the pane manigot opened last is killed so at most one\n// manigot pane exists at a time. It is the tmux-specific launch path — unlike\n// the generic launchDetached tail it runs the split-window client\n// synchronously because it needs its stdout (the new pane's id, via\n// -P -F '#{pane_id}') to tag the pane with tmuxPaneTag (select-pane -T, since\n// split-window has no -T on released tmux) and to record it for the next\n// replace; split-window itself returns as soon as the pane exists, the inner\n// command runs inside the pane, not in this client, so blocking on it is\n// milliseconds and Output() captures stdout+stderr so nothing leaks onto the\n// TUI's screen.\nfunc launchTmuxPane(inner string) (string, error) {\n\ttmuxMu.Lock()\n\tdefer tmuxMu.Unlock()\n\n\t// Replace, then split. The replace is best-effort: a failure to find the\n\t// previous pane must not prevent the new launch — if tmux is in a state\n\t// where list-panes fails, the split-window below will fail too and its\n\t// error is the accurate one to surface. The user having already closed\n\t// the previous pane is not an error at all (tolerated inside).\n\t_ = killPreviousTmuxPane()\n\n\tcmd, desc, err := buildCmd(inner, \"\")\n\tif err != nil {\n\t\treturn \"\", err\n\t}\n\tout, err := cmd.Output()\n\tif err != nil {\n\t\treturn \"\", fmt.Errorf(\"launch %s: %w\", desc, err)\n\t}\n\tpaneID := strings.TrimSpace(string(out))\n\tif paneID == \"\" {\n\t\treturn \"\", fmt.Errorf(\"launch %s: tmux split-window printed no pane id\", desc)\n\t}\n\n\t// Tag the new pane so a later launch can find it even after a TUI restart\n\t// (which loses tmuxLastPaneID). select-pane -T is supported by every\n\t// released tmux, unlike split-window -T. Best-effort: the pane is already\n\t// live and the in-memory id covers replacement within this TUI process, so\n\t// a tagging failure only narrows the restart-surviving lookup — it must\n\t// not abort a launch that already succeeded.\n\t_ = exec.Command(\"tmux\", \"select-pane\", \"-t\", paneID, \"-T\", tmuxPaneTag).Run()\n\n\ttmuxLastPaneID = paneID\n\treturn desc, nil\n}\n\n// killPreviousTmuxPane implements the \"replace\" reuse policy: it kills\n// the split pane manigot opened last so that at most one manigot pane exists\n// at a time. The pane is identified two ways, both safe against ever killing\n// a pane manigot did not itself open:\n//\n//   - tmuxLastPaneID — the pane_id learned from the previous split-window's\n//     -P output; reliable while this TUI process lives.\n//   - any pane in the current session whose title is tmuxPaneTag — set via\n//     select-pane -T after the split (see launchTmuxPane); survives TUI\n//     restarts because it lives in tmux, not in memory (a restart loses\n//     tmuxLastPaneID).\n//\n// list-panes is scoped to the current session (-s): the manigot pane is split\n// from the TUI's own pane, so it lives in the TUI's session (a tmux client\n// run as a subprocess of the TUI resolves \"current session\" from $TMUX_PANE),\n// and -s will also find it if the user moved it to another window of that\n// session — without reaching into a different session where a different\n// manigot TUI might legitimately have its own pane. A pane the user already\n// closed in the meantime is not an error: that kill-pane failure is ignored.\n// The kill is unconditional with respect to the pane's state — a pane mid-\n// hold-on-failure is replaced exactly like a live session.\n// Called with tmuxMu held.\nfunc killPreviousTmuxPane() error {\n\tcandidates := map[string]struct{}{}\n\tif tmuxLastPaneID != \"\" {\n\t\tcandidates[tmuxLastPaneID] = struct{}{}\n\t}\n\tout, err := exec.Command(\"tmux\", \"list-panes\", \"-s\", \"-F\", \"#{pane_id} #{pane_title}\").Output()\n\tif err != nil {\n\t\treturn fmt.Errorf(\"tmux list-panes: %w\", err)\n\t}\n\tfor _, line := range strings.Split(strings.TrimSpace(string(out)), \"\\n\") {\n\t\tfields := strings.SplitN(line, \" \", 2)\n\t\tif len(fields) == 2 && fields[1] == tmuxPaneTag {\n\t\t\tcandidates[fields[0]] = struct{}{}\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\tfor id := range candidates {\n\t\t// Already closed by the user (or its window closed with it) → the\n\t\t// replace is a no-op, which is the tolerated case.\n\t\t_ = exec.Command(\"tmux\", \"kill-pane\", \"-t\", id).Run()\n\t}\n\treturn nil\n}\n\n// launchDetached is the shared spawn/reap tail used by both launch paths: it\n// builds the *exec.Cmd for inner, discards its stdio so the launcher cannot\n// corrupt the TUI's alt screen, starts it, and reaps the launcher\n// asynchronously so it doesn't zombie (the actual terminal/pane outlives it).\n// It returns the short \"where it opened\" description buildCmd produces.\n//\n// terminal is config.Settings.Terminal: when non-empty it is used in place of\n// the macOS/Linux auto-detect chain (branch 3/4 below) — but only when the TUI\n// is NOT inside tmux. Inside tmux (with a tmux binary on PATH) the launch is\n// handled by launchTmuxPane instead, regardless of the override: a user who\n// runs the TUI inside tmux always gets the split-pane behavior, and the\n// override applies only outside tmux.\n//\n// An empty terminal falls back to TerminalSetting — the user's configured\n// override itself — before auto-detect, so every launch path honors the same\n// setting even when the caller doesn't have it loaded (the TUI passes it\n// explicitly; CLI paths like `mg done`'s git-solver handoff pass \"\" and get\n// the setting here). Only when the setting is also unset does the auto-detect\n// chain below apply. Inside tmux the terminal value is never consulted, so\n// the resolution is harmless there — the tmux split pane wins regardless.\n//\n// Inside tmux, the launch is handled by launchTmuxPane: it implements the\n// replace policy (killing the previously manigot-opened pane before splitting\n// a new one) and needs the split-window client's stdout (the new pane's id),\n// so it runs synchronously rather than through the detached tail below. The\n// same tmux detection ($TMUX set and a tmux binary on PATH) is used here and\n// in buildCmd.\n//\n// See Agent's doc comment for why a launcher that starts successfully but then\n// fails on its own (e.g. no display server) is not surfaced here — holdOnFailure\n// covers the inner command's own fast failures instead.\nfunc launchDetached(inner, terminal string) (string, error) {\n\t// The caller's explicit terminal wins; an empty one falls back to the\n\t// user's configured override (TerminalSetting) so the setting is honored\n\t// from every launch path, TUI and CLI alike. Auto-detect applies only when\n\t// neither is set. Inside tmux the resolved value is never consulted — the\n\t// tmux split pane below wins regardless.\n\tif terminal == \"\" {\n\t\tterminal = TerminalSetting()\n\t}\n\n\t// Inside tmux with a tmux binary on PATH, the tmux split pane always wins —\n\t// even when an override is set. The override applies only outside tmux.\n\tif os.Getenv(\"TMUX\") != \"\" {\n\t\tif _, err := exec.LookPath(\"tmux\"); err == nil {\n\t\t\treturn launchTmuxPane(inner)\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\n\tcmd, desc, err := buildCmd(inner, terminal)\n\tif err != nil {\n\t\treturn \"\", err\n\t}\n\tcmd.Stdin = nil\n\tcmd.Stdout = io.Discard\n\tcmd.Stderr = io.Discard\n\tif err := cmd.Start(); err != nil {\n\t\treturn \"\", fmt.Errorf(\"launch %s: %w\", desc, err)\n\t}\n\t// Reap the launcher to avoid a zombie; the actual terminal/pane outlives it.\n\tif cmd.Process != nil {\n\t\tgo func() { _ = cmd.Wait() }()\n\t}\n\treturn desc, nil\n}\n\n// shellCommand builds the shell string executed inside the new terminal:\n//\n//\tcd '<projectRoot>' && '<manigot>' --profile '<profile>' --agent '<agent>' --job '<jobID>'; ec=$?; ...\n//\n// cd-ing first matters because manigot finds the project root from $PWD (the\n// same walk-up job.FindProjectRoot uses). manigotPath is the absolute path from the\n// resolve package; it is quoted like every other value, so a checkout in a\n// directory with spaces survives both osascript and `bash -lc`. Arguments are\n// single-quoted and embedded single quotes are escaped, so no value can break\n// out of its quotes. An empty profile defaults to config.ProfileClaudePro so the\n// flag is always passed explicitly, regardless of what the session\n// launcher's own default happens to be.\n//\n// None of buildCmd's five spawn paths keep the\n// window/pane open once the inner command exits, success or failure — tmux\n// destroys a new window as soon as its command exits (no remain-on-exit),\n// Terminal.app's `do script` types the command but the window otherwise\n// behaves normally, and gnome-terminal/x-terminal-emulator/konsole/xterm all\n// close on exit by default. A fast failure (docker not running, resolve\n// failure inside the container, an auth error) is therefore invisible: the\n// window flashes and disappears before its output can be read — this is the\n// brief's \"a window appears and it immediately closes again\". The result is\n// wrapped in holdOnFailure so a non-zero exit pauses instead.\nfunc shellCommand(manigotPath, agent, jobID, projectRoot, profile string) string {\n\tif profile == \"\" {\n\t\tprofile = config.ProfileClaudePro\n\t}\n\tinner := fmt.Sprintf(\"cd %s && %s --profile %s --agent %s --job %s\",\n\t\tshellQuote(projectRoot), shellQuote(manigotPath), shellQuote(profile), shellQuote(agent), shellQuote(jobID))\n\treturn holdOnFailure(inner)\n}\n\n// quickShellCommand builds the shell string for a bare manigot session (no\n// --agent, no --job), executed inside the new terminal:\n//\n//\tcd '<projectRoot>' && '<manigot>' --profile '<profile>'; ec=$?; ...\n//\n// It is the --agent/--job-less counterpart to shellCommand: same cd-first,\n// shellQuote-everything, holdOnFailure-wrap behavior (so a fast failure of the\n// inner command still holds the window open), just without the\n// agent/job flags. An empty profile defaults to config.ProfileClaudePro for the\n// same reason shellCommand does. It is deliberately a separate function rather\n// than a generalization of shellCommand so the latter's exact-format tests stay\n// unchanged.\nfunc quickShellCommand(manigotPath, projectRoot, profile string) string {\n\tif profile == \"\" {\n\t\tprofile = config.ProfileClaudePro\n\t}\n\tinner := fmt.Sprintf(\"cd %s && %s --profile %s\",\n\t\tshellQuote(projectRoot), shellQuote(manigotPath), shellQuote(profile))\n\treturn holdOnFailure(inner)\n}\n\n// agentQuickShellCommand builds the shell string for AgentQuick's jobless\n// agent session (--agent, no --job), executed inside the new terminal:\n//\n//\tcd '<projectRoot>' && '<manigot>' --profile '<profile>' --agent '<agent>'; ec=$?; ...\n//\n// It sits between shellCommand (agent + job) and quickShellCommand (neither):\n// same cd-first, shellQuote-everything, holdOnFailure-wrap behavior, just\n// with --agent and no --job. An empty profile defaults to\n// config.ProfileClaudePro for the same reason the other two do. Deliberately\n// its own function rather than a generalization of either existing one, per\n// the file's \"deliberately a separate function\" convention — keeps\n// shellCommand/quickShellCommand's exact-format tests stable.\nfunc agentQuickShellCommand(manigotPath, agent, projectRoot, profile string) string {\n\tif profile == \"\" {\n\t\tprofile = config.ProfileClaudePro\n\t}\n\tinner := fmt.Sprintf(\"cd %s && %s --profile %s --agent %s\",\n\t\tshellQuote(projectRoot), shellQuote(manigotPath), shellQuote(profile), shellQuote(agent))\n\treturn holdOnFailure(inner)\n}\n\n// hostAgentShellCommand builds the shell string for HostAgent's host-mode\n// agent session (--agent, --prompt, no --job), executed inside the new\n// terminal:\n//\n//\tcd '<projectRoot>' && '<manigot>' host --profile '<profile>' --agent '<agent>' --prompt '<prompt>'; ec=$?; ...\n//\n// Same cd-first, shellQuote-everything, holdOnFailure-wrap behavior as the\n// other launch paths, with the `host` subcommand inserted and the prompt\n// passed via --prompt — the one launch path that carries a prompt. The prompt\n// is a free-form multi-sentence instruction, so shellQuote's embedded-quote\n// escaping matters here more than anywhere (see TestHostAgentShellCommandQuotesPrompt).\n// An empty profile defaults to config.ProfileClaudePro for the same reason the\n// other builders do. Deliberately its own function rather than a\n// generalization of the other shell-command builders, per the file's\n// \"deliberately separate function per launch path\" convention.\nfunc hostAgentShellCommand(manigotPath, agent, prompt, projectRoot, profile string) string {\n\tif profile == \"\" {\n\t\tprofile = config.ProfileClaudePro\n\t}\n\tinner := fmt.Sprintf(\"cd %s && %s host --profile %s --agent %s --prompt %s\",\n\t\tshellQuote(projectRoot), shellQuote(manigotPath), shellQuote(profile), shellQuote(agent), shellQuote(prompt))\n\treturn holdOnFailure(inner)\n}\n\n// tigShellCommand builds the shell string executed inside the new terminal:\n//\n//\tcd '<projectRoot>' && '<manigot>' diff '<jobID>' --tig; ec=$?; ...\n//\n// Same cd-first, shellQuote-everything, holdOnFailure-wrap behavior as the\n// other launch paths — the job's files are resolved by `mg diff` from $PWD,\n// so cd-ing first matters exactly as it does for Agent/Quick. No --profile\n// flag: `mg diff` takes none (it is a host git command, not a session\n// launch). Deliberately its own function rather than a generalization of the\n// other shell-command builders, per the file's \"separate function per launch\n// path\" convention — keeps their exact-format tests unchanged.\nfunc tigShellCommand(manigotPath, jobID, projectRoot string) string {\n\tinner := fmt.Sprintf(\"cd %s && %s diff %s --tig\",\n\t\tshellQuote(projectRoot), shellQuote(manigotPath), shellQuote(jobID))\n\treturn holdOnFailure(inner)\n}\n\n// tailShellCommand builds the shell string executed inside the new terminal:\n//\n//\ttail -f '<logPath>'\n//\n// The log path is absolute (the caller passes job.JDIRunLogPath's output), so\n// there is no cd-first like the other launch paths — and the inner command is\n// deliberately NOT wrapped in holdOnFailure, the one deviation from the\n// agent/quick/tig convention: a user ends `tail -f` with Ctrl+C (exit 130,\n// non-zero), so holding on a non-zero exit would leave a \"press enter to\n// close\" prompt every single time instead of just closing the pane. Same\n// shellQuote-everything behavior as the other builders — a path with spaces\n// or embedded quotes survives osascript and `bash -lc` as one word.\n// Deliberately its own function rather than a generalization of the other\n// shell-command builders, per the file's \"separate function per launch path\"\n// convention.\nfunc tailShellCommand(logPath string) string {\n\treturn \"tail -f \" + shellQuote(logPath)\n}\n\n// jqTailShellCommand builds the shell string executed inside the new\n// terminal when jq is available on the host (see Tail's own doc):\n//\n//\ttail -f '<logPath>' | jq -R -r 'fromjson? // .'\n//\n// session.log is not pure JSON: mg-jdi writes `=== <RFC3339> <agent> (attempt\n// N) ===` section headers and blank lines around each invocation's raw\n// stream, and the raw stream itself is JSONL for both agent CLIs (opencode\n// --format json; Claude --output-format stream-json). The filter reads each\n// line raw (-R), tries to parse it as JSON (fromjson?, jq's \"try\" form —\n// null/error on failure rather than aborting the whole pipeline) and falls\n// back to the raw line (// .) when parsing fails, so the headers and blank\n// lines pass through untouched while every JSONL line is pretty-printed\n// (jq's default output format) as it scrolls by. -r prints the fallback raw\n// lines without JSON-string quoting.\n//\n// Same shellQuote-everything behavior as tailShellCommand — a log path with\n// spaces or embedded quotes survives osascript and `bash -lc` as one word.\n// The jq filter itself is a fixed literal (no host input embedded in it), so\n// it needs no quoting beyond the single-quoted shell argument it already is.\nfunc jqTailShellCommand(logPath string) string {\n\treturn \"tail -f \" + shellQuote(logPath) + ` | jq -R -r 'fromjson? // .'`\n}\n\n// holdOnFailure wraps a shell command so that, if it exits non-zero, the\n// window/pane it is running in stays open with the failure message visible\n// instead of closing immediately. A clean exit (status 0) is left\n// alone — the launched agent session usually runs interactively until the\n// user ends it themselves, so closing right away once that happens is the\n// expected behavior; only a fast, invisible failure needs holding open.\n//\n// Wrapping the shared inner command string (rather than reaching for a\n// per-terminal mechanism like tmux's remain-on-exit or `xterm -hold`) keeps\n// the fix uniform across all five spawn paths in buildCmd and lets it be\n// exercised by ordinary string-based tests.\n//\n// The wrap behaves identically inside a tmux split pane as in a\n// window — tmux runs the pane's shell-command in a pty and destroys the pane\n// when the command exits, so the `read -r _ignored` blocking on a non-zero\n// exit keeps the pane open with the failure message visible exactly as it\n// held a new window open (the message may wrap in the narrower 35% pane, but\n// stays fully visible). And because the replace policy treats every\n// manigot pane alike, a pane mid-hold-on-failure is killed by a subsequent\n// launch just like a live session is — deliberate and unconditional (the\n// \"skip or warn?\" alternative was rejected: skipping would break\n// the at-most-one-pane invariant and needs unfeasible mid-hold detection from\n// outside the pane, and warning needs new TUI plumbing that is out of scope\n// for this job). A user who launches something new has moved on from the\n// failure they were present for when they launched it.\nfunc holdOnFailure(cmd string) string {\n\treturn cmd + `; ec=$?; if [ \"$ec\" -ne 0 ]; then echo; echo \"--- manigot: exited with status $ec ---\"; printf 'press enter to close... '; read -r _ignored; fi; exit \"$ec\"`\n}\n\n// shellQuote wraps s in single quotes, escaping any embedded single quote via\n// the standard '\\” idiom.\nfunc shellQuote(s string) string {\n\treturn \"'\" + strings.ReplaceAll(s, \"'\", `'\\''`) + \"'\"\n}\n\n// terminalCandidates is the Linux terminal-emulator candidate table used both\n// by buildCmd's auto-detect chain (branch 4) and, by name lookup, by\n// buildOverrideCmd's known-name-vs-\"-e\"-fallback flag-convention choice:\n// an override whose binary matches one of these names\n// (case-insensitively) reuses that name's own flag convention instead of the\n// \"-e\" default.\nvar terminalCandidates = []struct {\n\tname string\n\tpre  []string // args between the emulator name and the shell command\n}{\n\t{\"gnome-terminal\", []string{\"--\"}},\n\t{\"ptyxis\", []string{\"--\"}},\n\t{\"x-terminal-emulator\", []string{\"-e\"}},\n\t{\"konsole\", []string{\"-e\"}},\n\t{\"xterm\", []string{\"-e\"}},\n}\n\n// buildOverrideCmd constructs the *exec.Cmd for an explicit user-chosen\n// terminal override (config.Settings.Terminal): it takes over the auto-detect\n// spawn order — the tmux branch is only reachable inside tmux, where\n// launchDetached/buildCmd prefer the tmux pane, so an override is invoked when\n// the TUI is NOT inside tmux (or tmux is unavailable there), replacing the\n// macOS/Linux auto-detect chain (see launchDetached).\n//\n// terminal's first whitespace-separated token is the binary to run; any\n// remaining tokens are passed as leading arguments before the flag that hands\n// off the inner shell command — mirroring how config.Settings.Editor (and\n// $VISUAL/$EDITOR) already allow trailing args, e.g. \"code --wait\". The\n// binary is looked up via exec.LookPath exactly like the auto-detect\n// candidates in terminalCandidates, so a typo'd or missing binary surfaces a\n// clear \"launch <name>: exec: ... file not found\" error here instead of\n// silently falling back to auto-detect.\n//\n// The flag used to hand off the inner command is chosen the same way as the\n// existing candidates: if the binary case-insensitively matches one of\n// terminalCandidates' own names, that name's convention (\"--\" or \"-e\") is\n// reused; otherwise \"-e\" is used, since it is the more common convention\n// among terminals not already in that list (kitty, alacritty, wezterm, rxvt,\n// terminator all accept it). This is a best-effort convention, not a\n// guarantee for every possible terminal emulator — a known limitation, not\n// solved generally, since a fully general solution would need a per-terminal\n// flag field the brief did not ask for.\nfunc buildOverrideCmd(inner, terminal string) (*exec.Cmd, string, error) {\n\tfields := strings.Fields(terminal)\n\tif len(fields) == 0 {\n\t\treturn nil, \"\", fmt.Errorf(\"terminal override is blank\")\n\t}\n\tname := fields[0]\n\tleadingArgs := fields[1:]\n\n\tif _, err := exec.LookPath(name); err != nil {\n\t\treturn nil, \"\", fmt.Errorf(\"launch %s: %w\", name, err)\n\t}\n\n\tflag := \"-e\"\n\tfor _, c := range terminalCandidates {\n\t\tif strings.EqualFold(c.name, name) {\n\t\t\tflag = c.pre[0]\n\t\t\tbreak\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\n\targs := append(append([]string{}, leadingArgs...), flag, \"bash\", \"-lc\", inner)\n\treturn exec.Command(name, args...), name, nil\n}\n\n// buildCmd constructs the *exec.Cmd for the detected environment, or — when\n// terminal (config.Settings.Terminal) is non-empty and the TUI is not inside\n// tmux — for the explicit override instead (see buildOverrideCmd). The tmux\n// branch (1) is checked first and always wins when the TUI is inside tmux and\n// a tmux binary is on PATH, even over an override; branches 2–4 (override,\n// macOS Terminal.app, Linux emulators) run detached via launchDetached's\n// spawn/reap tail. The tmux branch is also the command launchTmuxPane runs\n// synchronously.\nfunc buildCmd(inner, terminal string) (*exec.Cmd, string, error) {\n\t// 1. Inside tmux (works on every OS that has tmux): split a pane off the\n\t// TUI's current window, replacing the previously manigot-opened\n\t// pane. Checked before the override so a session launched from a TUI\n\t// inside tmux always lands in a tmux pane, regardless of\n\t// config.Settings.Terminal.\n\tif os.Getenv(\"TMUX\") != \"\" {\n\t\tif _, err := exec.LookPath(\"tmux\"); err == nil {\n\t\t\t// -h splits side by side (vertical divider); -l 35% sizes the new\n\t\t\t// pane to 35% of the window width so the TUI's own pane (run\n\t\t\t// with no explicit -t, so this targets the TUI's own currently\n\t\t\t// active pane) keeps the majority share.\n\t\t\t// -l (not the older -p) is required: tmux deprecated -p's\n\t\t\t// percentage sizing in 3.1 in favor of -l with a \"%\" suffix, and\n\t\t\t// tmux 3.4 rejects -p outright with \"size missing\" (surfaced to\n\t\t\t// the user as the split-window client exiting 1) instead of\n\t\t\t// splitting.\n\t\t\t// -P -F '#{pane_id}' makes the split-window client print the new\n\t\t\t// pane's id to its stdout so launchTmuxPane can tag it with\n\t\t\t// tmuxPaneTag (via select-pane -T — split-window has no -T on any\n\t\t\t// released tmux) and record it for the replace policy.\n\t\t\t//\n\t\t\t// The trailing \"bash\", \"-lc\", inner (three separate argv elements,\n\t\t\t// not one combined string) matters: given a single shell-command\n\t\t\t// argument, tmux runs it via the pane's default-shell in -c mode,\n\t\t\t// which is non-login and non-interactive — .zshrc/.bash_profile are\n\t\t\t// never sourced, so any PATH customization living there (docker,\n\t\t\t// a manigot install dir, nvm, homebrew, ...) is invisible to it and\n\t\t\t// the inner command fails fast (caught by holdOnFailure, but the\n\t\t\t// session never actually starts). Passing multiple argv elements\n\t\t\t// instead makes tmux exec them directly with no shell in between,\n\t\t\t// so this becomes an ordinary `bash -lc inner` invocation — the\n\t\t\t// exact login-shell wrapping every other spawn path below already\n\t\t\t// uses (see shellArgs), just constructed inline since tmux's\n\t\t\t// argv-vs-single-string parsing is what selects shell-vs-direct-exec\n\t\t\t// here, unlike a plain exec.Command call.\n\t\t\treturn exec.Command(\"tmux\", \"split-window\", \"-h\", \"-l\", \"35%\",\n\t\t\t\t\"-P\", \"-F\", \"#{pane_id}\", \"bash\", \"-lc\", inner), \"tmux pane\", nil\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\n\t// 2. The explicit terminal override (config.Settings.Terminal), when the\n\t// TUI is not inside tmux (or tmux is unavailable there).\n\tif terminal != \"\" {\n\t\treturn buildOverrideCmd(inner, terminal)\n\t}\n\n\t// 3. macOS Terminal.app.\n\tif runtime.GOOS == \"darwin\" {\n\t\tscript := fmt.Sprintf(`tell application \"Terminal\" to do script %q`, inner)\n\t\treturn exec.Command(\"osascript\", \"-e\", script), \"Terminal.app\", nil\n\t}\n\n\t// 4. Linux terminal emulators, in order of preference.\n\tshellArgs := []string{\"bash\", \"-lc\", inner}\n\tfor _, c := range terminalCandidates {\n\t\tif _, err := exec.LookPath(c.name); err == nil {\n\t\t\targs := append(append([]string{}, c.pre...), shellArgs...)\n\t\t\treturn exec.Command(c.name, args...), c.name, nil\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\n\treturn nil, \"\", fmt.Errorf(\"no supported terminal launcher found (looked for: tmux, Terminal.app, gnome-terminal, ptyxis, x-terminal-emulator, konsole, xterm)\")\n}\n","numLines":871,"startLine":1,"totalLines":871}}}
