{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Ln2sru87UW2L3jNaQ96TwL","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Tasks: listener architecture\n2\t\n3\tid: wood\n4\tstatus: open\n5\tanalyst: wood\n6\tdate: 2026-08-27\n7\t\n8\t<!-- Produced by @analyst from brief.md. -->\n9\t\n10\t## Task breakdown\n11\t\n12\t<!-- TASK-1: Project registry in a new daemon package. Add a new internal package for the daemon (suggested name src/internal/serve — the home for the registry, HTTP server, handlers, auth, audit and locks; one package for all of it, mirroring how internal/session owns the launcher). Registry type: loads an explicit config file of project roots at startup — NO scanning, NO auto-adopting directories the daemon finds (brief scope item 2). Suggested config location and shape: <manigot checkout>/config/serve.json (config.Dir()) holding {\"projects\": [\"/abs/root\", ...]}, path overridable via --registry (TASK-2's flag) — the brief leaves the location open, confirm the choice while implementing. Each entry validated as an existing directory (a registered project needs docs/ for jobs, but only warn on that — a root without docs/ lists no jobs rather than failing startup). Registrations are read once at startup; changing the file means editing and restarting (v1). Provide Projects() (ordered copy) and a Project(name) lookup so every handler resolves ONLY against registered roots — the single choke point TASK-7's zero-path-inputs enforcement builds on. Include registry_test.go: parses the config, validates roots, rejects a missing file (empty registry, not an error), rejects non-directory entries, lookup by exact path and by base name.\n13\t     files: src/internal/serve/registry.go (new), src/internal/serve/registry_test.go (new)\n14\t     depends: none\n15\t     risk: low — pure config read + validation, no system interaction beyond os.Stat\n16\t\n17\tTASK-2: `mg serve` subcommand scaffold. Add `serve` to the dispatcher switch in src/cmd/mg/main.go (next to tui/jdi — a long-running in-process command) and to printHelp's command list; new src/cmd/mg/serve.go implementing runServe: flag parsing (--addr/--port with a sensible localhost default such as 127.0.0.1:8080, --registry <path> overriding the config location, --token <value> — token read precedence: flag, then MG_SERVE_TOKEN via config.EnvValue, see TASK-6), builds the Registry (TASK-1), constructs the Server (TASK-3) passing main's `version` var (internal/serve cannot import package main — the health endpoint needs it), enforces the bind/auth startup guard (TASK-6), then blocks on the serve loop and shuts down gracefully on SIGINT/SIGTERM (http.Server.Shutdown with a bounded drain). Long-running-process lifecycle is new territory for the single binary — the signal-handling and shutdown path must be testable (an httptest-able Serve/Shutdown seam, not a bare http.ListenAndServe call in main).\n18\t     files: src/cmd/mg/main.go, src/cmd/mg/serve.go (new), src/cmd/mg/serve_test.go (new)\n19\t     depends: TASK-1, TASK-3, TASK-6 (wires them; can be stubbed against their signatures and landed last among the code tasks)\n20\t     risk: medium — blocking serve + graceful shutdown is a new process shape for mg; the shutdown drain must be bounded so a hung request cannot wedge the exit\n21\t\n22\tTASK-3: HTTP server core + projects / jobs / job-files read endpoints. The net/http mux, JSON envelope helpers (no glamour/terminal rendering anywhere — the API serves raw markdown and JSON, never ANSI), and the first read endpoints (brief scope item 3): GET /projects (registered roots, from TASK-1); GET /projects/<project>/jobs (one row per job in the TUI's info design — id / status / stage / type / date / title via job.Discover + j.Stage(), in Discover's sort order, plus the mg-jdi activity state from job.ReadJDIStatus per job); GET /projects/<project>/jobs/<job>/files/<file> where <file> is whitelisted to brief|tasks|implementation|verdict (read from the job's own Dir via os.ReadFile, raw markdown text, 404 when the file is missing — missing files are normal, e.g. verdict.md before review). Project and job segments must go through the TASK-7 resolution helpers from day one (build the choke point in this task), not ad-hoc path joining.\n23\t     files: src/internal/serve/server.go (new), src/internal/serve/api.go (new), src/internal/serve/api_test.go (new)\n24\t     depends: TASK-1\n25\t     risk: medium — the first HTTP surface is the security boundary the brief stresses; the file whitelist and resolution choke point must be airtight here so TASK-7 is enforcement, not a retrofit\n26\t\n27\tTASK-4: jdi status + run.log + session.log + diff + agents endpoints. GET /projects/<project>/jobs/<job>/jdi: job.ReadJDIStatus (state/agent/updated), job.ReadJDIRunLogTail (run.log, bounded tail), and the job's session.log read from the job's own Dir (docs/jobs/<id>_<slug>/session.log — same bounded-tail semantics; ok=false when absent is a normal \"no run yet\" not an error). GET /projects/<project>/jobs/<job>/diff (brief scope item 3): resolve the base branch exactly as mg diff does — project.Load(root).BaseBranch, falling back to git.SymbolicRefHead(root) — and the job→branch via the same exact-then-prefix matching the CLI uses (git.ExactBranchMatch / git.PrefixBranchMatches over git.LocalBranches, i.e. cmd/mg/diff.go's resolveJobBranch chain; not-found and ambiguous return 404/409 with the CLI's error wording); default response is the quick eyeball (git.LogOneline + git.DiffStat, the same two calls mg diff makes), ?full=1 returns the complete patch via git.Diff. GET /projects/<project>/agents: agentlist.Discover (name + description only — never the agent files' contents beyond the description line). All git access goes through internal/git (the package that owns every git shell-out); no new git exec outside it.\n28\t     files: src/internal/serve/api.go (extend), src/internal/serve/api_test.go (extend)\n29\t     depends: TASK-3\n30\t     risk: medium — the diff endpoint's base-branch + job-resolution chain must mirror mg diff byte-for-byte in behavior (the same resolution the TUI's diff tab uses); read-only git commands only\n31\t\n32\tTASK-5: health endpoint + docker image presence helper. GET /health (brief scope item 3): version (the value TASK-2 passes in from main), docker image present, profiles ready. Add a small exported helper in internal/session (the package that owns docker shell-outs — e.g. ImagePresent() bool) using the existing stubbable dockerCommand seam from prune.go: `docker image inspect manigot`; docker missing or daemon down degrades to false, never an error. Profiles ready: per-profile credential readiness reusing session.ResolveProfile + CheckAuth for each of the five profiles (config.Profiles()) — ready = the profile's CheckAuth returns nil, reported as a per-profile boolean list, never the credential values themselves (a missing key is \"not ready\", not an error response).\n33\t     files: src/internal/session/docker.go (or a new src/internal/session/health.go), src/internal/session/health_test.go (new), src/internal/serve/api.go, src/internal/serve/api_test.go\n34\t     depends: TASK-1\n35\t     risk: low-medium — one new docker shell-out behind the existing seam; profile checks are pure env reads, no network\n36\t\n37\tTASK-6: binding + bearer-token auth (brief scope item 4 + Security). Token configured out-of-band — flag, else MG_SERVE_TOKEN via config.EnvValue (config/env per the brief; never issued, created, or returned by the API) — and when configured, require `Authorization: Bearer <token>` on every endpoint (constant-time comparison via crypto/subtle; a missing/wrong token is 401). Tokenless when unset, for the localhost default. Startup guard — the hard invariant: refuse to start (clear error, exit non-zero) when the resolved bind address is NOT loopback (neither 127.0.0.0/8 nor ::1) and NO token is configured — a remote daemon must be authed (brief: \"a token without TLS is no protection\"; TLS is the reverse proxy's job — the daemon always serves plain HTTP and never terminates TLS, which is also the only safe posture behind Caddy/nginx). auth_test.go: token present → 200 with header, 401 without/wrong; constant-time compare (no early-exit timing giveaway is fine to assert structurally); tokenless localhost starts; non-loopback without token refuses to start.\n38\t     files: src/internal/serve/auth.go (new), src/internal/serve/auth_test.go (new), src/cmd/mg/serve.go (startup guard)\n39\t     depends: TASK-3\n40\t     risk: high — the new trust boundary's gate; the non-loopback-without-token refusal must be unskippable (no flag that turns it off) and pinned by a test\n41\t\n42\tTASK-7: zero path inputs + job-id resolution (brief scope item 5, the Security section's first non-negotiable). Every handler resolves through the TASK-1/TASK-3 choke points: the URL project segment is matched against registry entries (by exact path, then base name) — NEVER joined into a filesystem path; the job segment is resolved against the project's Discovered jobs by ID, then by id_slug name, then by unique prefix (the same semantics as the CLI's branch-tail matching — an id_slug is the job directory name) — NEVER treated as a path; the file segment is the TASK-3 whitelist. Reject traversal outright: `..`, absolute paths, backslashes, percent-encoded and double-encoded variants, NUL bytes. security_test.go (new): a suite of hostile URLs (encoded traversal against every path position, e.g. /projects/..%2f..%2fetc%2fpasswd, absolute-path segments, Windows separators, double-encoding) asserting 4xx and — critically — no filesystem read outside the registered roots (the handler-level assertion is: the resolution helper returns not-found rather than a path the handler could open).\n43\t     files: src/internal/serve/api.go (the resolution helpers), src/internal/serve/security_test.go (new)\n44\t     depends: TASK-3, TASK-4\n45\t     risk: high — the brief's explicit \"nothing from the URL is trusted as a filesystem path\"; the choke point must be the only way any handler obtains a root/job/file path, or the guarantee is decorative\n46\t\n47\tTASK-8: credentials never returned (brief scope item 6, Security's second non-negotiable). Response-surface audit + enforcement-by-test: every response shape is whitelisted (registry data, job rows, file contents, jdi status, diff text, agent name+description, health booleans) and nothing else; credentials_test.go (new) loads the daemon's real credential source (config.EnvValue over the same .env/process env the daemon reads) and asserts that no response body from ANY endpoint contains the value of any credential key (CLAUDE_CODE_OAUTH_TOKEN, OPENCODE_API_KEY, ZHIPU_API_KEY, MG_SERVE_TOKEN, the OPENCODE_*_MODEL keys) nor any full line of .env content; the TASK-9 audit log likewise never records the Authorization header or the token. A test that puts a known credential value in the env, hits every endpoint, and greps every body is the guarantee the brief demands (\"no .env content, no keys, no tokens in any response, ever\").\n48\t     files: src/internal/serve/api.go (response-shape review), src/internal/serve/credentials_test.go (new)\n49\t     depends: TASK-3, TASK-6\n50\t     risk: high — a guarantee enforced only by tests over the whole surface; the fixtures must come from the same config.EnvValue path the daemon uses, so the test and the server can never drift on what counts as a credential\n51\t\n52\tTASK-9: audit trail (brief scope item 7). Request-log middleware: one line per request carrying timestamp, client (IP — the remote address; never log the Authorization header, the token, or request bodies), method + path (the operation), auth outcome (authed / tokenless / 401), and response status; written to a caller-supplied io.Writer (default: the daemon's stderr, the same stream mg serve already logs to; a file destination is a possible --flag addition but stderr default keeps v1 small). audit_test.go: lines carry timestamp/client/operation/outcome, every request is logged (including 4xx/401s), and no line contains credential material (a request carrying a known token value must not echo it).\n53\t     files: src/internal/serve/audit.go (new), src/internal/serve/audit_test.go (new)\n54\t     depends: TASK-3, TASK-6\n55\t     risk: low — middleware + writer, no state; the no-credentials-in-log assertion is the only teeth, and it overlaps TASK-8's guarantee\n56\t\n57\tTASK-10: serialization skeleton (brief scope item 8). Establish the per-project concurrency pattern now so job two's mutating API inherits it instead of inventing it under pressure: a keyed-lock structure (e.g. serve.ProjectLocks) keyed by registered project root — Lock(name) / Unlock(name) serializing a per-project critical section, independent across projects — with a doc comment stating this is the pattern job two's mutating handlers MUST use (internal/git has no locking today). v1 read endpoints do NOT take the locks (reads need no exclusion), so the structure ships unused by handlers but fully tested: locks_test.go proves mutual exclusion within one project key and independence across two keys, and pins the API shape the mutating job will call.\n58\t     files: src/internal/serve/locks.go (new), src/internal/serve/locks_test.go (new)\n59\t     depends: TASK-1 (keyed by registry roots)\n60\t     risk: low — additive structure, unused by v1 endpoints; the risk is drift between the skeleton and what job two expects, mitigated by the doc comment and the pinned API-shape test\n61\t\n62\tTASK-11: docs + help sync. docs/AGENTS.md: add `mg serve` to the dispatcher/command inventory (the Architecture \"cmd/mg/main.go\" list and the Commands section) and add a \"Listener / control plane\" architecture subsection — daemon, project registry (config file, restart-to-change), read-only API surface, binding + bearer-token auth model (tokenless localhost, token-required remote, TLS via reverse proxy), audit trail, and the security invariants (zero path inputs, credentials never returned, per-project serialization for job two). project-template/docs/AGENTS.md: mirror the same additions (hard rule: the two AGENTS.md files describe the same system). README.md: a command-table row for `mg serve` (next to mg tui/mg jdi) and a short \"Listener\" section — registry config file location/shape, MG_SERVE_TOKEN, localhost-vs-VPS binding rule, TLS via Caddy/nginx, and a pointer to the out-of-scope list (mutating endpoints / streaming / frontends are later jobs). docs/web-interface.md: its \"Job one: the daemon\" recommendation (the mg serve + registry + auth slice) is now DONE — annotate it as superseded by the listener decision per the brief's recommendation point 4 (\"this document supersedes that recommendation; no need to re-litigate\"), a short annotation, not a rewrite. Command help text itself is TASK-2's.\n63\t     files: docs/AGENTS.md, project-template/docs/AGENTS.md, README.md, docs/web-interface.md\n64\t     depends: TASK-1 through TASK-10 (accurate doc content needs the shipped behavior)\n65\t     risk: low — doc edits only; the sync rule is the discipline, and the web-interface.md annotation must record the supersession without contradicting the brief\n66\t\n67\t-->"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"5af88135-9c7b-453b-b119-c245e62a60fb","uuid":"9f431387-f63a-46dc-9984-eacff30969eb","timestamp":"2026-08-28T20:21:28.725Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/workspace/docs/jobs/archive/wood_listener-architecture/tasks.md","content":"# Tasks: listener architecture\n\nid: wood\nstatus: open\nanalyst: wood\ndate: 2026-08-27\n\n<!-- Produced by @analyst from brief.md. -->\n\n## Task breakdown\n\n<!-- TASK-1: Project registry in a new daemon package. Add a new internal package for the daemon (suggested name src/internal/serve — the home for the registry, HTTP server, handlers, auth, audit and locks; one package f