skippable (no flag that turns it off) and pinned by a test\n\nTASK-7: zero path inputs + job-id resolution (brief scope item 5, the Security section's first non-negotiable). Every handler resolves through the TASK-1/TASK-3 choke points: the URL project segment is matched against registry entries (by exact path, then base name) — NEVER joined into a filesystem path; the job segment is resolved against the project's Discovered jobs by ID, then by id_slug name, then by unique prefix (the same semantics as the CLI's branch-tail matching — an id_slug is the job directory name) — NEVER treated as a path; the file segment is the TASK-3 whitelist. Reject traversal outright: `..`, absolute paths, backslashes, percent-encoded and double-encoded variants, NUL bytes. security_test.go (new): a suite of hostile URLs (encoded traversal against every path position, e.g. /projects/..%2f..%2fetc%2fpasswd, absolute-path segments, Windows separators, double-encoding) asserting 4xx and — critically — no filesystem read outside the registered roots (the handler-level assertion is: the resolution helper returns not-found rather than a path the handler could open).\n     files: src/internal/serve/api.go (the resolution helpers), src/internal/serve/security_test.go (new)\n     depends: TASK-3, TASK-4\n     risk: high — the brief's explicit \"nothing from the URL is trusted as a filesystem path\"; the choke point must be the only way any handler obtains a root/job/file path, or the guarantee is decorative\n\nTASK-8: credentials never returned (brief scope item 6, Security's second non-negotiable). Response-surface audit + enforcement-by-test: every response shape is whitelisted (registry data, job rows, file contents, jdi status, diff text, agent name+description, health booleans) and nothing else; credentials_test.go (new) loads the daemon's real credential source (config.EnvValue over the same .env/process env the daemon reads) and asserts that no response body from ANY endpoint contains the value of any credential key (CLAUDE_CODE_OAUTH_TOKEN, OPENCODE_API_KEY, ZHIPU_API_KEY, MG_SERVE_TOKEN, the OPENCODE_*_MODEL keys) nor any full line of .env content; the TASK-9 audit log likewise never records the Authorization header or the token. A test that puts a known credential value in the env, hits every endpoint, and greps every body is the guarantee the brief demands (\"no .env content, no keys, no tokens in any response, ever\").\n     files: src/internal/serve/api.go (response-shape review), src/internal/serve/credentials_test.go (new)\n     depends: TASK-3, TASK-6\n     risk: high — a guarantee enforced only by tests over the whole surface; the fixtures must come from the same config.EnvValue path the daemon uses, so the test and the server can never drift on what counts as a credential\n\nTASK-9: audit trail (brief scope item 7). Request-log middleware: one line per request carrying timestamp, client (IP — the remote address; never log the Authorization header, the token, or request bodies), method + path (the operation), auth outcome (authed / tokenless / 401), and response status; written to a caller-supplied io.Writer (default: the daemon's stderr, the same stream mg serve already logs to; a file destination is a possible --flag addition but stderr default keeps v1 small). audit_test.go: lines carry timestamp/client/operation/outcome, every request is logged (including 4xx/401s), and no line contains credential material (a request carrying a known token value must not echo it).\n     files: src/internal/serve/audit.go (new), src/internal/serve/audit_test.go (new)\n     depends: TASK-3, TASK-6\n     risk: low — middleware + writer, no state; the no-credentials-in-log assertion is the only teeth, and it overlaps TASK-8's guarantee\n\nTASK-10: serialization skeleton (brief scope item 8). Establish the per-project concurrency pattern now so job two's mutating API inherits it instead of inventing it under pressure: a keyed-lock structure (e.g. serve.ProjectLocks) keyed by registered project root — Lock(name) / Unlock(name) serializing a per-project critical section, independent across projects — with a doc comment stating this is the pattern job two's mutating handlers MUST use (internal/git has no locking today). v1 read endpoints do NOT take the locks (reads need no exclusion), so the structure ships unused by handlers but fully tested: locks_test.go proves mutual exclusion within one project key and independence across two keys, and pins the API shape the mutating job will call.\n     files: src/internal/serve/locks.go (new), src/internal/serve/locks_test.go (new)\n     depends: TASK-1 (keyed by registry roots)\n     risk: low — additive structure, unused by v1 endpoints; the risk is drift between the skeleton and what job two expects, mitigated by the doc comment and the pinned API-shape test\n\nTASK-11: docs + help sync. docs/AGENTS.md: add `mg serve` to the dispatcher/command inventory (the Architecture \"cmd/mg/main.go\" list and the Commands section) and add a \"Listener / control plane\" architecture subsection — daemon, project registry (config file, restart-to-change), read-only API surface, binding + bearer-token auth model (tokenless localhost, token-required remote, TLS via reverse proxy), audit trail, and the security invariants (zero path inputs, credentials never returned, per-project serialization for job two). project-template/docs/AGENTS.md: mirror the same additions (hard rule: the two AGENTS.md files describe the same system). README.md: a command-table row for `mg serve` (next to mg tui/mg jdi) and a short \"Listener\" section — registry config file location/shape, MG_SERVE_TOKEN, localhost-vs-VPS binding rule, TLS via Caddy/nginx, and a pointer to the out-of-scope list (mutating endpoints / streaming / frontends are later jobs). docs/web-interface.md: its \"Job one: the daemon\" recommendation (the mg serve + registry + auth slice) is now DONE — annotate it as superseded by the listener decision per the brief's recommendation point 4 (\"this document supersedes that recommendation; no need to re-litigate\"), a short annotation, not a rewrite. Command help text itself is TASK-2's.\n     files: docs/AGENTS.md, project-template/docs/AGENTS.md, README.md, docs/web-interface.md\n     depends: TASK-1 through TASK-10 (accurate doc content needs the shipped behavior)\n     risk: low — doc edits only; the sync rule is the discipline, and the web-interface.md annotation must record the supersession without contradicting the brief\n\n-->","numLines":67,"startLine":1,"totalLines":67}}}
