{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01TCJZgJLpc83fvKavac7JNf","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Brief: part 2 of web ui tui path\n2\t\n3\tstatus: open\n4\ttype: feature\n5\tid: farmer\n6\tbranch: feature/farmer_part-2-of-web-ui-tui-path\n7\tdate: 2026-08-28\n8\tauthor: Leander Muskalla\n9\t\n10\t## What\n11\t\n12\tJob two of the control-plane sequence (`docs/listener.md`). Job one shipped\n13\t`mg serve` as a read-only daemon; this job adds mutating endpoints and live\n14\trun supervision on top of it, so the daemon becomes able to actually drive a\n15\tjob's lifecycle, not just report on it.\n16\t\n17\t- **Mutating endpoints**, each serialized per project via the existing\n18\t  `serve.ProjectLocks` skeleton (`internal/serve/locks.go` — `Lock(root)` /\n19\t  `defer Unlock(root)` around the critical section):\n20\t  - Create a job (`internal/job.CreateJob`).\n21\t  - Edit a job's `brief.md` (raw content replace — no `$EDITOR`, this is an\n22\t    HTTP body write).\n23\t  - Launch an agent detached, one-shot, via the `--print` path (never an\n24\t    attached/interactive session).\n25\t  - Launch `mg jdi` detached for a job.\n26\t  - `done` (`FinishJob`), `delete` (`DeleteJob`), push.\n27\t  - Orphan cleanup: `mg prune` (containers) and orphaned-worktree removal\n28\t    (`job.RemoveOrphans`) — a headless daemon with nobody running the CLI by\n29\t    hand will accumulate exactly this cruft, so this needs an endpoint (or a\n30\t    background sweep), not just CLI parity.\n31\t- **Live run supervision**: stream `session.log` growth over WebSocket/SSE\n32\t  instead of polling — the reader side of roadmap #5, riding the writer-side\n33\t  capture that already exists. This is the same file the TUI's `l` key tails\n34\t  (`tail -f` on `docs/jobs/<id>_<slug>/session.log`); job two exposes that\n35\t  same live tail over the network instead of a local pane. It is not a\n36\t  separate \"container log\" concept — `session.log` already is the captured\n37\t  stream for both containerized and host-mode runs.\n38\t- **`NEEDS-HUMAN-INPUT:` visibility**: job one's `/jdi` endpoint already\n39\t  surfaces the marker via the status sidecar. What job two needs to decide\n40\t  (see Notes) is whether \"answering\" one is its own endpoint or just the\n41\t  composition of the edit-brief + relaunch endpoints above.\n42\t\n43\t## Why\n44\t\n45\tThe read-only daemon (job one) can only show what happened; nobody can act on\n46\tit without going back to the terminal. That defeats the point of a remote/VPS\n47\tcontrol plane — the whole \"review from a phone, get a ping when a job needs\n48\tyou, act on it\" story requires write access and live output, not just a\n49\tstatus snapshot. This job is what turns `mg serve` from a dashboard into an\n50\tactual control plane, and it's the direct prerequisite for job three (the web\n51\tUI) — building a frontend against polling instead of this job's streaming\n52\twould just be \"a worse TUI with more pixels\" (per `docs/listener.md`).\n53\t\n54\t## Out of scope\n55\t\n56\t- **Interactive agent sessions** (`mg`, `mg host`) — no attach-and-chat over\n57\t  the API in any form. Roadmap item 6 (in-TUI embedded terminal) is the one\n58\t  place this bet gets built, on whichever surface the product commits to, not\n59\t  duplicated here.\n60\t- **Host-machine config commands** (`mg profiles`, `mg theme`, `mg setup`,\n61\t  `mg serve-token`) — credential/environment configuration stays CLI-only.\n62\t- **Any frontend** (web UI or native GUI) — job three.\n63\t- **User accounts / teams** — bearer-token auth stays the v1 model.\n64\t- **`mg pull`** — independently useful, its own job.\n65\t- Anything not listed under \"What\" above.\n66\t\n67\t## Notes\n68\t\n69\tOpen questions to resolve before this goes to @analyst:\n70\t\n71\t- **`mg done` conflict handling.** Today a squash-merge conflict is an\n72\t  interactive prompt: offer to hand off to `@git-solver` via `mg host`, or\n73\t  roll back on decline. There's no human at a terminal to answer that prompt\n74\t  over HTTP. Pin what the `done` endpoint does instead — most likely: report\n75\t  the conflict as a structured error and leave the job untouched (no\n76\t  automatic rollback, no automatic git-solver handoff), requiring an explicit\n77\t  follow-up decision through some other call. Do not let this fall back to\n78\t  silently picking one of the two existing CLI behaviors.\n79\t- **What \"answer `NEEDS-HUMAN-INPUT:`\" actually means.** There is no existing\n80\t  \"answer\" mechanism even in the CLI/TUI today — a human currently just edits\n81\t  the relevant file or launches an ordinary interactive agent, then re-runs\n82\t  `mg jdi`. Decide whether job two needs a dedicated endpoint at all, or\n83\t  whether \"answering\" is just: edit-brief (or another job file) + relaunch\n84\t  `mg jdi`, using the endpoints already in scope. Favor the latter unless a\n85\t  concrete need for something more shows up.\n86\t- **Streaming transport**: WebSocket vs. SSE — either is fine technically for\n87\t  a one-way tail; pick whichever is less code against the existing\n88\t  `net/http` server, and confirm it survives the daemon's graceful-shutdown\n89\t  drain (`serveShutdownDrain`) cleanly (client disconnect on shutdown, not a\n90\t  hang).\n91\t- **`ProjectLocks` scope**: confirm which operations actually need the lock.\n92\t  Per `internal/serve/locks.go`'s own doc comment, it's for git-mutating\n93\t  operations (create/done/delete/push) contending on the same project's git\n94\t  metadata — launching an agent or streaming logs doesn't touch git state and\n95\t  arguably shouldn't block behind the same lock. Don't serialize more than\n96\t  necessary; a job-launch that has to wait behind an unrelated `done` defeats\n97\t  the point of a responsive control plane.\n98\t- Reuse job one's zero-path-input discipline exactly: every mutating handler\n99\t  resolves project/job through the same `resolveProject`/`resolveJob` helpers\n100\t  in `internal/serve/api.go` — never a new ad hoc path-join.\n101\t\n102\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"ba948ea7-a157-4c42-8eb1-077540d9946d","uuid":"2a26c1e0-672d-4ec9-9085-b1f463104c89","timestamp":"2026-08-28T20:24:30.808Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/workspace/docs/jobs/farmer_part-2-of-web-ui-tui-path/brief.md","content":"# Brief: part 2 of web ui tui path\n\nstatus: open\ntype: feature\nid: farmer\nbranch: feature/farmer_part-2-of-web-ui-tui-path\ndate: 2026-08-28\nauthor: Leander Muskalla\n\n## What\n\nJob two of the control-plane sequence (`docs/listener.md`). Job one shipped\n`mg serve` as a read-only daemon; this job adds mutating endpoints and live\nrun supervision on top of it, so the daemon becomes able to actually drive a\njob's lifecycle, not just report on it.\n\n- **Mutating endpoints**, each serialized per project via the existing\n  `serve.ProjectLocks` skeleton (`internal/serve/locks.go` — `Lock(root)` /\n  `defer Unlock(root)` around the critical section):\n  - Create a job (`internal/job.CreateJob`).\n  - Edit a job's `brief.md` (raw content replace — no `$EDITOR`, this is an\n    HTTP body write).\n  - Launch an agent detached, one-shot, via the `--print` path (never an\n    attached/interactive session).\n  - Launch `mg jdi` detached for a job.\n  - `done` (`FinishJob`), `delete` (`DeleteJob`), push.\n  - Orphan cleanup: `mg prune` (containers) and orphaned-worktree removal\n    (`job.RemoveOrphans`) — a headless daemon with nobody running the CLI by\n    hand will accumulate exactly this cruft, so this needs an endpoint (or a\n    background sweep), not just CLI parity.\n- **Live run supervision**: stream `session.log` growth over WebSocket/SSE\n  instead of polling — the reader side of roadmap #5, riding the writer-side\n  capture that already exists. This is the same file the TUI's `l` key tails\n  (`tail -f` on `docs/jobs/<id>_<slug>/session.log`); job two exposes that\n  same live tail over the network instead of a local pane. It is not a\n  separate \"container log\" concept — `session.log` already is the captured\n  stream for both containerized and host-mode runs.\n- **`NEEDS-HUMAN-INPUT:` visibility**: job one's `/jdi` endpoint already\n  surfaces the marker via the status sidecar. What job two needs to decide\n  (see Notes) is whether \"answering\" one is its own endpoint or just the\n  composition of the edit-brief + relaunch endpoints above.\n\n## Why\n\nThe read-only daemon (job one) can only show what happened; nobody can act on\nit without going back to the terminal. That defeats the point of a remote/VPS\ncontrol plane — the whole \"review from a phone, get a ping when a job needs\nyou, act on it\" story requires write access and live output, not just a\nstatus snapshot. This job is what turns `mg serve` from a dashboard into an\nactual control plane, and it's the direct prerequisite for job three (the web\nUI) — building a frontend against polling instead of this job's streaming\nwould just be \"a worse TUI with more pixels\" (per `docs/listener.md`).\n\n## Out of scope\n\n- **Interactive agent sessions** (`mg`, `mg host`) — no attach-and-chat over\n  the API in any form. Roadmap item 6 (in-TUI embedded terminal) is the one\n  place this bet gets built, on whichever surface the product commits to, not\n  duplicated here.\n- **Host-machine config commands** (`mg profiles`, `mg theme`, `mg setup`,\n  `mg serve-token`) — credential/environment configuration stays CLI-only.\n- **Any frontend** (web UI or native GUI) — job three.\n- **User accounts / teams** — bearer-token auth stays the v1 model.\n- **`mg pull`** — independently useful, its own job.\n- Anything not listed under \"What\" above.\n\n## Notes\n\nOpen questions to resolve before this goes to @analyst:\n\n- **`mg done` conflict handling.** Today a squash-merge conflict is an\n  interactive prompt: offer to hand off to `@git-solver` via `mg host`, or\n  roll back on decline. There's no human at a terminal to answer that prompt\n  over HTTP. Pin what the `done` endpoint does instead — most likely: report\n  the conflict as a structured error and leave the job untouched (no\n  automatic rollback, no automatic git-solver handoff), requiring an explicit\n  follow-up decision through some other call. Do not let this fall back to\n  silently picking one of the two existing CLI behaviors.\n- **What \"answer `NEEDS-HUMAN-INPUT:`\" actually means.** There is no existing\n  \"answer\" mechanism even in the CLI/TUI today — a human currently just edits\n  the relevant file or launches an ordinary interactive agent, then re-runs\n  `mg jdi`. Decide whether job two needs a dedicated endpoint at all, or\n  whether \"answering\" is just: edit-brief (or another job file) + relaunch\n  `mg jdi`, using the endpoints already in scope. Favor the latter unless a\n  concrete need for something more shows up.\n- **Streaming transport**: WebSocket vs. SSE — either is fine technically for\n  a one-way tail; pick whichever is less code against the existing\n  `net/http` server, and confirm it survives the daemon's graceful-shutdown\n  drain (`serveShutdownDrain`) cleanly (client disconnect on shutdown, not a\n  hang).\n- **`ProjectLocks` scope**: confirm which operations actually need the lock.\n  Per `internal/serve/locks.go`'s own doc comment, it's for git-mutating\n  operations (create/done/delete/push) contending on the same project's git\n  metadata — launching an agent or streaming logs doesn't touch git state and\n  arguably shouldn't block behind the same lock. Don't serialize more than\n  necessary; a job-launch that has to wait behind an unrelated `done` defeats\n  the point of a responsive control plane.\n- Reuse job one's zero-path-input discipline exactly: every mutating handler\n  resolves project/job through the same `resolveProject`/`resolveJob` helpers\n  in `internal/serve/api.go` — never a new ad hoc path-join.\n\n","numLines":102,"startLine":1,"totalLines":102}}}
