{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_013AAPfUecKRx376JjWrNpzf","type":"tool_result","content":"320\t//\n321\t// profile is one of the subscription profiles in config.Profiles, matching\n322\t// Agent/Quick — an empty value defaults to config.ProfileClaudePro, mg-jdi's\n323\t// own default (see cmd/mg/jdi.go).\n324\t//\n325\t// cmd.Dir stays on projectRoot (the main worktree) even though the job lives\n326\t// in its own worktree: mg-jdi — like every other\n327\t// `--job` invocation — re-derives the effective worktree root itself from the\n328\t// job id it is given, per invocation, so it must be launched from the project\n329\t// root that find_project_root expects, never from a job worktree directly.\n330\t// See Agent's doc for the full reasoning, shared by all four launch paths.\n331\t//\n332\t// Unlike Agent/Quick there is no \"where it opened\" description to return —\n333\t// there is no terminal/pane to describe — so this returns only an error (nil\n334\t// on a successful start). Like launchDetached, the started process's stdio is\n335\t// discarded and it is reaped asynchronously so it cannot corrupt the TUI's\n336\t// alt screen or zombie; mg-jdi may run for a long time (several full agent\n337\t// sessions), so this must not block waiting for it.\n338\tfunc Jdi(jobID, projectRoot, profile string) error {\n339\t\tif profile == \"\" {\n340\t\t\tprofile = config.ProfileClaudePro\n341\t\t}\n342\t\texe, err := JdiExe()\n343\t\tif err != nil {\n344\t\t\treturn fmt.Errorf(\"locate mg binary: %w\", err)\n345\t\t}\n346\t\tcmd := exec.Command(exe, \"jdi\", \"--job\", jobID, \"--profile\", profile)\n347\t\tcmd.Dir = projectRoot\n348\t\tcmd.Stdin = nil\n349\t\tcmd.Stdout = io.Discard\n350\t\tcmd.Stderr = io.Discard\n351\t\tif err := cmd.Start(); err != nil {\n352\t\t\treturn fmt.Errorf(\"start mg jdi: %w\", err)\n353\t\t}\n354\t\tif cmd.Process != nil {\n355\t\t\tgo func() { _ = cmd.Wait() }()\n356\t\t}\n357\t\treturn nil\n358\t}\