em into the same session.log section rather than dropping them silently. Flag explicitly, as a documented known limitation rather than something this task solves: a concurrently-running mg-jdi loop against the SAME job would also be writing to the same session.log file at the same time (safe against file corruption — both are append-only, single-writer-per-section — but the underlying git worktree itself would have two independent processes touching it, which this job does not attempt to detect or prevent).\n     files: new file under src/internal/serve (or src/internal/session, whichever avoids an import cycle and best sets up cmd/mg/jdi.go to eventually reuse it too — confirm the exact home while implementing) plus its test file with a stubbed/fake docker invocation, mirroring cmd/mg/jdi_test.go's AgentRunner fake\n     depends: TASK-1\n     risk: medium — a new concurrency shape (a goroutine outliving its triggering HTTP request) around a docker/container invocation path that must not silently swallow a fast failure (auth error, docker not running) with no way for the caller to learn about it except by tailing session.log.\n\nTASK-3: Create-job endpoint. POST /projects/{project}/jobs — wraps job.CreateJob(root, title, opts, out) under s.locks.Lock(root)/defer Unlock(root) (git-mutating: creates a branch + worktree, one of the operations the brief's Notes names explicitly). Request body: {\"title\": \"...\", \"type\": \"feature|fix|chore\" (optional, default feature), \"baseBranch\": \"...\" (optional override)} — validate type against CreateJob's own accepted set before calling so a bad value is a clean 400 rather than surfacing CreateJob's own \"Invalid type\" error text as a 500. Response: the created job's row (mirroring the existing jobRow shape) plus the branch/worktree path from CreateResult. CreateJob's out io.Writer (its human-readable summary) can be discarded or captured for debugging — decide while implementing; the response's structured fields are the source of truth either way.\n     files: src/internal/serve/api.go, src/internal/serve/server.go (route registration), src/internal/serve/api_test.go\n     depends: none\n     risk: low — job.CreateJob is an existing, well-tested pure function already used by the CLI and TUI; this is a thin adapter plus the lock.\n\nTASK-4: Edit-brief endpoint. PUT /projects/{project}/jobs/{job}/files/brief — raw-body replace of the job's brief.md, the one job file this job's brief explicitly makes writable (\"no $EDITOR, this is an HTTP body write\" — tasks/implementation/verdict stay read-only, written only by their respective agents). After writing the new content to j.Dir/brief.md, immediately commit it in the job's own worktree via session.SweepJobWorktree(root, diag) (the same \"commit everything uncommitted\" primitive every session-ending sweep already uses, message \"[<id>] chore: commit all\") — without this, an edit made through this endpoint sits as an uncommitted change in the job's worktree until something else happens to sweep it, risking silent loss the next time anything resets/checks out that worktree. Recommend this task also takes s.locks.Lock(root): the commit itself touches git, even though the brief's Notes paragraph only names create/done/delete/push explicitly in its git-mutating set — flagging this as this task's own reasoned addition (a real race if a done/delete runs against the same worktree mid-write), not something the brief pins either way. Validate the request body is non-empty and under a sane size cap.\n     files: src/internal/serve/api.go, src/internal/serve/server.go, src/internal/serve/api_test.go\n     depends: none\n     risk: medium — the commit-after-write design (and whether it needs the project lock) is a real decision with data-loss implications if skipped; otherwise low implementation complexity.\n\nTASK-5: Launch-agent-detached endpoint. POST /projects/{project}/jobs/{job}/agents/{agent} — launches one agent, one-shot, via TASK-2's primitive (never an attached/interactive session, per the brief's explicit out-of-scope note — no attach-and-chat over the API in any form). Validate {agent} against agentlist.Discover(root) first, for a fast 404 on an unknown agent name rather than letting an invalid agent fail deep inside a container spin-up. Accept an optional {\"profile\": \"...\"} body field, defaulting to config.ProfileClaudePro like every other launch path. Does NOT take s.locks: per the brief's Notes, launching an agent doesn't touch git state and shouldn't block behind an unrelated done/delete. Response: 202 Accepted — the run is now happening in the background; the caller watches it via TASK-12's session-log stream or by polling the existing GET .../jdi endpoint's sessionLog tail.\n     files: src/internal/serve/api.go, src/internal/serve/server.go, src/internal/serve/api_test.go\n     depends: TASK-2\n     risk: medium — the async response contract (202, then \"go watch the log\") is new to this API's surface (every existing v1 endpoint is synchronous); getting the client-facing contract right here sets the pattern TASK-6 also follows.\n\nTASK-6: Launch-jdi-detached endpoint. POST /projects/{project}/jobs/{job}/jdi — wraps launch.Jdi(jobID, root, profile), which is already exactly the shape this needs (a detached subprocess, no attached terminal, stdio discarded, reaped asynchronously — no changes needed to internal/launch itself). Before launching, check job.ReadJDIStatus(root, j.Name) for an already-running state and reject with 409 if found — a best-effort guard against two concurrent mg-jdi loops racing on the same job's git branch. Note explicitly that this is not airtight (the sidecar \"running\" status is written by the launched process itself, so two launches issued back-to-back before the first process has written its status could still both proceed) — an accepted, documented gap rather than something this task builds real locking for, which the brief does not ask for. No s.locks (same reasoning as TASK-5). Response: 202 Accepted.\n     files: src/internal/serve/api.go, src/internal/serve/server.go, src/internal/serve/api_test.go\n     depends: none\n     risk: low-medium — launch.Jdi is an existing, already-daemon-safe function; the main new risk is the double-launch race, explicitly documented as best-effort.\n\nTASK-7: Done endpoint + FinishJob conflict-handling change. POST /projects/{project}/jobs/{job}/done — wraps job.FinishJob under s.locks.Lock(root) (one of the brief's explicitly named git-mutating operations). The brief pins that a squash-merge conflict must take NEITHER of FinishJob's two existing interactive behaviors (offer @git-solver via mg host, or auto-rollback) — it must report a structured error and leave things as-is, \"requiring an explicit follow-up decision through some other call,\" and explicitly forbids silently falling back to either existing behavior. This needs a small, additive change to internal/job/finish.go: today handleSquashMergeFailure always prompts (via the confirm func) and, on decline, unconditionally rolls the main worktree back with git.ResetHard. Add a new option (e.g. a FinishOptions struct wrapping the existing positional args, or a package-level sentinel confirm value FinishJob recognizes) that, on a squash-merge conflict, skips the prompt-and-recover branch entirely and returns a new, distinguishable error (e.g. a wrapped ErrSquashMergeConflict) immediately — no rollback, no git-solver launch — leaving the main worktree in its conflicted-merge state and the job already archived-and-committed in its own worktree (that step runs before the merge attempt in FinishJob's existing sequence and cannot be undone without a much larger restructuring that is out of scope here; the response should say so explicitly rather than implying \"nothing happened\"). This must be purely additive: existing callers (the CLI's mg done, the TUI) keep their current confirm-based behavior unchanged — verify against finish_test.go's existing coverage untouched. Separately, and NOT pinned by the brief at all: decide what the done endpoint does about the earlier \"verdict not approved\" / \"no verdict.md\" warnings. Recommend requiring the caller to pass {\"force\": true} to proceed past those warnings (echoing the CLI's own warning text back in a 409 when force is ab... (line truncated to 2000 chars)\n     files: src/internal/job/finish.go, src/internal/job/finish_test.go, src/internal/serve/api.go, src/internal/serve/server.go, src/internal/serve/api_test.go\n     depends: none\n     risk: high — the most residual product ambiguity of any task here (the brief explicitly forbids guessing between the two existing CLI behaviors, and the force-vs-auto-approve call for the earlier warnings isn't pinned at all); it also modifies job.FinishJob, a shared lifecycle function the CLI and TUI both depend on today, so the change must be strictly additive.\n\nTASK-8: Delete endpoint. POST /projects/{project}/jobs/{job}/delete — wraps job.DeleteJob under s.locks.Lock(root) (git-mutating, named explicitly in the brief). Passes a pre-approved confirm function, mirroring internal/ui/app.go's yesConfirm — the HTTP call itself is the confirmation, the same precedent the TUI already established for its own confirm-then-call pattern. No conflict-handling ambiguity like done — DeleteJob has no merge step. Response: the DeleteResult (job name, branch, dir).\n     files: src/internal/serve/api.go, src/internal/serve/server.go, src/internal/serve/api_test.go\n     depends: none\n     risk: low — DeleteJob's confirm-bypass is an already-proven pattern (yesConfirm), just reused from a new caller.\n\nTASK-9: Push endpoint. POST /projects/{project}/jobs/{job}/push — resolves the job (the existing resolveJob helper) and runs git.PushWithContext(ctx, root, j.Branch) (git push -u origin <branch>, mirroring the TUI git panel's \"Push to origin\" action — src/internal/ui/app.go's pushCmd) under s.locks.Lock(root) (named explicitly in the brief's git-mutating set). Bound the call with a timeout mirroring the TUI's own hostGitTimeout (30s, src/internal/ui/app.go) rather than an unbounded context — long enough for a real push, short enough that a stalled/unreachable remote can't hang the request forever. A push failure (auth, network, non-fast-forward) surfaces as a structured error with git's own message, never silently swallowed.\n     files: src/internal/serve/api.go, src/internal/serve/server.go, src/internal/serve/api_test.go\n     depends: none\n     risk: low-medium — a real network call inside an HTTP handler; the main risk is an unbounded hang without a deliberate timeout, mitigated by reusing the TUI's own proven timeout value.\n\nTASK-10: Prune endpoint. POST /prune — wraps session.PruneOrphans, the same function cmd/mg/prune.go's runPrune calls. Decide during implementation whether this belongs under /projects/{project}/ at all: orphaned manigot-* containers are not partitioned by project (docker has no concept of \"which registered project\" a leftover container belongs to), so a single top-level, project-less route matching mg prune's own scope is likely the more honest shape than nesting it under one project's URL. No s.locks (no git or project-scoped state at all). Response mirrors mg prune's own reporting: removed count, running count.\n     files: src/internal/serve/api.go, src/internal/serve/server.go, src/internal/serve/api_test.go\n     depends: none\n     risk: low — thin wrapper around an existing, already-daemon-safe function with no TTY/interactive dependency.\n\nTASK-11: Orphaned-worktree list + cleanup endpoints. GET /projects/{project}/orphans (read-only, lists job.DiscoverOrphans(root) — name/dir/gitdir, mirroring the existing read-only endpoints' style) and POST /projects/{project}/orphans/{name}/delete (resolves one named orphan via job.MatchOrphan, then job.RemoveOrphansConfirmed(root, []Orphan{o}, out) for that single orphan) — one named resource per mutating call, consistent with every other endpoint in this API, rather than a single \"delete everything\" call that risks removing something the caller didn't expect to see gone. Take s.locks.Lock(root) for the delete: RemoveOrphans/RemoveOrphansConfirmed calls git.WorktreePrune, which touches git worktree metadata and can race with a concurrent create/done/delete on the same project. Flag explicitly: the brief's Notes paragraph does not name orphan cleanup in its git-mutating set at all, so locking it here is this task's own reasoned addition, offered for confirmation rather than assumed as already-decided.\n     files: src/internal/serve/api.go, src/internal/serve/server.go, src/internal/serve/api_test.go\n     depends: none\n     risk: medium — the \"list then delete-by-name\" API shape is a design call not explicitly pinned by the brief (which also floats \"a background sweep\" as an alternative); consistent with the rest of the surface, but worth confirming against what job three's UI actually wants before client-side assumptions build on it.\n\nTASK-12: session.log SSE streaming endpoint. GET /projects/{project}/jobs/{job}/session-log/stream — Server-Sent Events, chosen over WebSocket per the brief's own steer (\"pick whichever is less code against the existing net/http server\"): SSE is plain HTTP response streaming with no upgrade handshake or framing and needs no new dependency, unlike WebSocket, which Go's stdlib doesn't support natively. Implementation: open the job's session.log, seek to EOF (or accept a ?from=<byte offset> query param mirroring readFileTail's tail semantics for a client reconnecting after a drop), then poll for growth on a short interval (e.g. 500ms — the file is a local append-only file, so a simple stat+read-new-bytes loop is sufficient; no fsnotify/inotify needed) and flush each new chunk as an SSE `data:` frame (http.Flusher). Must stop cleanly on client disconnect (r.Context().Done()) AND participate in the daemon's graceful shutdown drain (serveShutdownDrain in cmd/mg/serve.go): plain http.Server.Shutdown does NOT itself interrupt a handler stuck in a polling loop — it only stops accepting new connections and waits for in-flight handlers to return — so the Server struct needs its own shutdown signal (e.g. a context stored on *Server, cancelled at the top of Shutdown before calling s.http.Shutdown) that this handler selects on alongside the request context and its poll ticker. Without this, every open stream would force the daemon to wait out the full drain timeout on every graceful shutdown — exactly what the brief calls out to confirm/avoid.\n     files: src/internal/serve/server.go (Server gets a shutdown context/field), src/internal/serve/stream.go (new), src/internal/serve/stream_test.go (new — httptest-driven: asserts growth is observed live, disconnect stops the handler goroutine, and a simulated shutdown returns promptly rather than hanging)\n     depends: none structurally, but a realistic end-to-end test benefits from TASK-1/TASK-2 already writing something to session.log during a launch\n     risk: high — the graceful-shutdown interaction is the one correctness requirement the brief explicitly calls out as needing verification, and a background-goroutine-per-stream shape is new to this codebase; getting it wrong leaks goroutines or hangs shutdown.\n\nTASK-13: Security + credentials test extension across the new mutating surface. Extend the existing security_test.go (zero-path-inputs) and credentials_test.go (no credential material in any response) suites — both established by job one — to cover every endpoint added in TASK-3 through TASK-12: hostile/encoded traversal segments against the new {project}/{job}/{agent}/{orphan-name} URL positions, and a pass confirming no mutating endpoint's response or error body ever echoes .env content, tokens, or account UUIDs (the same known-credential-value-in-env-then-grep-every-body technique job one's credentials_test.go already uses, re-run across the new surface).\n     files: src/internal/serve/security_test.go, src/internal/serve/credentials_test.go\n     depends: TASK-3, TASK-4, TASK-5, TASK-6, TASK-7, TASK-8, TASK-9, TASK-10, TASK-11, TASK-12\n     risk: low — test-only, but the surface to cover is large; the real risk is a rushed pass leaving a gap in the two invariants job one treated as non-negotiable.\n\nTASK-14: ProjectLocks boundary verification pass. A focused review + test pass confirming the locking boundary actually matches the design each task above states: create (TASK-3), edit-brief (TASK-4), done (TASK-7), delete (TASK-8), push (TASK-9), and orphan-delete (TASK-11) take s.locks.Lock(root); launch-agent (TASK-5), launch-jdi (TASK-6), prune (TASK-10), and every read endpoint do NOT. Add a concurrency test (extending locks_test.go, or a new integration test) proving two concurrent mutating calls against the SAME project serialize (e.g. a slow fake done blocks a concurrent delete) while a launch-agent call against the same project completes without waiting behind it, and that two different projects' mutating calls proceed independently — the exact property the brief's Notes worries about: \"a job-launch that has to wait behind an unrelated done defeats the point of a responsive control plane.\"\n     files: src/internal/serve/locks_test.go, or a new src/internal/serve/concurrency_test.go\n     depends: TASK-3, TASK-4, TASK-5, TASK-6, TASK-7, TASK-8, TASK-9, TASK-10, TASK-11\n     risk: low-medium — mostly verification against already-implemented handlers, but the locking boundary is a real correctness property (both over- and under-serializing are bugs), so it earns its own explicit test rather than trusting each task's author silently.\n\nTASK-15: Docs + help sync. docs/AGENTS.md (and its mirror project-template/docs/AGENTS.md — the hard rule that both describe the same system): extend the \"Listener / control plane\" subsection job one added with the new mutating endpoints, the async 202-then-poll/stream contract, the SSE streaming endpoint, and the ProjectLocks boundary actually shipped (which operations lock, which don't, and why). README.md: extend its existing \"Listener\" section (line ~1153) the same way. docs/listener.md: annotate its \"Sequencing after this job\" section marking \"Job two — mutating API + run supervision\" as done, mirroring how job one's own doc-sync task annotated docs/web-interface.md rather than rewriting it (see docs/jobs/archive/wood_listener-architecture/tasks.md TASK-11 for the precedent) — a short annotation, not a rewrite, and without contradicting the brief's own recorded decisions.\n     files: docs/AGENTS.md, project-template/docs/AGENTS.md, README.md, docs/listener.md\n     depends: TASK-1 through TASK-14 (accurate doc content needs the shipped behavior, especially TASK-7's and TASK-11's final API shapes)\n     risk: low — doc edits only; the discipline is keeping the three files in sync and not overstating what shipped.\n\n-->","lineStart":1,"lineEnd":87,"totalLines":87,"truncated":false}},"title":"docs/jobs/farmer_part-2-of-web-ui-tui-path/tasks.md","time":{"start":1787950239136,"end":1787950239143}},"id":"prt_04a238b280014RPaReRWLltl2f","sessionID":"ses_fb5dcd7a6ffeSGBPKFVeVpziRm","messageID":"msg_04a2383f1001aVVKFNBUklEBqA"}}
