{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1787952189225,"sessionID":"ses_fb5dcd7a6ffeSGBPKFVeVpziRm","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"read","callID":"call_87073d96aede4c3c88b7ee0f","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"/workspace/docs/AGENTS.md","limit":25,"offset":775},"output":"<path>/workspace/docs/AGENTS.md</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n775:   `~/.config/opencode/AGENTS.md` for OpenCode) as a copy — never a symlink,\n776:   since a symlink would let Claude's `/memory` writes and agent edits land\n777:   back in the checkout — and never clobbering an existing host file. For work\n778:   that must touch the host itself (thematic alias: `mg wild`, same\n779:   command/behavior)\n780: - `mg serve [--addr <host>] [--port <n>] [--registry <path>] [--token <value>]`\n781:   — the listener: a long-running daemon exposing a read-only control API\n782:   (projects, jobs, job files, jdi status + logs, diff, agents, health) over a\n783:   registry of project roots, so any surface — a web UI, a native GUI, a future\n784:   CLI — can attach to it as a client, from localhost or from a VPS. Binds\n785:   `127.0.0.1:8080` by default (tokenless — the machine's own user is the trust\n786:   boundary); a non-loopback bind REQUIRES a bearer token (`--token` or\n787:   `$MG_SERVE_TOKEN`) or the daemon refuses to start. TLS is the reverse\n788:   proxy's job (Caddy/nginx), never the daemon's. See \"Listener / control\n789:   plane\" below.\n790: - `mg serve-token` — generate a fresh secure bearer token for `mg serve` and\n791:   write it into manigot's `.env` as `MG_SERVE_TOKEN` (32 random bytes hex via\n792:   `crypto/rand`); the daemon reads the token once at startup, so it takes\n793:   effect on the next start. The only way the token is provisioned — the API\n794:   itself never issues, creates, or returns one.\n795: \n796: ## Job workflow\n797: Each job lives in `docs/jobs/<id>_<slug>/` with four files:\n798: `brief.md` (what/why, filled in by the user), `tasks.md` (`@analyst`),\n799: `implementation.md` (`@developer`), `verdict.md` (`@reviewer` / `@security`).\n\n(Showing lines 775-799 of 847. Use offset=800 to continue.)\n</content>","metadata":{"preview":"  `~/.config/opencode/AGENTS.md` for OpenCode) as a copy — never a symlink,\n  since a symlink would let Claude's `/memory` writes and agent edits land\n  back in the checkout — and never clobbering an existing host file. For work\n  that must touch the host itself (thematic alias: `mg wild`, same\n  command/behavior)\n- `mg serve [--addr <host>] [--port <n>] [--registry <path>] [--token <value>]`\n  — the listener: a long-running daemon exposing a read-only control API\n  (projects, jobs, job files, jdi status + logs, diff, agents, health) over a\n  registry of project roots, so any surface — a web UI, a native GUI, a future\n  CLI — can attach to it as a client, from localhost or from a VPS. Binds\n  `127.0.0.1:8080` by default (tokenless — the machine's own user is the trust\n  boundary); a non-loopback bind REQUIRES a bearer token (`--token` or\n  `$MG_SERVE_TOKEN`) or the daemon refuses to start. TLS is the reverse\n  proxy's job (Caddy/nginx), never the daemon's. See \"Listener / control\n  plane\" below.\n- `mg serve-token` — generate a fresh secure bearer token for `mg serve` and\n  write it into manigot's `.env` as `MG_SERVE_TOKEN` (32 random bytes hex via\n  `crypto/rand`); the daemon reads the token once at startup, so it takes\n  effect on the next start. The only way the token is provisioned — the API\n  itself never issues, creates, or returns one.","truncated":true,"loaded":[],"display":{"type":"file","path":"/workspace/docs/AGENTS.md","text":"  `~/.config/opencode/AGENTS.md` for OpenCode) as a copy — never a symlink,\n  since a symlink would let Claude's `/memory` writes and agent edits land\n  back in the checkout — and never clobbering an existing host file. For work\n  that must touch the host itself (thematic alias: `mg wild`, same\n  command/behavior)\n- `mg serve [--addr <host>] [--port <n>] [--registry <path>] [--token <value>]`\n  — the listener: a long-running daemon exposing a read-only control API\n  (projects, jobs, job files, jdi status + logs, diff, agents, health) over a\n  registry of project roots, so any surface — a web UI, a native GUI, a future\n  CLI — can attach to it as a client, from localhost or from a VPS. Binds\n  `127.0.0.1:8080` by default (tokenless — the machine's own user is the trust\n  boundary); a non-loopback bind REQUIRES a bearer token (`--token` or\n  `$MG_SERVE_TOKEN`) or the daemon refuses to start. TLS is the reverse\n  proxy's job (Caddy/nginx), never the daemon's. See \"Listener / control\n  plane\" below.\n- `mg serve-token` — generate a fresh secure bearer token for `mg serve` and\n  write it into manigot's `.env` as `MG_SERVE_TOKEN` (32 random bytes hex via\n  `crypto/rand`); the daemon reads the token once at startup, so it takes\n  effect on the next start. The only way the token is provisioned — the API\n  itself never issues, creates, or returns one.\n\n## Job workflow\nEach job lives in `docs/jobs/<id>_<slug>/` with four files:\n`brief.md` (what/why, filled in by the user), `tasks.md` (`@analyst`),\n`implementation.md` (`@developer`), `verdict.md` (`@reviewer` / `@security`).","lineStart":775,"lineEnd":799,"totalLines":847,"truncated":true}},"title":"docs/AGENTS.md","time":{"start":1787952189220,"end":1787952189224}},"id":"prt_04a414e70001hzdD0QYJ7AeaO0","sessionID":"ses_fb5dcd7a6ffeSGBPKFVeVpziRm","messageID":"msg_04a4148f30017uUge18XxnYnuJ"}}
