{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1787952215209,"sessionID":"ses_fb5dcd7a6ffeSGBPKFVeVpziRm","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"read","callID":"call_c51114cd423544be9ada2245","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"/workspace/project-template/docs/AGENTS.md","limit":45,"offset":60},"output":"<path>/workspace/project-template/docs/AGENTS.md</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n60: The manigot TUI's job detail view also offers a `t` key that opens the job's\n61: branch diff in tig (`mg diff <job> --tig`) in a tmux split pane / new\n62: terminal, gated on tig being installed on the host, and an `l` key that\n63: tails the job's `session.log` — the verbose raw stream mg-jdi writes each\n64: invocation's output into as it happens, distinct from the log tab's\n65: `run.log` event summary — live in a tmux split pane / new terminal (piped\n66: through `jq` when available on the host, falling back to a plain `tail -f`\n67: otherwise), gated on a session.log existing for the job (created at mg-jdi\n68: run start).\n69: `mg serve` is the listener: a long-running daemon exposing a read-only control\n70: API over a registry of named project roots (an explicit config file,\n71: `<manigot checkout>/config/serve.json`, holding `{\"projects\":\n72: [{\"name\": \"my-project\", \"path\": \"/abs/root\"}, ...]}` — `name` is the required,\n73: operator-chosen URL segment the project is served under; no scanning;\n74: changing it means editing and restarting), so any surface — a web UI, a\n75: native GUI, a future CLI — can attach to it as a client from localhost or a\n76: VPS. It binds `127.0.0.1:8080` by default (tokenless —\n77: the machine's own user is the trust boundary); a non-loopback bind REQUIRES a\n78: bearer token (`--token` or `$MG_SERVE_TOKEN`) or the daemon refuses to start,\n79: and TLS is the reverse proxy's job (Caddy/nginx), never the daemon's. The v1\n80: API is read-only (projects, jobs, job files, jdi status + logs, diff, agents,\n81: health) and is a new trust boundary: URL segments are never joined into\n82: filesystem paths, credentials are never returned in any response, every\n83: request is audit-logged (never the token), and mutating operations (a later\n84: job) must serialize per project root via the `serve.ProjectLocks` pattern.\n85: The token is provisioned out-of-band: `mg serve-token` generates a fresh one\n86: into the manigot `.env` as `MG_SERVE_TOKEN`, read once at daemon startup — the\n87: API itself never issues, creates, or returns one.\n88: Copying text from inside a session uses OSC 52: your terminal emulator must\n89: support it, and tmux needs `set-clipboard on` when the session runs inside\n90: tmux (mg forwards your terminal environment into the container and warns at\n91: session start when it detects tmux would swallow the clipboard writes — see\n92: the manigot README's \"Clipboard / copying from agent sessions\" section).\n93: One deliberate exception in that forwarding: OpenCode sessions never see\n94: `TMUX`/`TMUX_PANE`, because OpenCode's TMUX-gated tmux DCS-passthrough OSC 52\n95: form is discarded by default tmux config (`allow-passthrough` off) — stripping\n96: `TMUX` makes it emit plain OSC 52, which tmux's `set-clipboard on` handles.\n97: The `shot` tool (`/usr/local/bin/shot`, see the manigot README's PLAYWRIGHT\n98: doc) renders a URL to a PNG + model-free render report. The developer agent\n99: uses it to verify rendered work; read-only agents consume the artifacts and\n100: never run it.\n101: Claude Code respects your host terminal's own color theme automatically;\n102: OpenCode needs an explicit theme setting, which manigot exposes as a global\n103: setting shared across every OpenCode profile (`mg theme <name>`, or the\n104: TUI's settings screen) — see the manigot README's Theme section. This is a\n\n(Showing lines 60-104 of 133. Use offset=105 to continue.)\n</content>","metadata":{"preview":"The manigot TUI's job detail view also offers a `t` key that opens the job's\nbranch diff in tig (`mg diff <job> --tig`) in a tmux split pane / new\nterminal, gated on tig being installed on the host, and an `l` key that\ntails the job's `session.log` — the verbose raw stream mg-jdi writes each\ninvocation's output into as it happens, distinct from the log tab's\n`run.log` event summary — live in a tmux split pane / new terminal (piped\nthrough `jq` when available on the host, falling back to a plain `tail -f`\notherwise), gated on a session.log existing for the job (created at mg-jdi\nrun start).\n`mg serve` is the listener: a long-running daemon exposing a read-only control\nAPI over a registry of named project roots (an explicit config file,\n`<manigot checkout>/config/serve.json`, holding `{\"projects\":\n[{\"name\": \"my-project\", \"path\": \"/abs/root\"}, ...]}` — `name` is the required,\noperator-chosen URL segment the project is served under; no scanning;\nchanging it means editing and restarting), so any surface — a web UI, a\nnative GUI, a future CLI — can attach to it as a client from localhost or a\nVPS. It binds `127.0.0.1:8080` by default (tokenless —\nthe machine's own user is the trust boundary); a non-loopback bind REQUIRES a\nbearer token (`--token` or `$MG_SERVE_TOKEN`) or the daemon refuses to start,\nand TLS is the reverse proxy's job (Caddy/nginx), never the daemon's. The v1","truncated":true,"loaded":[],"display":{"type":"file","path":"/workspace/project-template/docs/AGENTS.md","text":"The manigot TUI's job detail view also offers a `t` key that opens the job's\nbranch diff in tig (`mg diff <job> --tig`) in a tmux split pane / new\nterminal, gated on tig being installed on the host, and an `l` key that\ntails the job's `session.log` — the verbose raw stream mg-jdi writes each\ninvocation's output into as it happens, distinct from the log tab's\n`run.log` event summary — live in a tmux split pane / new terminal (piped\nthrough `jq` when available on the host, falling back to a plain `tail -f`\notherwise), gated on a session.log existing for the job (created at mg-jdi\nrun start).\n`mg serve` is the listener: a long-running daemon exposing a read-only control\nAPI over a registry of named project roots (an explicit config file,\n`<manigot checkout>/config/serve.json`, holding `{\"projects\":\n[{\"name\": \"my-project\", \"path\": \"/abs/root\"}, ...]}` — `name` is the required,\noperator-chosen URL segment the project is served under; no scanning;\nchanging it means editing and restarting), so any surface — a web UI, a\nnative GUI, a future CLI — can attach to it as a client from localhost or a\nVPS. It binds `127.0.0.1:8080` by default (tokenless —\nthe machine's own user is the trust boundary); a non-loopback bind REQUIRES a\nbearer token (`--token` or `$MG_SERVE_TOKEN`) or the daemon refuses to start,\nand TLS is the reverse proxy's job (Caddy/nginx), never the daemon's. The v1\nAPI is read-only (projects, jobs, job files, jdi status + logs, diff, agents,\nhealth) and is a new trust boundary: URL segments are never joined into\nfilesystem paths, credentials are never returned in any response, every\nrequest is audit-logged (never the token), and mutating operations (a later\njob) must serialize per project root via the `serve.ProjectLocks` pattern.\nThe token is provisioned out-of-band: `mg serve-token` generates a fresh one\ninto the manigot `.env` as `MG_SERVE_TOKEN`, read once at daemon startup — the\nAPI itself never issues, creates, or returns one.\nCopying text from inside a session uses OSC 52: your terminal emulator must\nsupport it, and tmux needs `set-clipboard on` when the session runs inside\ntmux (mg forwards your terminal environment into the container and warns at\nsession start when it detects tmux would swallow the clipboard writes — see\nthe manigot README's \"Clipboard / copying from agent sessions\" section).\nOne deliberate exception in that forwarding: OpenCode sessions never see\n`TMUX`/`TMUX_PANE`, because OpenCode's TMUX-gated tmux DCS-passthrough OSC 52\nform is discarded by default tmux config (`allow-passthrough` off) — stripping\n`TMUX` makes it emit plain OSC 52, which tmux's `set-clipboard on` handles.\nThe `shot` tool (`/usr/local/bin/shot`, see the manigot README's PLAYWRIGHT\ndoc) renders a URL to a PNG + model-free render report. The developer agent\nuses it to verify rendered work; read-only agents consume the artifacts and\nnever run it.\nClaude Code respects your host terminal's own color theme automatically;\nOpenCode needs an explicit theme setting, which manigot exposes as a global\nsetting shared across every OpenCode profile (`mg theme <name>`, or the\nTUI's settings screen) — see the manigot README's Theme section. This is a","lineStart":60,"lineEnd":104,"totalLines":133,"truncated":true}},"title":"project-template/docs/AGENTS.md","time":{"start":1787952215204,"end":1787952215207}},"id":"prt_04a41b2800011PgHGFu1pKRotT","sessionID":"ses_fb5dcd7a6ffeSGBPKFVeVpziRm","messageID":"msg_04a41aa80001EHW9HUFPgW64Rn"}}
