{"type":"step_finish","timestamp":1787952251086,"sessionID":"ses_fb5dcd7a6ffeSGBPKFVeVpziRm","part":{"id":"prt_04a4240c4001ROcbu4c4DpqETQ","reason":"tool-calls","snapshot":"","messageID":"msg_04a42033e001kFWlKH6o8myf7g","sessionID":"ses_fb5dcd7a6ffeSGBPKFVeVpziRm","type":"step-finish","tokens":{"total":302999,"input":1397,"output":2338,"reasoning":0,"cache":{"write":0,"read":299264}},"cost":0.009229612}}
{"type":"step_start","timestamp":1787952252822,"sessionID":"ses_fb5dcd7a6ffeSGBPKFVeVpziRm","part":{"id":"prt_04a424795001Tv5GLVvZkw0A34","messageID":"msg_04a4240cd001XabsYT5tzBgVbA","sessionID":"ses_fb5dcd7a6ffeSGBPKFVeVpziRm","snapshot":"","type":"step-start"}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1787952253216,"sessionID":"ses_fb5dcd7a6ffeSGBPKFVeVpziRm","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"read","callID":"call_5bbb9ca901ab45a2a2096763","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"/workspace/docs/listener.md"},"output":"<path>/workspace/docs/listener.md</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n1: # Listener: one control plane on a port — any surface attaches\n2: \n3: Exploration notes (2026-08-27). Verdict: **SHIP** — recorded as the basis for\n4: the listener job (job one of the control-plane sequence). This resolves the\n5: fork that `docs/gui.md` and `docs/web-interface.md` both hit the same day:\n6: instead of choosing between a web UI and a native GUI as *the* surface, the\n7: daemon is built once, and every surface — web UI, native GUI, future CLI —\n8: becomes an interchangeable client of the same API. No third surface; one\n9: foundation, two frontends, built one at a time.\n10: \n11: ## Decision recorded (2026-08-27)\n12: \n13: - **The listener is the product; the GUI is a client of it.** \"Make mg listen\n14:   on a port\" is the buildable unit. The GUI is the last slice, not the first.\n15: - **The web-vs-GUI fork is dissolved.** Both `gui.md` and `web-interface.md`\n16:   concluded REVISIT because a surface had to be chosen before the foundations\n17:   existed. A shared control API removes the choice: local or VPS, browser or\n18:   desktop — same daemon, same API, different binding and auth.\n19: - **Control plane only, on every surface.** No interactive agent terminals in\n20:   any frontend until roadmap item 6 (in-TUI embedded terminal) lands. That bet\n21:   is built once, on the surface the product commits to — not twice in two\n22:   frontends.\n23: - **The TUI stays in-process.** The listener is additive. Forcing the TUI\n24:   through the API in v1 buys nothing and risks the working 70%.\n25: - **One frontend at a time.** The listener makes the second surface cheap (one\n26:   shared API client, one shared info-design port) — not free. Build one first;\n27:   the second is a smaller job only after the first proves the need.\n28: - **The daemon is a state server, not a command socket.** Its value is live\n29:   state — jobs, runs, files, diffs — with commands attached. A pure\n30:   \"command-execution over HTTP\" listener is a worse TUI with more pixels.\n31: \n32: ## The idea\n33: \n34: A long-running `mg serve` process that listens on a port and exposes a control\n35: API over the existing in-process machinery — `internal/job`, `internal/git`,\n36: the `mg jdi` state machine, the session launcher — so anything can connect to\n37: it: a web UI, a native GUI, a future CLI, from localhost or from a VPS, and\n38: control everything.\n39: \n40: The same daemon serves both deployment shapes the user asked for:\n41: \n42: - **Local:** bound to `127.0.0.1`, no auth (the machine's own user is the\n43:   trust boundary, as it is for the CLI today).\n44: - **Remote (VPS):** bound to an address behind a TLS reverse proxy, bearer\n45:   token required.\n46: \n47: The process model changes: today every `mg` invocation is a one-shot CLI that\n48: discovers a single project from `$PWD`. The daemon is long-lived and holds a\n49: **project registry** — multi-project is a new concept for manigot.\n50: \n51: ## What already works\n52: \n53: The heavy machinery is terminal-independent and in-process-callable today:\n54: \n55: - **`internal/job`** — `CreateJob`, `FinishJob`, `DeleteJob`, `Discover`,\n56:   `ReadJDIStatus` are pure functions over a project root. An HTTP handler can\n57:   call the same functions in-process. No subprocess mg, no TTY.\n58: - **`internal/git`** — worktree create/remove, squash merge, push, diff,\n59:   commits, branch resolution (the job id → branch → worktree matching the CLI\n60:   already uses: exact then prefix).\n61: - **`mg jdi`** — fully unattended via the `--print` path, pollable status\n62:   sidecars (`.manigot/jdi-status/`), `NEEDS-HUMAN-INPUT:` markers, ntfy\n63:   notifications. This is the state model the API renders.\n64: - **`session.log`** — roadmap #5's writer side has landed: every\n65:   non-interactive invocation's step-level output is persisted to the job.\n66:   The reader side (streaming it live) is part of job two below.\n67: - **The sweep-commit** — every job-worktree session ends committed by the host.\n68:   What makes headless server operation safe in the first place.\n69: - **Docker isolation** — the containment story is the product's core safety\n70:   property and works identically on a server. The daemon does not touch it.\n71: - **Credentials + profiles** — resolved via the existing `internal/config`\n72:   layer; the daemon reads them exactly as the session launcher does.\n73: \n74: ## The listener job — scope\n75: \n76: The basis for `mg job`. Scope is the daemon and a **read-only** API.\n77: \n78: ### In scope\n79: \n80: 1. **`mg serve` subcommand** — a new subcommand in the single binary's\n81:    dispatcher (like `tui` and `jdi`), long-running, listening on a port.\n82:    Bind address/port via flags with a sensible localhost default.\n83: 2. **Project registry** — an explicit config file of named project roots:\n84:    `{\"projects\": [{\"name\": \"my-project\", \"path\": \"/abs/root\"}, ...]}`, where\n85:    `name` is the required, operator-chosen URL segment the project is served\n86:    under. No scanning, no auto-adopting directories the daemon finds.\n87:    Registrations are read at startup; changing them means editing the config\n88:    and restarting (v1). The daemon resolves nothing outside the registered\n89:    roots, and a URL segment resolves by configured `name` only.\n90: 3. **Read-only API** — the state the TUI renders today, exposed over HTTP:\n91:    - projects list\n92:    - jobs per project — the TUI's info design: id / status / stage / type /\n93:      date / title, one row per job\n94:    - job files — brief / tasks / implementation / verdict (read)\n95:    - jdi status + `run.log` + `session.log` (read)\n96:    - job diff — the `mg diff` quick eyeball (log + stat; full patch behind a\n97:      flag), resolved against the job's base branch\n98:    - agents available to a project (the `mg agents` list)\n99:    - a health/status endpoint (version, image present, profiles ready)\n100: 4. **Binding and auth** — localhost default, no auth; remote exposure requires\n101:    a bearer token. TLS is the reverse proxy's job (Caddy/nginx), not the\n102:    daemon's. Token configured out-of-band (config/env), never issued by the\n103:    API.\n104: 5. **Zero path inputs** — every operation resolved against the registry's\n105:    roots; job IDs resolved via the same branch-matching logic the CLI uses.\n106:    Nothing from the URL is trusted as a filesystem path.\n107: 6. **Credentials never returned** — no `.env` content, no keys, no tokens in\n108:    any response, ever.\n109: 7. **Audit trail** — a request log (timestamp, client, operation). A remote or\n110:    shared box changes the accountability picture; the local TUI never needed\n111:    \"who ran what\", the daemon does.\n112: 8. **Serialization skeleton** — the concurrency pattern for mutating\n113:    operations (per-project) is established in the daemon's structure now, even\n114:    though v1 exposes no mutating endpoints — `internal/git` has no locking\n115:    today, and job two's mutating API inherits the pattern.\n116: \n117: ### Out of scope\n118: \n119: - **Mutating endpoints** — create/edit/launch/`done`/`delete`/push. That is\n120:   job two.\n121: - **Detached run supervision with streamed logs** — watching a live agent run\n122:   with step-level output (the #5 reader side). Job two.\n123: - **WebSocket/SSE streaming.** Job two.\n124: - **Any frontend** — web UI or native GUI. Job three.\n125: - **Interactive agent terminals** in any surface. Roadmap item 6, built once,\n126:   last.\n127: - **Migrating the TUI onto the API** — the TUI stays in-process.\n128: - **`mg pull`** — small, independently useful, its own job.\n129: - **User accounts / teams** — single user + bearer token is the v1 auth model.\n130: - **Anything not in the list above.** The API is read-only; if an endpoint\n131:   mutates state, it is out of scope.\n132: \n133: ## Security\n134: \n135: The daemon is a new trust boundary with root-adjacent power: it drives docker\n136: and git and holds subscription credentials. A compromised endpoint means an\n137: attacker running agents on the paid subscription and committing to the repos.\n138: Non-negotiable:\n139: \n140: - **Bearer-token auth over TLS only.** Localhost binds may be tokenless; a\n141:   token without TLS is no protection.\n142: - **Zero path inputs from the client** — see scope item 5.\n143: - **Credentials never returned** by any API or rendered anywhere — see scope\n144:   item 6.\n145: - **Audit trail** — see scope item 7.\n146: - **Concurrency** — mutating operations serialized per project (job two's\n147:   implementation; the skeleton lands in this job).\n148: - **Cost** — a remote daemon makes firing runs easy, and unattended agent runs\n149:   burn subscription quota. The API surfaces run activity; ntfy stays the away\n150:   digest. No new notification machinery.\n151: \n152: ## Sequencing after this job\n153: \n154: - **Now, zero code (validation):** put `mg jdi` + ntfy on a VPS and drive a\n155:   real job from a phone. The away-digest experience may cover a large share of\n156:   the \"control from anywhere\" need at near-zero cost, and it proves the server\n157:   story before more code exists. It also tells us which frontend to build\n158:   first: remote need → web UI; local glanceable need → native GUI.\n159: - **Job two — mutating API + run supervision:** create/edit brief, launch\n160:   agents and `mg jdi` detached, watch runs live with streamed logs (the #5\n161:   reader side, riding the `session.log` capture already landed), answer\n162:   `NEEDS-HUMAN-INPUT:` markers, `done`/`delete`/push. Per-project serialization\n163:   enforced here.\n164: - **Job three — one frontend:** an embedded web UI port of the TUI's\n165:   information design (on-brand one-binary, works from a phone, matches the VPS\n166:   story). The native GUI is then a port of the same API client + same info\n167:   design — a much smaller job, only if the local, glanceable need survives\n168:   real use.\n169: - **Independent:** `mg pull`; roadmap #4 (headless/queue) feeds the daemon's\n170:   value but blocks nothing here.\n171: \n172: ## Concerns\n173: \n174: 1. **The daemon's power** — see Security. This is the part with real teeth,\n175:    and the read-only-first scope is what makes the first job safe to ship.\n176: 2. **Concurrency** — no locking in `internal/git`; the mutating API must\n177:    serialize per project. The skeleton lands in this job so job two inherits\n178:    it rather than inventing it under pressure.\n179: 3. **Cost** — unattended runs on a server burn subscription quota; run\n180:    activity must be visible in the API from day one.\n181: 4. **Scope creep** — \"and control everything\" is how a read-only daemon becomes\n182:    a remote-desktop product. The out-of-scope list is explicit; if an endpoint\n183:    mutates state, it is a later job.\n184: \n185: ## Recommendation\n186: \n187: 1. Cut **job one (this job)** from the scope above: `mg serve` + project\n188:    registry + read-only API + localhost default + audit log + serialization\n189:    skeleton. Read-only first is what keeps the new trust boundary safe.\n190: 2. Validate the cheap version (jdi + ntfy on a VPS) in parallel — zero code,\n191:    and it decides which frontend job three should be.\n192: 3. Do **not** start the GUI or the mutating API until the read-only listener\n193:    is real and the event-stream reader side (job two's supervision) exists —\n194:    a surface built on polling is a worse TUI with more pixels.\n195: 4. After the listener lands, `docs/web-interface.md`'s \"job one: the daemon\"\n196:    is done — this document supersedes that recommendation; no need to re-litigate.\n\n(End of file - total 196 lines)\n</content>","metadata":{"preview":"# Listener: one control plane on a port — any surface attaches\n\nExploration notes (2026-08-27). Verdict: **SHIP** — recorded as the basis for\nthe listener job (job one of the control-plane sequence). This resolves the\nfork that `docs/gui.md` and `docs/web-interface.md` both hit the same day:\ninstead of choosing between a web UI and a native GUI as *the* surface, the\ndaemon is built once, and every surface — web UI, native GUI, future CLI —\nbecomes an interchangeable client of the same API. No third surface; one\nfoundation, two frontends, built one at a time.\n\n## Decision recorded (2026-08-27)\n\n- **The listener is the product; the GUI is a client of it.** \"Make mg listen\n  on a port\" is the buildable unit. The GUI is the last slice, not the first.\n- **The web-vs-GUI fork is dissolved.** Both `gui.md` and `web-interface.md`\n  concluded REVISIT because a surface had to be chosen before the foundations\n  existed. A shared control API removes the choice: local or VPS, browser or\n  desktop — same daemon, same API, different binding and auth.\n- **Control plane only, on every surface.** No interactive agent terminals in\n  any frontend until roadmap item 6 (in-TUI embedded terminal) lands. That bet","truncated":false,"loaded":[],"display":{"type":"file","path":"/workspace/docs/listener.md","text":"# Listener: one control plane on a port — any surface attaches\n\nExploration notes (2026-08-27). Verdict: **SHIP** — recorded as the basis for\nthe listener job (job one of the control-plane sequence). This resolves the\nfork that `docs/gui.md` and `docs/web-interface.md` both hit the same day:\ninstead of choosing between a web UI and a native GUI as *the* surface, the\ndaemon is built once, and every surface — web UI, native GUI, future CLI —\nbecomes an interchangeable client of the same API. No third surface; one\nfoundation, two frontends, built one at a time.\n\n## Decision recorded (2026-08-27)\n\n- **The listener is the product; the GUI is a client of it.** \"Make mg listen\n  on a port\" is the buildable unit. The GUI is the last slice, not the first.\n- **The web-vs-GUI fork is dissolved.** Both `gui.md` and `web-interface.md`\n  concluded REVISIT because a surface had to be chosen before the foundations\n  existed. A shared control API removes the choice: local or VPS, browser or\n  desktop — same daemon, same API, different binding and auth.\n- **Control plane only, on every surface.** No interactive agent terminals in\n  any frontend until roadmap item 6 (in-TUI embedded terminal) lands. That bet\n  is built once, on the surface the product commits to — not twice in two\n  frontends.\n- **The TUI stays in-process.** The listener is additive. Forcing the TUI\n  through the API in v1 buys nothing and risks the working 70%.\n- **One frontend at a time.** The listener makes the second surface cheap (one\n  shared API client, one shared info-design port) — not free. Build one first;\n  the second is a smaller job only after the first proves the need.\n- **The daemon is a state server, not a command socket.** Its value is live\n  state — jobs, runs, files, diffs — with commands attached. A pure\n  \"command-execution over HTTP\" listener is a worse TUI with more pixels.\n\n## The idea\n\nA long-running `mg serve` process that listens on a port and exposes a control\nAPI over the existing in-process machinery — `internal/job`, `internal/git`,\nthe `mg jdi` state machine, the session launcher — so anything can connect to\nit: a web UI, a native GUI, a future CLI, from localhost or from a VPS, and\ncontrol everything.\n\nThe same daemon serves both deployment shapes the user asked for:\n\n- **Local:** bound to `127.0.0.1`, no auth (the machine's own user is the\n  trust boundary, as it is for the CLI today).\n- **Remote (VPS):** bound to an address behind a TLS reverse proxy, bearer\n  token required.\n\nThe process model changes: today every `mg` invocation is a one-shot CLI that\ndiscovers a single project from `$PWD`. The daemon is long-lived and holds a\n**project registry** — multi-project is a new concept for manigot.\n\n## What already works\n\nThe heavy machinery is terminal-independent and in-process-callable today:\n\n- **`internal/job`** — `CreateJob`, `FinishJob`, `DeleteJob`, `Discover`,\n  `ReadJDIStatus` are pure functions over a project root. An HTTP handler can\n  call the same functions in-process. No subprocess mg, no TTY.\n- **`internal/git`** — worktree create/remove, squash merge, push, diff,\n  commits, branch resolution (the job id → branch → worktree matching the CLI\n  already uses: exact then prefix).\n- **`mg jdi`** — fully unattended via the `--print` path, pollable status\n  sidecars (`.manigot/jdi-status/`), `NEEDS-HUMAN-INPUT:` markers, ntfy\n  notifications. This is the state model the API renders.\n- **`session.log`** — roadmap #5's writer side has landed: every\n  non-interactive invocation's step-level output is persisted to the job.\n  The reader side (streaming it live) is part of job two below.\n- **The sweep-commit** — every job-worktree session ends committed by the host.\n  What makes headless server operation safe in the first place.\n- **Docker isolation** — the containment story is the product's core safety\n  property and works identically on a server. The daemon does not touch it.\n- **Credentials + profiles** — resolved via the existing `internal/config`\n  layer; the daemon reads them exactly as the session launcher does.\n\n## The listener job — scope\n\nThe basis for `mg job`. Scope is the daemon and a **read-only** API.\n\n### In scope\n\n1. **`mg serve` subcommand** — a new subcommand in the single binary's\n   dispatcher (like `tui` and `jdi`), long-running, listening on a port.\n   Bind address/port via flags with a sensible localhost default.\n2. **Project registry** — an explicit config file of named project roots:\n   `{\"projects\": [{\"name\": \"my-project\", \"path\": \"/abs/root\"}, ...]}`, where\n   `name` is the required, operator-chosen URL segment the project is served\n   under. No scanning, no auto-adopting directories the daemon finds.\n   Registrations are read at startup; changing them means editing the config\n   and restarting (v1). The daemon resolves nothing outside the registered\n   roots, and a URL segment resolves by configured `name` only.\n3. **Read-only API** — the state the TUI renders today, exposed over HTTP:\n   - projects list\n   - jobs per project — the TUI's info design: id / status / stage / type /\n     date / title, one row per job\n   - job files — brief / tasks / implementation / verdict (read)\n   - jdi status + `run.log` + `session.log` (read)\n   - job diff — the `mg diff` quick eyeball (log + stat; full patch behind a\n     flag), resolved against the job's base branch\n   - agents available to a project (the `mg agents` list)\n   - a health/status endpoint (version, image present, profiles ready)\n4. **Binding and auth** — localhost default, no auth; remote exposure requires\n   a bearer token. TLS is the reverse proxy's job (Caddy/nginx), not the\n   daemon's. Token configured out-of-band (config/env), never issued by the\n   API.\n5. **Zero path inputs** — every operation resolved against the registry's\n   roots; job IDs resolved via the same branch-matching logic the CLI uses.\n   Nothing from the URL is trusted as a filesystem path.\n6. **Credentials never returned** — no `.env` content, no keys, no tokens in\n   any response, ever.\n7. **Audit trail** — a request log (timestamp, client, operation). A remote or\n   shared box changes the accountability picture; the local TUI never needed\n   \"who ran what\", the daemon does.\n8. **Serialization skeleton** — the concurrency pattern for mutating\n   operations (per-project) is established in the daemon's structure now, even\n   though v1 exposes no mutating endpoints — `internal/git` has no locking\n   today, and job two's mutating API inherits the pattern.\n\n### Out of scope\n\n- **Mutating endpoints** — create/edit/launch/`done`/`delete`/push. That is\n  job two.\n- **Detached run supervision with streamed logs** — watching a live agent run\n  with step-level output (the #5 reader side). Job two.\n- **WebSocket/SSE streaming.** Job two.\n- **Any frontend** — web UI or native GUI. Job three.\n- **Interactive agent terminals** in any surface. Roadmap item 6, built once,\n  last.\n- **Migrating the TUI onto the API** — the TUI stays in-process.\n- **`mg pull`** — small, independently useful, its own job.\n- **User accounts / teams** — single user + bearer token is the v1 auth model.\n- **Anything not in the list above.** The API is read-only; if an endpoint\n  mutates state, it is out of scope.\n\n## Security\n\nThe daemon is a new trust boundary with root-adjacent power: it drives docker\nand git and holds subscription credentials. A compromised endpoint means an\nattacker running agents on the paid subscription and committing to the repos.\nNon-negotiable:\n\n- **Bearer-token auth over TLS only.** Localhost binds may be tokenless; a\n  token without TLS is no protection.\n- **Zero path inputs from the client** — see scope item 5.\n- **Credentials never returned** by any API or rendered anywhere — see scope\n  item 6.\n- **Audit trail** — see scope item 7.\n- **Concurrency** — mutating operations serialized per project (job two's\n  implementation; the skeleton lands in this job).\n- **Cost** — a remote daemon makes firing runs easy, and unattended agent runs\n  burn subscription quota. The API surfaces run activity; ntfy stays the away\n  digest. No new notification machinery.\n\n## Sequencing after this job\n\n- **Now, zero code (validation):** put `mg jdi` + ntfy on a VPS and drive a\n  real job from a phone. The away-digest experience may cover a large share of\n  the \"control from anywhere\" need at near-zero cost, and it proves the server\n  story before more code exists. It also tells us which frontend to build\n  first: remote need → web UI; local glanceable need → native GUI.\n- **Job two — mutating API + run supervision:** create/edit brief, launch\n  agents and `mg jdi` detached, watch runs live with streamed logs (the #5\n  reader side, riding the `session.log` capture already landed), answer\n  `NEEDS-HUMAN-INPUT:` markers, `done`/`delete`/push. Per-project serialization\n  enforced here.\n- **Job three — one frontend:** an embedded web UI port of the TUI's\n  information design (on-brand one-binary, works from a phone, matches the VPS\n  story). The native GUI is then a port of the same API client + same info\n  design — a much smaller job, only if the local, glanceable need survives\n  real use.\n- **Independent:** `mg pull`; roadmap #4 (headless/queue) feeds the daemon's\n  value but blocks nothing here.\n\n## Concerns\n\n1. **The daemon's power** — see Security. This is the part with real teeth,\n   and the read-only-first scope is what makes the first job safe to ship.\n2. **Concurrency** — no locking in `internal/git`; the mutating API must\n   serialize per project. The skeleton lands in this job so job two inherits\n   it rather than inventing it under pressure.\n3. **Cost** — unattended runs on a server burn subscription quota; run\n   activity must be visible in the API from day one.\n4. **Scope creep** — \"and control everything\" is how a read-only daemon becomes\n   a remote-desktop product. The out-of-scope list is explicit; if an endpoint\n   mutates state, it is a later job.\n\n## Recommendation\n\n1. Cut **job one (this job)** from the scope above: `mg serve` + project\n   registry + read-only API + localhost default + audit log + serialization\n   skeleton. Read-only first is what keeps the new trust boundary safe.\n2. Validate the cheap version (jdi + ntfy on a VPS) in parallel — zero code,\n   and it decides which frontend job three should be.\n3. Do **not** start the GUI or the mutating API until the read-only listener\n   is real and the event-stream reader side (job two's supervision) exists —\n   a surface built on polling is a worse TUI with more pixels.\n4. After the listener lands, `docs/web-interface.md`'s \"job one: the daemon\"\n   is done — this document supersedes that recommendation; no need to re-litigate.","lineStart":1,"lineEnd":196,"totalLines":196,"truncated":false}},"title":"docs/listener.md","time":{"start":1787952253212,"end":1787952253215}},"id":"prt_04a4248a5001D3jKzY6fDs75HZ","sessionID":"ses_fb5dcd7a6ffeSGBPKFVeVpziRm","messageID":"msg_04a4240cd001XabsYT5tzBgVbA"}}
