{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1787952466839,"sessionID":"ses_fb5ba93f2ffe9RBBAgSFG35dIN","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"read","callID":"call_0d95cf21935a44cd9513c2a2","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"/workspace/docs/jobs/farmer_part-2-of-web-ui-tui-path/tasks.md"},"output":"<path>/workspace/docs/jobs/farmer_part-2-of-web-ui-tui-path/tasks.md</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n1: # Tasks: part 2 of web ui tui path\n2: \n3: id: farmer\n4: status: open\n5: analyst: farmer\n6: date: 2026-08-28\n7: \n8: <!-- Produced by @analyst from brief.md. -->\n9: \n10: ## Task breakdown\n11: \n12: <!-- TASK-1: Relocate the session-log section-writer machinery out of cmd/mg. The sessionLog type, openSessionLog, and ensureSessionLogFile currently live privately in src/cmd/mg/jdioutput.go and are only ever called from cmd/mg/jdi.go's Run loop. A finding from reading the code: today NOTHING writes a job's session.log for a bare `mg --print --agent X --job Y` invocation outside the mg-jdi loop — contrary to docs/listener.md's claim that \"session.log already is the captured stream for both containerized and host-mode runs\" and this job's brief framing (\"riding the writer-side capture that already exists\"). Move sessionLog/openSessionLog/ensureSessionLogFile into a new exported file in internal/job (e.g. src/internal/job/sessionlog.go) so both cmd/mg and the new internal/serve package can write a live, tailable session.log for a one-shot invocation, not just an mg-jdi-driven one. This must be a pure relocation: the exact same on-disk format (blank-line section separator, \"=== <RFC3339> <agent> (attempt N) ===\" header, trailing-newline guarantee on Close) — cmd/mg/jdi.go is updated to call the relocated functions with no behavior change, and its existing coverage (jdioutput_test.go) moves/adapts to the new package rather than being rewritten, so mg-jdi's already-shipped log format (which the TUI's \"l\" tail and its jq pretty-print convention both depend on) cannot silently drift.\n13:      files: src/cmd/mg/jdioutput.go, src/cmd/mg/jdioutput_test.go, src/cmd/mg/jdi.go, src/internal/job/sessionlog.go (new), src/internal/job/sessionlog_test.go (new)\n14:      depends: none\n15:      risk: medium — touches mg-jdi's well-tested, carefully-documented session.log machinery; the relocation must be format-preserving, verified by moving rather than rewriting the existing tests.\n16: \n17: TASK-2: One-shot detached agent-run primitive. Add a \"run one agent invocation for a job, in-process, detached from the triggering HTTP request\" primitive that TASK-5's launch-agent endpoint calls. Mirror cmd/mg/jdi.go's commandAgentRunner.Run shape — session.ResolveProfile → session.ResolveRootFrom → info.CheckAuth → session.BuildDockerInvocation → inv.Run → session.SweepJobWorktree on success — but for a single --print invocation with no orchestrate.Next loop around it, opening one session.log section via TASK-1's relocated openSessionLog (attempt fixed at 1 — this is not part of an mg-jdi run, so there is no per-agent attempt counter to thread through) and closing it when the invocation returns. Run the whole thing in a background goroutine started by the HTTP handler so the request returns immediately instead of blocking for the invocation's full duration (which can be minutes); the goroutine's own errors have nowhere to report back to an already-returned HTTP response, so write them into the same session.log section rather than dropping them silently. Flag explicitly, as a documented known limitation rather than something this task solves: a concurrently-running mg-jdi loop against the SAME job would also be writing to the same session.log file at the same time (safe against file corruption — both are append-only, single-writer-per-section — but the underlying git worktree itself would have two independent processes touching it, which this job does not attempt to detect or prevent).\n18:      files: new file under src/internal/serve (or src/internal/session, whichever avoids an import cycle and best sets up cmd/mg/jdi.go to eventually reuse it too — confirm the exact home while implementing) plus its test file with a stubbed/fake docker invocation, mirroring cmd/mg/jdi_test.go's AgentRunner fake\n19:      depends: TASK-1\n20:      risk: medium — a new concurrency shape (a goroutine outliving its triggering HTTP request) around a docker/container invocation path that must not silently swallow a fast failure (auth error, docker not running) with no way for the caller to learn about it except by tailing session.log.\n21: \n22: TASK-3: Create-job endpoint. POST /projects/{project}/jobs — wraps job.CreateJob(root, title, opts, out) under s.locks.Lock(root)/defer Unlock(root) (git-mutating: creates a branch + worktree, one of the operations the brief's Notes names explicitly). Request body: {\"title\": \"...\", \"type\": \"feature|fix|chore\" (optional, default feature), \"baseBranch\": \"...\" (optional override)} — validate type against CreateJob's own accepted set before calling so a bad value is a clean 400 rather than surfacing CreateJob's own \"Invalid type\" error text as a 500. Response: the created job's row (mirroring the existing jobRow shape) plus the branch/worktree path from CreateResult. CreateJob's out io.Writer (its human-readable summary) can be discarded or captured for debugging — decide while implementing; the response's structured fields are the source of truth either way.\n23:      files: src/internal/serve/api.go, src/internal/serve/server.go (route registration), src/internal/serve/api_test.go\n24:      depends: none\n25:      risk: low — job.CreateJob is an existing, well-tested pure function already used by the CLI and TUI; this is a thin adapter plus the lock.\n26: \n27: TASK-4: Edit-brief endpoint. PUT /projects/{project}/jobs/{job}/files/brief — raw-body replace of the job's brief.md, the one job file this job's brief explicitly makes writable (\"no $EDITOR, this is an HTTP body write\" — tasks/implementation/verdict stay read-only, written only by their respective agents). After writing the new content to j.Dir/brief.md, immediately commit it in the job's own worktree via session.SweepJobWorktree(root, diag) (the same \"commit everything uncommitted\" primitive every session-ending sweep already uses, message \"[<id>] chore: commit all\") — without this, an edit made through this endpoint sits as an uncommitted change in the job's worktree until something else happens to sweep it, risking silent loss the next time anything resets/checks out that worktree. Recommend this task also takes s.locks.Lock(root): the commit itself touches git, even though the brief's Notes paragraph only names create/done/delete/push explicitly in its git-mutating set — flagging this as this task's own reasoned addition (a real race if a done/delete runs against the same worktree mid-write), not something the brief pins either way. Validate the request body is non-empty and under a sane size cap.\n28:      files: src/internal/serve/api.go, src/internal/serve/server.go, src/internal/serve/api_test.go\n29:      depends: none\n30:      risk: medium — the commit-after-write design (and whether it needs the project lock) is a real decision with data-loss implications if skipped; otherwise low implementation complexity.\n31: \n32: TASK-5: Launch-agent-detached endpoint. POST /projects/{project}/jobs/{job}/agents/{agent} — launches one agent, one-shot, via TASK-2's primitive (never an attached/interactive session, per the brief's explicit out-of-scope note — no attach-and-chat over the API in any form). Validate {agent} against agentlist.Discover(root) first, for a fast 404 on an unknown agent name rather than letting an invalid agent fail deep inside a container spin-up. Accept an optional {\"profile\": \"...\"} body field, defaulting to config.ProfileClaudePro like every other launch path. Does NOT take s.locks: per the brief's Notes, launching an agent doesn't touch git state and shouldn't block behind an unrelated done/delete. Response: 202 Accepted — the run is now happening in the background; the caller watches it via TASK-12's session-log stream or by polling the existing GET .../jdi endpoint's sessionLog tail.\n33:      files: src/internal/serve/api.go, src/internal/serve/server.go, src/internal/serve/api_test.go\n34:      depends: TASK-2\n35:      risk: medium — the async response contract (202, then \"go watch the log\") is new to this API's surface (every existing v1 endpoint is synchronous); getting the client-facing contract right here sets the pattern TASK-6 also follows.\n36: \n37: TASK-6: Launch-jdi-detached endpoint. POST /projects/{project}/jobs/{job}/jdi — wraps launch.Jdi(jobID, root, profile), which is already exactly the shape this needs (a detached subprocess, no attached terminal, stdio discarded, reaped asynchronously — no changes needed to internal/launch itself). Before launching, check job.ReadJDIStatus(root, j.Name) for an already-running state and reject with 409 if found — a best-effort guard against two concurrent mg-jdi loops racing on the same job's git branch. Note explicitly that this is not airtight (the sidecar \"running\" status is written by the launched process itself, so two launches issued back-to-back before the first process has written its status could still both proceed) — an accepted, documented gap rather than something this task builds real locking for, which the brief does not ask for. No s.locks (same reasoning as TASK-5). Response: 202 Accepted.\n38:      files: src/internal/serve/api.go, src/internal/serve/server.go, src/internal/serve/api_test.go\n39:      depends: none\n40:      risk: low-medium — launch.Jdi is an existing, already-daemon-safe function; the main new risk is the double-launch race, explicitly documented as best-effort.\n41: \n42: TASK-7: Done endpoint + FinishJob conflict-handling change. POST /projects/{project}/jobs/{job}/done — wraps job.FinishJob under s.locks.Lock(root) (one of the brief's explicitly named git-mutating operations). The brief pins that a squash-merge conflict must take NEITHER of FinishJob's two existing interactive behaviors (offer @git-solver via mg host, or auto-rollback) — it must report a structured error and leave things as-is, \"requiring an explicit follow-up decision through some other call,\" and explicitly forbids silently falling back to either existing behavior. This needs a small, additive change to internal/job/finish.go: today handleSquashMergeFailure always prompts (via the confirm func) and, on decline, unconditionally rolls the main worktree back with git.ResetHard. Add a new option (e.g. a FinishOptions struct wrapping the existing positional args, or a package-level sentinel confirm value FinishJob recognizes) that, on a squash-merge conflict, skips the prompt-and-recover branch entirely and returns a new, distinguishable error (e.g. a wrapped ErrSquashMergeConflict) immediately — no rollback, no git-solver launch — leaving the main worktree in its conflicted-merge state and the job already archived-and-committed in its own worktree (that step runs before the merge attempt in FinishJob's existing sequence and cannot be undone without a much larger restructuring that is out of scope here; the response should say so explicitly rather than implying \"nothing happened\"). This must be purely additive: existing callers (the CLI's mg done, the TUI) keep their current confirm-based behavior unchanged — verify against finish_test.go's existing coverage untouched. Separately, and NOT pinned by the brief at all: decide what the done endpoint does about the earlier \"verdict not approved\" / \"no verdict.md\" warnings. Recommend requiring the caller to pass {\"force\": true} to proceed past those warnings (echoing the CLI's own warning text back in a 409 when force is ab... (line truncated to 2000 chars)\n43:      files: src/internal/job/finish.go, src/internal/job/finish_test.go, src/internal/serve/api.go, src/internal/serve/server.go, src/internal/serve/api_test.go\n44:      depends: none\n45:      risk: high — the most residual product ambiguity of any task here (the brief explicitly forbids guessing between the two existing CLI behaviors, and the force-vs-auto-approve call for the earlier warnings isn't pinned at all); it also modifies job.FinishJob, a shared lifecycle function the CLI and TUI both depend on today, so the change must be strictly additive.\n46: \n47: TASK-8: Delete endpoint. POST /projects/{project}/jobs/{job}/delete — wraps job.DeleteJob under s.locks.Lock(root) (git-mutating, named explicitly in the brief). Passes a pre-approved confirm function, mirroring internal/ui/app.go's yesConfirm — the HTTP call itself is the confirmation, the same precedent the TUI already established for its own confirm-then-call pattern. No conflict-handling ambiguity like done — DeleteJob has no merge step. Response: the DeleteResult (job name, branch, dir).\n48:      files: src/internal/serve/api.go, src/internal/serve/server.go, src/internal/serve/api_test.go\n49:      depends: none\n50:      risk: low — DeleteJob's confirm-bypass is an already-proven pattern (yesConfirm), just reused from a new caller.\n51: \n52: TASK-9: Push endpoint. POST /projects/{project}/jobs/{job}/push — resolves the job (the existing resolveJob helper) and runs git.PushWithContext(ctx, root, j.Branch) (git push -u origin <branch>, mirroring the TUI git panel's \"Push to origin\" action — src/internal/ui/app.go's pushCmd) under s.locks.Lock(root) (named explicitly in the brief's git-mutating set). Bound the call with a timeout mirroring the TUI's own hostGitTimeout (30s, src/internal/ui/app.go) rather than an unbounded context — long enough for a real push, short enough that a stalled/unreachable remote can't hang the request forever. A push failure (auth, network, non-fast-forward) surfaces as a structured error with git's own message, never silently swallowed.\n53:      files: src/internal/serve/api.go, src/internal/serve/server.go, src/internal/serve/api_test.go\n54:      depends: none\n55:      risk: low-medium — a real network call inside an HTTP handler; the main risk is an unbounded hang without a deliberate timeout, mitigated by reusing the TUI's own proven timeout value.\n56: \n57: TASK-10: Prune endpoint. POST /prune — wraps session.PruneOrphans, the same function cmd/mg/prune.go's runPrune calls. Decide during implementation whether this belongs under /projects/{project}/ at all: orphaned manigot-* containers are not partitioned by project (docker has no concept of \"which registered project\" a leftover container belongs to), so a single top-level, project-less route matching mg prune's own scope is likely the more honest shape than nesting it under one project's URL. No s.locks (no git or project-scoped state at all). Response mirrors mg prune's own reporting: removed count, running count.\n58:      files: src/internal/serve/api.go, src/internal/serve/server.go, src/internal/serve/api_test.go\n59:      depends: none\n60:      risk: low — thin wrapper around an existing, already-daemon-safe function with no TTY/interactive dependency.\n61: \n62: TASK-11: Orphaned-worktree list + cleanup endpoints. GET /projects/{project}/orphans (read-only, lists job.DiscoverOrphans(root) — name/dir/gitdir, mirroring the existing read-only endpoints' style) and POST /projects/{project}/orphans/{name}/delete (resolves one named orphan via job.MatchOrphan, then job.RemoveOrphansConfirmed(root, []Orphan{o}, out) for that single orphan) — one named resource per mutating call, consistent with every other endpoint in this API, rather than a single \"delete everything\" call that risks removing something the caller didn't expect to see gone. Take s.locks.Lock(root) for the delete: RemoveOrphans/RemoveOrphansConfirmed calls git.WorktreePrune, which touches git worktree metadata and can race with a concurrent create/done/delete on the same project. Flag explicitly: the brief's Notes paragraph does not name orphan cleanup in its git-mutating set at all, so locking it here is this task's own reasoned addition, offered for confirmation rather than assumed as already-decided.\n63:      files: src/internal/serve/api.go, src/internal/serve/server.go, src/internal/serve/api_test.go\n64:      depends: none\n65:      risk: medium — the \"list then delete-by-name\" API shape is a design call not explicitly pinned by the brief (which also floats \"a background sweep\" as an alternative); consistent with the rest of the surface, but worth confirming against what job three's UI actually wants before client-side assumptions build on it.\n66: \n67: TASK-12: session.log SSE streaming endpoint. GET /projects/{project}/jobs/{job}/session-log/stream — Server-Sent Events, chosen over WebSocket per the brief's own steer (\"pick whichever is less code against the existing net/http server\"): SSE is plain HTTP response streaming with no upgrade handshake or framing and needs no new dependency, unlike WebSocket, which Go's stdlib doesn't support natively. Implementation: open the job's session.log, seek to EOF (or accept a ?from=<byte offset> query param mirroring readFileTail's tail semantics for a client reconnecting after a drop), then poll for growth on a short interval (e.g. 500ms — the file is a local append-only file, so a simple stat+read-new-bytes loop is sufficient; no fsnotify/inotify needed) and flush each new chunk as an SSE `data:` frame (http.Flusher). Must stop cleanly on client disconnect (r.Context().Done()) AND participate in the daemon's graceful shutdown drain (serveShutdownDrain in cmd/mg/serve.go): plain http.Server.Shutdown does NOT itself interrupt a handler stuck in a polling loop — it only stops accepting new connections and waits for in-flight handlers to return — so the Server struct needs its own shutdown signal (e.g. a context stored on *Server, cancelled at the top of Shutdown before calling s.http.Shutdown) that this handler selects on alongside the request context and its poll ticker. Without this, every open stream would force the daemon to wait out the full drain timeout on every graceful shutdown — exactly what the brief calls out to confirm/avoid.\n68:      files: src/internal/serve/server.go (Server gets a shutdown context/field), src/internal/serve/stream.go (new), src/internal/serve/stream_test.go (new — httptest-driven: asserts growth is observed live, disconnect stops the handler goroutine, and a simulated shutdown returns promptly rather than hanging)\n69:      depends: none structurally, but a realistic end-to-end test benefits from TASK-1/TASK-2 already writing something to session.log during a launch\n70:      risk: high — the graceful-shutdown interaction is the one correctness requirement the brief explicitly calls out as needing verification, and a background-goroutine-per-stream shape is new to this codebase; getting it wrong leaks goroutines or hangs shutdown.\n71: \n72: TASK-13: Security + credentials test extension across the new mutating surface. Extend the existing security_test.go (zero-path-inputs) and credentials_test.go (no credential material in any response) suites — both established by job one — to cover every endpoint added in TASK-3 through TASK-12: hostile/encoded traversal segments against the new {project}/{job}/{agent}/{orphan-name} URL positions, and a pass confirming no mutating endpoint's response or error body ever echoes .env content, tokens, or account UUIDs (the same known-credential-value-in-env-then-grep-every-body technique job one's credentials_test.go already uses, re-run across the new surface).\n73:      files: src/internal/serve/security_test.go, src/internal/serve/credentials_test.go\n74:      depends: TASK-3, TASK-4, TASK-5, TASK-6, TASK-7, TASK-8, TASK-9, TASK-10, TASK-11, TASK-12\n75:      risk: low — test-only, but the surface to cover is large; the real risk is a rushed pass leaving a gap in the two invariants job one treated as non-negotiable.\n76: \n77: TASK-14: ProjectLocks boundary verification pass. A focused review + test pass confirming the locking boundary actually matches the design each task above states: create (TASK-3), edit-brief (TASK-4), done (TASK-7), delete (TASK-8), push (TASK-9), and orphan-delete (TASK-11) take s.locks.Lock(root); launch-agent (TASK-5), launch-jdi (TASK-6), prune (TASK-10), and every read endpoint do NOT. Add a concurrency test (extending locks_test.go, or a new integration test) proving two concurrent mutating calls against the SAME project serialize (e.g. a slow fake done blocks a concurrent delete) while a launch-agent call against the same project completes without waiting behind it, and that two different projects' mutating calls proceed independently — the exact property the brief's Notes worries about: \"a job-launch that has to wait behind an unrelated done defeats the point of a responsive control plane.\"\n78:      files: src/internal/serve/locks_test.go, or a new src/internal/serve/concurrency_test.go\n79:      depends: TASK-3, TASK-4, TASK-5, TASK-6, TASK-7, TASK-8, TASK-9, TASK-10, TASK-11\n80:      risk: low-medium — mostly verification against already-implemented handlers, but the locking boundary is a real correctness property (both over- and under-serializing are bugs), so it earns its own explicit test rather than trusting each task's author silently.\n81: \n82: TASK-15: Docs + help sync. docs/AGENTS.md (and its mirror project-template/docs/AGENTS.md — the hard rule that both describe the same system): extend the \"Listener / control plane\" subsection job one added with the new mutating endpoints, the async 202-then-poll/stream contract, the SSE streaming endpoint, and the ProjectLocks boundary actually shipped (which operations lock, which don't, and why). README.md: extend its existing \"Listener\" section (line ~1153) the same way. docs/listener.md: annotate its \"Sequencing after this job\" section marking \"Job two — mutating API + run supervision\" as done, mirroring how job one's own doc-sync task annotated docs/web-interface.md rather than rewriting it (see docs/jobs/archive/wood_listener-architecture/tasks.md TASK-11 for the precedent) — a short annotation, not a rewrite, and without contradicting the brief's own recorded decisions.\n83:      files: docs/AGENTS.md, project-template/docs/AGENTS.md, README.md, docs/listener.md\n84:      depends: TASK-1 through TASK-14 (accurate doc content needs the shipped behavior, especially TASK-7's and TASK-11's final API shapes)\n85:      risk: low — doc edits only; the discipline is keeping the three files in sync and not overstating what shipped.\n86: \n87: -->\n\n(End of file - total 87 lines)\n</content>","metadata":{"preview":"# Tasks: part 2 of web ui tui path\n\nid: farmer\nstatus: open\nanalyst: farmer\ndate: 2026-08-28\n\n<!-- Produced by @analyst from brief.md. -->\n\n## Task breakdown\n\n<!-- TASK-1: Relocate the session-log section-writer machinery out of cmd/mg. The sessionLog type, openSessionLog, and ensureSessionLogFile currently live privately in src/cmd/mg/jdioutput.go and are only ever called from cmd/mg/jdi.go's Run loop. A finding from reading the code: today NOTHING writes a job's session.log for a bare `mg --print --agent X --job Y` invocation outside the mg-jdi loop — contrary to docs/listener.md's claim that \"session.log already is the captured stream for both containerized and host-mode runs\" and this job's brief framing (\"riding the writer-side capture that already exists\"). Move sessionLog/openSessionLog/ensureSessionLogFile into a new exported file in internal/job (e.g. src/internal/job/sessionlog.go) so both cmd/mg and the new internal/serve package can write a live, tailable session.log for a one-shot invocation, not just an mg-jdi-driven one. This must be a pure relocation: the exact same on-disk format (blank-line section separator, \"=== <RFC3339> <agent> (attempt N) ===\" header, trailing-newline guarantee on Close) — cmd/mg/jdi.go is updated to call the relocated functions with no behavior change, and its existing coverage (jdioutput_test.go) moves/adapts to the new package rather than being rewritten, so mg-jdi's already-shipped log format (which the TUI's \"l\" tail and its jq pretty-print convention both depend on) cannot silently drift.\n     files: src/cmd/mg/jdioutput.go, src/cmd/mg/jdioutput_test.go, src/cmd/mg/jdi.go, src/internal/job/sessionlog.go (new), src/internal/job/sessionlog_test.go (new)\n     depends: none\n     risk: medium — touches mg-jdi's well-tested, carefully-documented session.log machinery; the relocation must be format-preserving, verified by moving rather than rewriting the existing tests.\n\nTASK-2: One-shot detached agent-run primitive. Add a \"run one agent invocation for a job, in-process, detached from the triggering HTTP request\" primitive that TASK-5's launch-agent endpoint calls. Mirror cmd/mg/jdi.go's commandAgentRunner.Run shape — session.ResolveProfile → session.ResolveRootFrom → info.CheckAuth → session.BuildDockerInvocation → inv.Run → session.SweepJobWorktree on success — but for a single --print invocation with no orchestrate.Next loop around it, opening one session.log section via TASK-1's relocated openSessionLog (attempt fixed at 1 — this is not part of an mg-jdi run, so there is no per-agent attempt counter to thread through) and closing it when the invocation returns. Run the whole thing in a background goroutine started by the HTTP handler so the request returns immediately instead of blocking for the invocation's full duration (which can be minutes); the goroutine's own errors have nowhere to report back to an already-returned HTTP response, so write them into the same session.log section rather than dropping them silently. Flag explicitly, as a documented known limitation rather than something this task solves: a concurrently-running mg-jdi loop against the SAME job would also be writing to the same session.log file at the same time (safe against file corruption — both are append-only, single-writer-per-section — but the underlying git worktree itself would have two independent processes touching it, which this job does not attempt to detect or prevent).\n     files: new file under src/internal/serve (or src/internal/session, whichever avoids an import cycle and best sets up cmd/mg/jdi.go to eventually reuse it too — confirm the exact home while implementing) plus its test file with a stubbed/fake docker invocation, mirroring cmd/mg/jdi_test.go's AgentRunner fake\n     depends: TASK-1\n     risk: medium — a new concurrency shape (a goroutine outliving its triggering HTTP request) around a docker/container invocation path that must not silently swallow a fast failure (auth error, docker not running) with no way for the caller to learn about it except by tailing session.log.","truncated":false,"loaded":[],"display":{"type":"file","path":"/workspace/docs/jobs/farmer_part-2-of-web-ui-tui-path/tasks.md","text":"# Tasks: part 2 of web ui tui path\n\nid: farmer\nstatus: open\nanalyst: farmer\ndate: 2026-08-28\n\n<!-- Produced by @analyst from brief.md. -->\n\n## Task breakdown\n\n<!-- TASK-1: Relocate the session-log section-writer machinery out of cmd/mg. The sessionLog type, openSessionLog, and ensureSessionLogFile currently live privately in src/cmd/mg/jdioutput.go and are only ever called from cmd/mg/jdi.go's Run loop. A finding from reading the code: today NOTHING writes a job's session.log for a bare `mg --print --agent X --job Y` invocation outside the mg-jdi loop — contrary to docs/listener.md's claim that \"session.log already is the captured stream for both containerized and host-mode runs\" and this job's brief framing (\"riding the writer-side capture that already exists\"). Move sessionLog/openSessionLog/ensureSessionLogFile into a new exported file in internal/job (e.g. src/internal/job/sessionlog.go) so both cmd/mg and the new internal/serve package can write a live, tailable session.log for a one-shot invocation, not just an mg-jdi-driven one. This must be a pure relocation: the exact same on-disk format (blank-line section separator, \"=== <RFC3339> <agent> (attempt N) ===\" header, trailing-newline guarantee on Close) — cmd/mg/jdi.go is updated to call the relocated functions with no behavior change, and its existing coverage (jdioutput_test.go) moves/adapts to the new package rather than being rewritten, so mg-jdi's already-shipped log format (which the TUI's \"l\" tail and its jq pretty-print convention both depend on) cannot silently drift.\n     files: src/cmd/mg/jdioutput.go, src/cmd/mg/jdioutput_test.go, src/cmd/mg/jdi.go, src/internal/job/sessionlog.go (new), src/internal/job/sessionlog_test.go (new)\n     depends: none\n     risk: medium — touches mg-jdi's well-tested, carefully-documented session.log machinery; the relocation must be format-preserving, verified by moving rather than rewriting the existing tests.\n\nTASK-2: One-shot detached agent-run primitive. Add a \"run one agent invocation for a job, in-process, detached from the triggering HTTP request\" primitive that TASK-5's launch-agent endpoint calls. Mirror cmd/mg/jdi.go's commandAgentRunner.Run shape — session.ResolveProfile → session.ResolveRootFrom → info.CheckAuth → session.BuildDockerInvocation → inv.Run → session.SweepJobWorktree on success — but for a single --print invocation with no orchestrate.Next loop around it, opening one session.log section via TASK-1's relocated openSessionLog (attempt fixed at 1 — this is not part of an mg-jdi run, so there is no per-agent attempt counter to thread through) and closing it when the invocation returns. Run the whole thing in a background goroutine started by the HTTP handler so the request returns immediately instead of blocking for the invocation's full duration (which can be minutes); the goroutine's own errors have nowhere to report back to an already-returned HTTP response, so write them into the same session.log section rather than dropping them silently. Flag explicitly, as a documented known limitation rather than something this task solves: a concurrently-running mg-jdi loop against the SAME job would also be writing to the same session.log file at the same time (safe against file corruption — both are append-only, single-writer-per-section — but the underlying git worktree itself would have two independent processes touching it, which this job does not attempt to detect or prevent).\n     files: new file under src/internal/serve (or src/internal/session, whichever avoids an import cycle and best sets up cmd/mg/jdi.go to eventually reuse it too — confirm the exact home while implementing) plus its test file with a stubbed/fake docker invocation, mirroring cmd/mg/jdi_test.go's AgentRunner fake\n     depends: TASK-1\n     risk: medium — a new concurrency shape (a goroutine outliving its triggering HTTP request) around a docker/container invocation path that must not silently swallow a fast failure (auth error, docker not running) with no way for the caller to learn about it except by tailing session.log.\n\nTASK-3: Create-job endpoint. POST /projects/{project}/jobs — wraps job.CreateJob(root, title, opts, out) under s.locks.Lock(root)/defer Unlock(root) (git-mutating: creates a branch + worktree, one of the operations the brief's Notes names explicitly). Request body: {\"title\": \"...\", \"type\": \"feature|fix|chore\" (optional, default feature), \"baseBranch\": \"...\" (optional override)} — validate type against CreateJob's own accepted set before calling so a bad value is a clean 400 rather than surfacing CreateJob's own \"Invalid type\" error text as a 500. Response: the created job's row (mirroring the existing jobRow shape) plus the branch/worktree path from CreateResult. CreateJob's out io.Writer (its human-readable summary) can be discarded or captured for debugging — decide while implementing; the response's structured fields are the source of truth either way.\n     files: src/internal/serve/api.go, src/internal/serve/server.go (route registration), src/internal/serve/api_test.go\n     depends: none\n     risk: low — job.CreateJob is an existing, well-tested pure function already used by the CLI and TUI; this is a thin adapter plus the lock.\n\nTASK-4: Edit-brief endpoint. PUT /projects/{project}/jobs/{job}/files/brief — raw-body replace of the job's brief.md, the one job file this job's brief explicitly makes writable (\"no $EDITOR, this is an HTTP body write\" — tasks/implementation/verdict stay read-only, written only by their respective agents). After writing the new content to j.Dir/brief.md, immediately commit it in the job's own worktree via session.SweepJobWorktree(root, diag) (the same \"commit everything uncommitted\" primitive every session-ending sweep already uses, message \"[<id>] chore: commit all\") — without this, an edit made through this endpoint sits as an uncommitted change in the job's worktree until something else happens to sweep it, risking silent loss the next time anything resets/checks out that worktree. Recommend this task also takes s.locks.Lock(root): the commit itself touches git, even though the brief's Notes paragraph only names create/done/delete/push explicitly in its git-mutating set — flagging this as this task's own reasoned addition (a real race if a done/delete runs against the same worktree mid-write), not something the brief pins either way. Validate the request body is non-empty and under a sane size cap.\n     files: src/internal/serve/api.go, src/internal/serve/server.go, src/internal/serve/api_test.go\n     depends: none\n     risk: medium — the commit-after-write design (and whether it needs the project lock) is a real decision with data-loss implications if skipped; otherwise low implementation complexity.\n\nTASK-5: Launch-agent-detached endpoint. POST /projects/{project}/jobs/{job}/agents/{agent} — launches one agent, one-shot, via TASK-2's primitive (never an attached/interactive session, per the brief's explicit out-of-scope note — no attach-and-chat over the API in any form). Validate {agent} against agentlist.Discover(root) first, for a fast 404 on an unknown agent name rather than letting an invalid agent fail deep inside a container spin-up. Accept an optional {\"profile\": \"...\"} body field, defaulting to config.ProfileClaudePro like every other launch path. Does NOT take s.locks: per the brief's Notes, launching an agent doesn't touch git state and shouldn't block behind an unrelated done/delete. Response: 202 Accepted — the run is now happening in the background; the caller watches it via TASK-12's session-log stream or by polling the existing GET .../jdi endpoint's sessionLog tail.\n     files: src/internal/serve/api.go, src/internal/serve/server.go, src/internal/serve/api_test.go\n     depends: TASK-2\n     risk: medium — the async response contract (202, then \"go watch the log\") is new to this API's surface (every existing v1 endpoint is synchronous); getting the client-facing contract right here sets the pattern TASK-6 also follows.\n\nTASK-6: Launch-jdi-detached endpoint. POST /projects/{project}/jobs/{job}/jdi — wraps launch.Jdi(jobID, root, profile), which is already exactly the shape this needs (a detached subprocess, no attached terminal, stdio discarded, reaped asynchronously — no changes needed to internal/launch itself). Before launching, check job.ReadJDIStatus(root, j.Name) for an already-running state and reject with 409 if found — a best-effort guard against two concurrent mg-jdi loops racing on the same job's git branch. Note explicitly that this is not airtight (the sidecar \"running\" status is written by the launched process itself, so two launches issued back-to-back before the first process has written its status could still both proceed) — an accepted, documented gap rather than something this task builds real locking for, which the brief does not ask for. No s.locks (same reasoning as TASK-5). Response: 202 Accepted.\n     files: src/internal/serve/api.go, src/internal/serve/server.go, src/internal/serve/api_test.go\n     depends: none\n     risk: low-medium — launch.Jdi is an existing, already-daemon-safe function; the main new risk is the double-launch race, explicitly documented as best-effort.\n\nTASK-7: Done endpoint + FinishJob conflict-handling change. POST /projects/{project}/jobs/{job}/done — wraps job.FinishJob under s.locks.Lock(root) (one of the brief's explicitly named git-mutating operations). The brief pins that a squash-merge conflict must take NEITHER of FinishJob's two existing interactive behaviors (offer @git-solver via mg host, or auto-rollback) — it must report a structured error and leave things as-is, \"requiring an explicit follow-up decision through some other call,\" and explicitly forbids silently falling back to either existing behavior. This needs a small, additive change to internal/job/finish.go: today handleSquashMergeFailure always prompts (via the confirm func) and, on decline, unconditionally rolls the main worktree back with git.ResetHard. Add a new option (e.g. a FinishOptions struct wrapping the existing positional args, or a package-level sentinel confirm value FinishJob recognizes) that, on a squash-merge conflict, skips the prompt-and-recover branch entirely and returns a new, distinguishable error (e.g. a wrapped ErrSquashMergeConflict) immediately — no rollback, no git-solver launch — leaving the main worktree in its conflicted-merge state and the job already archived-and-committed in its own worktree (that step runs before the merge attempt in FinishJob's existing sequence and cannot be undone without a much larger restructuring that is out of scope here; the response should say so explicitly rather than implying \"nothing happened\"). This must be purely additive: existing callers (the CLI's mg done, the TUI) keep their current confirm-based behavior unchanged — verify against finish_test.go's existing coverage untouched. Separately, and NOT pinned by the brief at all: decide what the done endpoint does about the earlier \"verdict not approved\" / \"no verdict.md\" warnings. Recommend requiring the caller to pass {\"force\": true} to proceed past those warnings (echoing the CLI's own warning text back in a 409 when force is ab... (line truncated to 2000 chars)\n     files: src/internal/job/finish.go, src/internal/job/finish_test.go, src/internal/serve/api.go, src/internal/serve/server.go, src/internal/serve/api_test.go\n     depends: none\n     risk: high — the most residual product ambiguity of any task here (the brief explicitly forbids guessing between the two existing CLI behaviors, and the force-vs-auto-approve call for the earlier warnings isn't pinned at all); it also modifies job.FinishJob, a shared lifecycle function the CLI and TUI both depend on today, so the change must be strictly additive.\n\nTASK-8: Delete endpoint. POST /projects/{project}/jobs/{job}/delete — wraps job.DeleteJob under s.locks.Lock(root) (git-mutating, named explicitly in the brief). Passes a pre-approved confirm function, mirroring internal/ui/app.go's yesConfirm — the HTTP call itself is the confirmation, the same precedent the TUI already established for its own confirm-then-call pattern. No conflict-handling ambiguity like done — DeleteJob has no merge step. Response: the DeleteResult (job name, branch, dir).\n     files: src/internal/serve/api.go, src/internal/serve/server.go, src/internal/serve/api_test.go\n     depends: none\n     risk: low — DeleteJob's confirm-bypass is an already-proven pattern (yesConfirm), just reused from a new caller.\n\nTASK-9: Push endpoint. POST /projects/{project}/jobs/{job}/push — resolves the job (the existing resolveJob helper) and runs git.PushWithContext(ctx, root, j.Branch) (git push -u origin <branch>, mirroring the TUI git panel's \"Push to origin\" action — src/internal/ui/app.go's pushCmd) under s.locks.Lock(root) (named explicitly in the brief's git-mutating set). Bound the call with a timeout mirroring the TUI's own hostGitTimeout (30s, src/internal/ui/app.go) rather than an unbounded context — long enough for a real push, short enough that a stalled/unreachable remote can't hang the request forever. A push failure (auth, network, non-fast-forward) surfaces as a structured error with git's own message, never silently swallowed.\n     files: src/internal/serve/api.go, src/internal/serve/server.go, src/internal/serve/api_test.go\n     depends: none\n     risk: low-medium — a real network call inside an HTTP handler; the main risk is an unbounded hang without a deliberate timeout, mitigated by reusing the TUI's own proven timeout value.\n\nTASK-10: Prune endpoint. POST /prune — wraps session.PruneOrphans, the same function cmd/mg/prune.go's runPrune calls. Decide during implementation whether this belongs under /projects/{project}/ at all: orphaned manigot-* containers are not partitioned by project (docker has no concept of \"which registered project\" a leftover container belongs to), so a single top-level, project-less route matching mg prune's own scope is likely the more honest shape than nesting it under one project's URL. No s.locks (no git or project-scoped state at all). Response mirrors mg prune's own reporting: removed count, running count.\n     files: src/internal/serve/api.go, src/internal/serve/server.go, src/internal/serve/api_test.go\n     depends: none\n     risk: low — thin wrapper around an existing, already-daemon-safe function with no TTY/interactive dependency.\n\nTASK-11: Orphaned-worktree list + cleanup endpoints. GET /projects/{project}/orphans (read-only, lists job.DiscoverOrphans(root) — name/dir/gitdir, mirroring the existing read-only endpoints' style) and POST /projects/{project}/orphans/{name}/delete (resolves one named orphan via job.MatchOrphan, then job.RemoveOrphansConfirmed(root, []Orphan{o}, out) for that single orphan) — one named resource per mutating call, consistent with every other endpoint in this API, rather than a single \"delete everything\" call that risks removing something the caller didn't expect to see gone. Take s.locks.Lock(root) for the delete: RemoveOrphans/RemoveOrphansConfirmed calls git.WorktreePrune, which touches git worktree metadata and can race with a concurrent create/done/delete on the same project. Flag explicitly: the brief's Notes paragraph does not name orphan cleanup in its git-mutating set at all, so locking it here is this task's own reasoned addition, offered for confirmation rather than assumed as already-decided.\n     files: src/internal/serve/api.go, src/internal/serve/server.go, src/internal/serve/api_test.go\n     depends: none\n     risk: medium — the \"list then delete-by-name\" API shape is a design call not explicitly pinned by the brief (which also floats \"a background sweep\" as an alternative); consistent with the rest of the surface, but worth confirming against what job three's UI actually wants before client-side assumptions build on it.\n\nTASK-12: session.log SSE streaming endpoint. GET /projects/{project}/jobs/{job}/session-log/stream — Server-Sent Events, chosen over WebSocket per the brief's own steer (\"pick whichever is less code against the existing net/http server\"): SSE is plain HTTP response streaming with no upgrade handshake or framing and needs no new dependency, unlike WebSocket, which Go's stdlib doesn't support natively. Implementation: open the job's session.log, seek to EOF (or accept a ?from=<byte offset> query param mirroring readFileTail's tail semantics for a client reconnecting after a drop), then poll for growth on a short interval (e.g. 500ms — the file is a local append-only file, so a simple stat+read-new-bytes loop is sufficient; no fsnotify/inotify needed) and flush each new chunk as an SSE `data:` frame (http.Flusher). Must stop cleanly on client disconnect (r.Context().Done()) AND participate in the daemon's graceful shutdown drain (serveShutdownDrain in cmd/mg/serve.go): plain http.Server.Shutdown does NOT itself interrupt a handler stuck in a polling loop — it only stops accepting new connections and waits for in-flight handlers to return — so the Server struct needs its own shutdown signal (e.g. a context stored on *Server, cancelled at the top of Shutdown before calling s.http.Shutdown) that this handler selects on alongside the request context and its poll ticker. Without this, every open stream would force the daemon to wait out the full drain timeout on every graceful shutdown — exactly what the brief calls out to confirm/avoid.\n     files: src/internal/serve/server.go (Server gets a shutdown context/field), src/internal/serve/stream.go (new), src/internal/serve/stream_test.go (new — httptest-driven: asserts growth is observed live, disconnect stops the handler goroutine, and a simulated shutdown returns promptly rather than hanging)\n     depends: none structurally, but a realistic end-to-end test benefits from TASK-1/TASK-2 already writing something to session.log during a launch\n     risk: high — the graceful-shutdown interaction is the one correctness requirement the brief explicitly calls out as needing verification, and a background-goroutine-per-stream shape is new to this codebase; getting it wrong leaks goroutines or hangs shutdown.\n\nTASK-13: Security + credentials test extension across the new mutating surface. Extend the existing security_test.go (zero-path-inputs) and credentials_test.go (no credential material in any response) suites — both established by job one — to cover every endpoint added in TASK-3 through TASK-12: hostile/encoded traversal segments against the new {project}/{job}/{agent}/{orphan-name} URL positions, and a pass confirming no mutating endpoint's response or error body ever echoes .env content, tokens, or account UUIDs (the same known-credential-value-in-env-then-grep-every-body technique job one's credentials_test.go already uses, re-run across the new surface).\n     files: src/internal/serve/security_test.go, src/internal/serve/credentials_test.go\n     depends: TASK-3, TASK-4, TASK-5, TASK-6, TASK-7, TASK-8, TASK-9, TASK-10, TASK-11, TASK-12\n     risk: low — test-only, but the surface to cover is large; the real risk is a rushed pass leaving a gap in the two invariants job one treated as non-negotiable.\n\nTASK-14: ProjectLocks boundary verification pass. A focused review + test pass confirming the locking boundary actually matches the design each task above states: create (TASK-3), edit-brief (TASK-4), done (TASK-7), delete (TASK-8), push (TASK-9), and orphan-delete (TASK-11) take s.locks.Lock(root); launch-agent (TASK-5), launch-jdi (TASK-6), prune (TASK-10), and every read endpoint do NOT. Add a concurrency test (extending locks_test.go, or a new integration test) proving two concurrent mutating calls against the SAME project serialize (e.g. a slow fake done blocks a concurrent delete) while a launch-agent call against the same project completes without waiting behind it, and that two different projects' mutating calls proceed independently — the exact property the brief's Notes worries about: \"a job-launch that has to wait behind an unrelated done defeats the point of a responsive control plane.\"\n     files: src/internal/serve/locks_test.go, or a new src/internal/serve/concurrency_test.go\n     depends: TASK-3, TASK-4, TASK-5, TASK-6, TASK-7, TASK-8, TASK-9, TASK-10, TASK-11\n     risk: low-medium — mostly verification against already-implemented handlers, but the locking boundary is a real correctness property (both over- and under-serializing are bugs), so it earns its own explicit test rather than trusting each task's author silently.\n\nTASK-15: Docs + help sync. docs/AGENTS.md (and its mirror project-template/docs/AGENTS.md — the hard rule that both describe the same system): extend the \"Listener / control plane\" subsection job one added with the new mutating endpoints, the async 202-then-poll/stream contract, the SSE streaming endpoint, and the ProjectLocks boundary actually shipped (which operations lock, which don't, and why). README.md: extend its existing \"Listener\" section (line ~1153) the same way. docs/listener.md: annotate its \"Sequencing after this job\" section marking \"Job two — mutating API + run supervision\" as done, mirroring how job one's own doc-sync task annotated docs/web-interface.md rather than rewriting it (see docs/jobs/archive/wood_listener-architecture/tasks.md TASK-11 for the precedent) — a short annotation, not a rewrite, and without contradicting the brief's own recorded decisions.\n     files: docs/AGENTS.md, project-template/docs/AGENTS.md, README.md, docs/listener.md\n     depends: TASK-1 through TASK-14 (accurate doc content needs the shipped behavior, especially TASK-7's and TASK-11's final API shapes)\n     risk: low — doc edits only; the discipline is keeping the three files in sync and not overstating what shipped.\n\n-->","lineStart":1,"lineEnd":87,"totalLines":87,"truncated":false}},"title":"docs/jobs/farmer_part-2-of-web-ui-tui-path/tasks.md","time":{"start":1787952466834,"end":1787952466837}},"id":"prt_04a458b3d001iaCTU5G4gTjZLJ","sessionID":"ses_fb5ba93f2ffe9RBBAgSFG35dIN","messageID":"msg_04a4586e6001uYK4U9UfM0DkJ3"}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1787952466970,"sessionID":"ses_fb5ba93f2ffe9RBBAgSFG35dIN","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"read","callID":"call_ffe38ce8272544119889bd3d","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"/workspace/docs/jobs/farmer_part-2-of-web-ui-tui-path/implementation.md"},"output":"<path>/workspace/docs/jobs/farmer_part-2-of-web-ui-tui-path/implementation.md</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n1: # Implementation: part 2 of web ui tui path\n2: \n3: id: farmer\n4: status: open\n5: developer: farmer\n6: date: 2026-08-28\n7: \n8: <!-- Produced by @developer after implementation. -->\n9: \n10: ## Summary\n11: \n12: Job two of the control-plane sequence: the `mg serve` daemon grew from\n13: read-only (job one) into an actual control plane. It can now drive a job's\n14: full lifecycle over HTTP — create, edit brief, launch agents and `mg jdi`\n15: detached, done, delete, push, prune, and orphaned-worktree cleanup — and\n16: stream a live agent run's `session.log` over Server-Sent Events, the network\n17: twin of the TUI's `l` tail.\n18: \n19: ## Changes\n20: \n21: TASK-1: Relocated the session.log section-writer machinery out of `cmd/mg`\n22:   into `internal/job/sessionlog.go` (exported `SessionLog`/`OpenSessionLog`/\n23:   `EnsureSessionLogFile`), so both the mg-jdi loop and the new one-shot\n24:   agent-run primitive can write a live, tailable session.log. Pure relocation:\n25:   on-disk format unchanged, `cmd/mg/jdi.go` updated to call the relocated\n26:   functions, and `jdioutput_test.go`'s coverage moved (not rewritten) to\n27:   `internal/job/sessionlog_test.go`. Previously nothing wrote a job's\n28:   session.log for a bare `mg --print` invocation outside the mg-jdi loop.\n29: \n30: TASK-2: Added the one-shot detached agent-run primitive in\n31:   `internal/session/oneshot.go` (`AgentRunner`, `CommandAgentRunner`,\n32:   `RunOneShot`): a single `--print` invocation via the launcher's own\n33:   ResolveProfile → ResolveRootFrom → CheckAuth → BuildDockerInvocation → Run\n34:   path, ending with the same post-run `SweepJobWorktree` mg-jdi performs,\n35:   opening one session.log section at attempt 1 and writing any runner error\n36:   into the same section (the only place left to report once the triggering\n37:   HTTP request has returned). Lives in internal/session so `internal/serve`\n38:   can reuse it without importing cmd/mg. Known documented limitation: a\n39:   concurrently-running mg-jdi loop against the same job would write to the\n40:   same session.log — safe against corruption, but two processes touching the\n41:   same git worktree is not detected or prevented.\n42: \n43: TASK-3: `POST /projects/{project}/jobs` create-job endpoint\n44:   (`handleCreateJob` in `internal/serve/mutate.go`) — wraps `job.CreateJob`\n45:   under `s.locks.Lock(root)`, validates `type` against CreateJob's accepted\n46:   set up front (clean 400 instead of a 500), returns 201 with the created\n47:   job's row plus branch/worktree path.\n48: \n49: TASK-4: `PUT /projects/{project}/jobs/{job}/files/brief` edit-brief endpoint\n50:   (`handleEditBrief`) — raw-body replace of brief.md (no `$EDITOR`), then\n51:   immediately commits it in the job's own worktree via\n52:   `session.SweepJobWorktree` so the edit never sits uncommitted. Takes the\n53:   project lock (this task's reasoned addition to the brief's git-mutating\n54:   set: the commit touches git). Empty/oversized bodies are clean 400s; a\n55:   non-git project or a branch with no registered worktree reports a warning\n56:   (the write still succeeds).\n57: \n58: TASK-5: `POST /projects/{project}/jobs/{job}/agents/{agent}` launch-agent\n59:   endpoint (`handleLaunchAgent`) — validates the agent against\n60:   `agentlist.Discover` first (fast 404 on unknown names), accepts an optional\n61:   `{\"profile\": \"...\"}` (default `claude-pro`), then starts TASK-2's\n62:   `RunOneShot` in a background goroutine and returns 202. No lock taken. The\n63:   caller watches the run via the session-log stream or by polling\n64:   `GET .../jdi`'s sessionLog tail.\n65: \n66: TASK-6: `POST /projects/{project}/jobs/{job}/jdi` launch-jdi endpoint\n67:   (`handleLaunchJDI`) — wraps `launch.Jdi` (already a detached subprocess),\n68:   refusing 409 when the job's status sidecar says running (best-effort\n69:   double-launch guard, documented as not airtight). No lock. Returns 202.\n70: \n71: TASK-7: `POST /projects/{project}/jobs/{job}/done` done endpoint\n72:   (`handleDoneJob`) plus the additive `FinishJobWithOptions` change in\n73:   `internal/job/finish.go`. A squash-merge conflict under the endpoint is a\n74:   structured 409 (`job.ErrSquashMergeConflict` via\n75:   `FinishOptions.NoConflictRecovery`) — neither of the two interactive CLI\n76:   behaviors (git-solver handoff, auto-rollback) is taken; the job is left\n77:   archived-and-committed in its own worktree with the main worktree in its\n78:   conflicted state, for an explicit follow-up call. Existing callers (CLI mg\n79:   done, TUI) keep their confirm-based behavior unchanged. The earlier\n80:   \"verdict not approved\"/\"no verdict.md\" warnings require `{\"force\": true}`\n81:   (409 echoing the CLI's warning text otherwise).\n82: \n83: TASK-8: `POST /projects/{project}/jobs/{job}/delete` delete endpoint\n84:   (`handleDeleteJob`) — wraps `job.DeleteJob` under the lock with a\n85:   pre-approved confirm (the HTTP call itself is the confirmation, the same\n86:   precedent the TUI's yesConfirm established). Returns the DeleteResult.\n87: \n88: TASK-9: `POST /projects/{project}/jobs/{job}/push` push endpoint\n89:   (`handlePushJob`) — `git.PushWithContext` under the lock, bounded by the\n90:   TUI's own 30s timeout; failures surface as structured errors with git's own\n91:   message.\n92: \n93: TASK-10: `POST /prune` prune endpoint (`handlePrune`) — wraps\n94:   `session.PruneOrphans`, deliberately top-level (containers are not\n95:   partitioned by project). Reports removed + running counts.\n96: \n97: TASK-11: Orphaned-worktree endpoints — `GET /projects/{project}/orphans`\n98:   (read-only list via `job.DiscoverOrphans`) and\n99:   `POST /projects/{project}/orphans/{name}/delete` (one named orphan via\n100:   `job.MatchOrphan` + `job.RemoveOrphansConfirmed`, under the lock — this\n101:   task's reasoned addition, since RemoveOrphansConfirmed calls\n102:   `git.WorktreePrune`).\n103: \n104: TASK-12: `GET /projects/{project}/jobs/{job}/session-log/stream` SSE endpoint\n105:   (`internal/serve/stream.go`) — Server-Sent Events over the plain net/http\n106:   server (no WebSocket, no new dependency), tailing the same session.log the\n107:   TUI's `l` key tails. Starts at EOF (tail -f), byte 0 when the file doesn't\n108:   exist yet, or `?from=<byte offset>` for reconnects; a truncated file resets\n109:   to byte 0 with a fresh `start` event. The `Server` struct gained a\n110:   shutdown context cancelled at the top of `Shutdown`, so an open stream\n111:   unblocks and the drain returns promptly instead of waiting out its full\n112:   timeout. Two fixes were needed to the swept-in implementation: the handler\n113:   was missing its `bytes`/`io` imports and an undefined `readAll`, and the\n114:   `start` event payload was written as a bare number despite the documented\n115:   `{\"offset\":N}` protocol. Also fixed the audit middleware's `statusRecorder`,\n116:   which wrapped the ResponseWriter in a way that made the stream handler's\n117:   `w.(http.Flusher)` assertion fail — every stream would have returned\n118:   \"streaming unsupported\" in the real daemon; the recorder now delegates\n119:   Flush.\n120: \n121: TASK-13: Extended `security_test.go` (hostile/encoded traversal segments at\n122:   every new URL position: {project} on create, {job} on all mutating routes,\n123:   {agent}, and orphan {name}) and `credentials_test.go` (every mutating\n124:   endpoint's success and error bodies grepped for the known credential values\n125:   planted in `.env` + env — the same technique job one used, re-run across\n126:   the new surface).\n127: \n128: TASK-14: `internal/serve/concurrency_test.go` — pins the ProjectLocks\n129:   boundary across the handler surface: create, edit-brief, done, delete,\n130:   push and orphan-delete block behind a held project lock (they serialize);\n131:   launch-agent, launch-jdi, prune and reads complete immediately (they\n132:   don't); two different projects' mutating calls proceed independently.\n133: \n134: TASK-15: Docs sync — `docs/AGENTS.md` and its mirror\n135:   `project-template/docs/AGENTS.md` now document the mutating API, the async\n136:   202-then-poll/stream contract, the SSE endpoint, and the shipped\n137:   ProjectLocks boundary; `README.md`'s Listener section and command table\n138:   row updated the same way; `docs/listener.md`'s \"Sequencing after this job\"\n139:   marks job two as DONE with a short annotation (not a rewrite), including\n140:   the recorded decision that \"answering\" a `NEEDS-HUMAN-INPUT:` marker is the\n141:   composition of edit-brief + relaunch, not its own endpoint. The `mg serve`\n142:   help text no longer claims the API is read-only.\n143: \n144: ## Known issues / follow-ups\n145: \n146: - **Test-environment note:** `internal/ui` and `cmd/mg` tests that shell out\n147:   to `git init` fail when the manigot session git shim is on PATH (it refuses\n148:   `git init`); they pass with a real-git-only PATH. This is a pre-existing\n149:   session-environment artifact, not a regression from this job.\n150: - The launch-jdi double-launch guard (409 on a running status sidecar) is\n151:   best-effort by design: two launches issued back-to-back before the first\n152:   process writes its status could both proceed. Documented, not solved —\n153:   the brief does not ask for real locking here.\n154: - A concurrently-running mg-jdi loop against the same job as a detached\n155:   agent launch writes to the same session.log (safe: append-only,\n156:   single-writer-per-section) but means two processes touch the same git\n157:   worktree — not detected or prevented (documented limitation of TASK-2).\n158: - The done endpoint's conflict path leaves the main worktree in its\n159:   conflicted-merge state by design; resolving it requires an explicit\n160:   follow-up (e.g. launching @git-solver via the agent endpoint, or `mg host`\n161:   from a terminal). The response says so explicitly rather than implying\n162:   nothing happened.\n\n(End of file - total 162 lines)\n</content>","metadata":{"preview":"# Implementation: part 2 of web ui tui path\n\nid: farmer\nstatus: open\ndeveloper: farmer\ndate: 2026-08-28\n\n<!-- Produced by @developer after implementation. -->\n\n## Summary\n\nJob two of the control-plane sequence: the `mg serve` daemon grew from\nread-only (job one) into an actual control plane. It can now drive a job's\nfull lifecycle over HTTP — create, edit brief, launch agents and `mg jdi`\ndetached, done, delete, push, prune, and orphaned-worktree cleanup — and\nstream a live agent run's `session.log` over Server-Sent Events, the network\ntwin of the TUI's `l` tail.\n\n## Changes\n","truncated":false,"loaded":[],"display":{"type":"file","path":"/workspace/docs/jobs/farmer_part-2-of-web-ui-tui-path/implementation.md","text":"# Implementation: part 2 of web ui tui path\n\nid: farmer\nstatus: open\ndeveloper: farmer\ndate: 2026-08-28\n\n<!-- Produced by @developer after implementation. -->\n\n## Summary\n\nJob two of the control-plane sequence: the `mg serve` daemon grew from\nread-only (job one) into an actual control plane. It can now drive a job's\nfull lifecycle over HTTP — create, edit brief, launch agents and `mg jdi`\ndetached, done, delete, push, prune, and orphaned-worktree cleanup — and\nstream a live agent run's `session.log` over Server-Sent Events, the network\ntwin of the TUI's `l` tail.\n\n## Changes\n\nTASK-1: Relocated the session.log section-writer machinery out of `cmd/mg`\n  into `internal/job/sessionlog.go` (exported `SessionLog`/`OpenSessionLog`/\n  `EnsureSessionLogFile`), so both the mg-jdi loop and the new one-shot\n  agent-run primitive can write a live, tailable session.log. Pure relocation:\n  on-disk format unchanged, `cmd/mg/jdi.go` updated to call the relocated\n  functions, and `jdioutput_test.go`'s coverage moved (not rewritten) to\n  `internal/job/sessionlog_test.go`. Previously nothing wrote a job's\n  session.log for a bare `mg --print` invocation outside the mg-jdi loop.\n\nTASK-2: Added the one-shot detached agent-run primitive in\n  `internal/session/oneshot.go` (`AgentRunner`, `CommandAgentRunner`,\n  `RunOneShot`): a single `--print` invocation via the launcher's own\n  ResolveProfile → ResolveRootFrom → CheckAuth → BuildDockerInvocation → Run\n  path, ending with the same post-run `SweepJobWorktree` mg-jdi performs,\n  opening one session.log section at attempt 1 and writing any runner error\n  into the same section (the only place left to report once the triggering\n  HTTP request has returned). Lives in internal/session so `internal/serve`\n  can reuse it without importing cmd/mg. Known documented limitation: a\n  concurrently-running mg-jdi loop against the same job would write to the\n  same session.log — safe against corruption, but two processes touching the\n  same git worktree is not detected or prevented.\n\nTASK-3: `POST /projects/{project}/jobs` create-job endpoint\n  (`handleCreateJob` in `internal/serve/mutate.go`) — wraps `job.CreateJob`\n  under `s.locks.Lock(root)`, validates `type` against CreateJob's accepted\n  set up front (clean 400 instead of a 500), returns 201 with the created\n  job's row plus branch/worktree path.\n\nTASK-4: `PUT /projects/{project}/jobs/{job}/files/brief` edit-brief endpoint\n  (`handleEditBrief`) — raw-body replace of brief.md (no `$EDITOR`), then\n  immediately commits it in the job's own worktree via\n  `session.SweepJobWorktree` so the edit never sits uncommitted. Takes the\n  project lock (this task's reasoned addition to the brief's git-mutating\n  set: the commit touches git). Empty/oversized bodies are clean 400s; a\n  non-git project or a branch with no registered worktree reports a warning\n  (the write still succeeds).\n\nTASK-5: `POST /projects/{project}/jobs/{job}/agents/{agent}` launch-agent\n  endpoint (`handleLaunchAgent`) — validates the agent against\n  `agentlist.Discover` first (fast 404 on unknown names), accepts an optional\n  `{\"profile\": \"...\"}` (default `claude-pro`), then starts TASK-2's\n  `RunOneShot` in a background goroutine and returns 202. No lock taken. The\n  caller watches the run via the session-log stream or by polling\n  `GET .../jdi`'s sessionLog tail.\n\nTASK-6: `POST /projects/{project}/jobs/{job}/jdi` launch-jdi endpoint\n  (`handleLaunchJDI`) — wraps `launch.Jdi` (already a detached subprocess),\n  refusing 409 when the job's status sidecar says running (best-effort\n  double-launch guard, documented as not airtight). No lock. Returns 202.\n\nTASK-7: `POST /projects/{project}/jobs/{job}/done` done endpoint\n  (`handleDoneJob`) plus the additive `FinishJobWithOptions` change in\n  `internal/job/finish.go`. A squash-merge conflict under the endpoint is a\n  structured 409 (`job.ErrSquashMergeConflict` via\n  `FinishOptions.NoConflictRecovery`) — neither of the two interactive CLI\n  behaviors (git-solver handoff, auto-rollback) is taken; the job is left\n  archived-and-committed in its own worktree with the main worktree in its\n  conflicted state, for an explicit follow-up call. Existing callers (CLI mg\n  done, TUI) keep their confirm-based behavior unchanged. The earlier\n  \"verdict not approved\"/\"no verdict.md\" warnings require `{\"force\": true}`\n  (409 echoing the CLI's warning text otherwise).\n\nTASK-8: `POST /projects/{project}/jobs/{job}/delete` delete endpoint\n  (`handleDeleteJob`) — wraps `job.DeleteJob` under the lock with a\n  pre-approved confirm (the HTTP call itself is the confirmation, the same\n  precedent the TUI's yesConfirm established). Returns the DeleteResult.\n\nTASK-9: `POST /projects/{project}/jobs/{job}/push` push endpoint\n  (`handlePushJob`) — `git.PushWithContext` under the lock, bounded by the\n  TUI's own 30s timeout; failures surface as structured errors with git's own\n  message.\n\nTASK-10: `POST /prune` prune endpoint (`handlePrune`) — wraps\n  `session.PruneOrphans`, deliberately top-level (containers are not\n  partitioned by project). Reports removed + running counts.\n\nTASK-11: Orphaned-worktree endpoints — `GET /projects/{project}/orphans`\n  (read-only list via `job.DiscoverOrphans`) and\n  `POST /projects/{project}/orphans/{name}/delete` (one named orphan via\n  `job.MatchOrphan` + `job.RemoveOrphansConfirmed`, under the lock — this\n  task's reasoned addition, since RemoveOrphansConfirmed calls\n  `git.WorktreePrune`).\n\nTASK-12: `GET /projects/{project}/jobs/{job}/session-log/stream` SSE endpoint\n  (`internal/serve/stream.go`) — Server-Sent Events over the plain net/http\n  server (no WebSocket, no new dependency), tailing the same session.log the\n  TUI's `l` key tails. Starts at EOF (tail -f), byte 0 when the file doesn't\n  exist yet, or `?from=<byte offset>` for reconnects; a truncated file resets\n  to byte 0 with a fresh `start` event. The `Server` struct gained a\n  shutdown context cancelled at the top of `Shutdown`, so an open stream\n  unblocks and the drain returns promptly instead of waiting out its full\n  timeout. Two fixes were needed to the swept-in implementation: the handler\n  was missing its `bytes`/`io` imports and an undefined `readAll`, and the\n  `start` event payload was written as a bare number despite the documented\n  `{\"offset\":N}` protocol. Also fixed the audit middleware's `statusRecorder`,\n  which wrapped the ResponseWriter in a way that made the stream handler's\n  `w.(http.Flusher)` assertion fail — every stream would have returned\n  \"streaming unsupported\" in the real daemon; the recorder now delegates\n  Flush.\n\nTASK-13: Extended `security_test.go` (hostile/encoded traversal segments at\n  every new URL position: {project} on create, {job} on all mutating routes,\n  {agent}, and orphan {name}) and `credentials_test.go` (every mutating\n  endpoint's success and error bodies grepped for the known credential values\n  planted in `.env` + env — the same technique job one used, re-run across\n  the new surface).\n\nTASK-14: `internal/serve/concurrency_test.go` — pins the ProjectLocks\n  boundary across the handler surface: create, edit-brief, done, delete,\n  push and orphan-delete block behind a held project lock (they serialize);\n  launch-agent, launch-jdi, prune and reads complete immediately (they\n  don't); two different projects' mutating calls proceed independently.\n\nTASK-15: Docs sync — `docs/AGENTS.md` and its mirror\n  `project-template/docs/AGENTS.md` now document the mutating API, the async\n  202-then-poll/stream contract, the SSE endpoint, and the shipped\n  ProjectLocks boundary; `README.md`'s Listener section and command table\n  row updated the same way; `docs/listener.md`'s \"Sequencing after this job\"\n  marks job two as DONE with a short annotation (not a rewrite), including\n  the recorded decision that \"answering\" a `NEEDS-HUMAN-INPUT:` marker is the\n  composition of edit-brief + relaunch, not its own endpoint. The `mg serve`\n  help text no longer claims the API is read-only.\n\n## Known issues / follow-ups\n\n- **Test-environment note:** `internal/ui` and `cmd/mg` tests that shell out\n  to `git init` fail when the manigot session git shim is on PATH (it refuses\n  `git init`); they pass with a real-git-only PATH. This is a pre-existing\n  session-environment artifact, not a regression from this job.\n- The launch-jdi double-launch guard (409 on a running status sidecar) is\n  best-effort by design: two launches issued back-to-back before the first\n  process writes its status could both proceed. Documented, not solved —\n  the brief does not ask for real locking here.\n- A concurrently-running mg-jdi loop against the same job as a detached\n  agent launch writes to the same session.log (safe: append-only,\n  single-writer-per-section) but means two processes touch the same git\n  worktree — not detected or prevented (documented limitation of TASK-2).\n- The done endpoint's conflict path leaves the main worktree in its\n  conflicted-merge state by design; resolving it requires an explicit\n  follow-up (e.g. launching @git-solver via the agent endpoint, or `mg host`\n  from a terminal). The response says so explicitly rather than implying\n  nothing happened.","lineStart":1,"lineEnd":162,"totalLines":162,"truncated":false}},"title":"docs/jobs/farmer_part-2-of-web-ui-tui-path/implementation.md","time":{"start":1787952466961,"end":1787952466968}},"id":"prt_04a458bbf001lrKDCszP1HckBn","sessionID":"ses_fb5ba93f2ffe9RBBAgSFG35dIN","messageID":"msg_04a4586e6001uYK4U9UfM0DkJ3"}}
