{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1787952641555,"sessionID":"ses_fb5ba93f2ffe9RBBAgSFG35dIN","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"read","callID":"call_f1d738b1dcda4457974c675d","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"/workspace/src/cmd/mg/jdi.go","limit":120,"offset":540},"output":"<path>/workspace/src/cmd/mg/jdi.go</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n540: \t\t// One bounded context for this iteration's pre-agent git probes\n541: \t\t// (see jdiProbeTimeout): a hung git must stall one probe, not the\n542: \t\t// whole run. Each context is cancelled at its point of use — never\n543: \t\t// deferred, which would accumulate up to maxIterations timers until\n544: \t\t// Run returns. The post-agent stall probe gets its own fresh context\n545: \t\t// below: runner.Run is a minutes-long docker/LLM invocation, so a\n546: \t\t// context created here would be long expired by the time that probe\n547: \t\t// runs and would read \"\" for HEAD, silently defeating the stall\n548: \t\t// backstop.\n549: \t\tprobeCtx, cancelProbes := context.WithTimeout(context.Background(), jdiProbeTimeout)\n550: \n551: \t\tstageBefore := j.Stage()\n552: \t\trounds, _ := git.CountVerdictCommitsWithContext(probeCtx, root, j.Branch, j.ID)\n553: \t\ttipIsVerdict, _ := git.LatestCommitIsVerdictWithContext(probeCtx, root, j.Branch, j.ID)\n554: \t\tdecision := orchestrate.Next(stageBefore, rounds, tipIsVerdict)\n555: \n556: \t\tif decision.Kind != orchestrate.RunAgent {\n557: \t\t\tcancelProbes()\n558: \t\t\tincludeReason := true\n559: \t\t\tif !agentEverRan {\n560: \t\t\t\t// No agent has run yet this whole loop (typically the very\n561: \t\t\t\t// first iteration, e.g. an unwritten brief.md) — logInvocation\n562: \t\t\t\t// never gets a chance to run for this stop, which otherwise\n563: \t\t\t\t// leaves run.log at 0 bytes for a case that has just as much\n564: \t\t\t\t// reason to explain itself there as any agent invocation does.\n565: \t\t\t\tlogImmediateStop(log, decision.Reason)\n566: \t\t\t\tincludeReason = false\n567: \t\t\t}\n568: \t\t\treturn finish(decision.Kind, decision.Reason, includeReason)\n569: \t\t}\n570: \n571: \t\treport(job.JDIRunning, decision.Agent)\n572: \t\tattempts[decision.Agent]++\n573: \t\tlogAgentInvoked(log, decision.Agent, attempts[decision.Agent])\n574: \t\theadBefore, _ := git.HeadCommitWithContext(probeCtx, root, j.Branch)\n575: \t\tcancelProbes() // pre-agent probes done; the agent invocation below can take minutes\n576: \n577: \t\t// Session log: open the job's session.log and write the section\n578: \t\t// header (agent, attempt, timestamp) BEFORE the invocation, then\n579: \t\t// stream the invocation's raw bytes into it live as they arrive —\n580: \t\t// the runner tees through the live writer (see commandAgentRunner.\n581: \t\t// Run's io.MultiWriter), so a `tail -f` on the file sees the\n582: \t\t// blow-by-blow grow during the run instead of only after it\n583: \t\t// finishes. This replaces the old post-hoc appendSessionLog call.\n584: \t\t// The section is written for EVERY invocation, failed ones included,\n585: \t\t// so what an agent did during an unattended run survives. Best-effort:\n586: \t\t// an open failure only warns through the log writer (Run has no\n587: \t\t// stderr) and never aborts the loop — the invocation still runs and\n588: \t\t// its output still lands in run.log's summary; only this invocation's\n589: \t\t// verbose persistence is lost, and the next invocation retries the\n590: \t\t// open on its own.\n591: \t\tsess, sessErr := job.OpenSessionLog(filepath.Join(j.Dir, \"session.log\"), decision.Agent, attempts[decision.Agent])\n592: \t\tif sessErr != nil {\n593: \t\t\tfmt.Fprintf(log, \"mg jdi: warning: could not open session log: %v\\n\", sessErr)\n594: \t\t}\n595: \t\tlive := io.Writer(io.Discard)\n596: \t\tif sess != nil {\n597: \t\t\tlive = sess\n598: \t\t}\n599: \t\tout, runErr := runner.Run(decision.Agent, j, live)\n600: \t\tagentEverRan = true\n601: \t\tif sess != nil {\n602: \t\t\tif cerr := sess.Close(); cerr != nil {\n603: \t\t\t\tfmt.Fprintf(log, \"mg jdi: warning: could not finalize session log: %v\\n\", cerr)\n604: \t\t\t}\n605: \t\t}\n606: \n607: \t\t// Read the just-run agent's expected target file fresh off\n608: \t\t// disk, so logInvocation can skip re-printing output that's already\n609: \t\t// the same as what got written to the job's own markdown file. Safe\n610: \t\t// to read directly from j.Dir — see orchestrate.AgentTargetFile's own\n611: \t\t// doc. A missing/unreadable file (targetContent left \"\") just means\n612: \t\t// logInvocation's dedup check never matches, which is the same as\n613: \t\t// not having this feature at all for that call.\n614: \t\tvar targetContent string\n615: \t\ttargetFile := orchestrate.AgentTargetFile[decision.Agent]\n616: \t\tif targetFile != \"\" {\n617: \t\t\tif data, rerr := os.ReadFile(filepath.Join(j.Dir, targetFile)); rerr == nil {\n618: \t\t\t\ttargetContent = string(data)\n619: \t\t\t}\n620: \t\t}\n621: \n622: \t\t// Fan-out: log gets the same formatted section\n623: \t\t// regardless of whether it's os.Stdout, the sidecar run.log, or (in\n624: \t\t// tests) an in-memory buffer — see main()'s io.MultiWriter and\n625: \t\t// jdioutput.go's logInvocation. DetectSignal below scans the raw bytes\n626: \t\t// directly (it does its own JSON extraction), independent of what\n627: \t\t// logInvocation writes for a human to read.\n628: \t\tlogInvocation(log, decision.Agent, attempts[decision.Agent], out, targetFile, targetContent)\n629: \n630: \t\tif sig, ok := orchestrate.DetectSignal(out); ok {\n631: \t\t\tlastAgent = decision.Agent\n632: \t\t\treturn finish(orchestrate.StopNeedsHuman, fmt.Sprintf(\"%s asked for human input: %s\", decision.Agent, sig.Reason), true)\n633: \t\t}\n634: \t\tif runErr != nil {\n635: \t\t\tlastAgent = decision.Agent\n636: \t\t\treturn finish(orchestrate.StopNeedsHuman, fmt.Sprintf(\"%s invocation failed: %v\", decision.Agent, runErr), true)\n637: \t\t}\n638: \n639: \t\t// Stall backstop: the same agent invoked twice in a\n640: \t\t// row, each time persisting nothing at all (neither Stage() nor the\n641: \t\t// branch HEAD moved across that invocation), stops on the second\n642: \t\t// such occurrence — a single no-op invocation is tolerated (it may\n643: \t\t// be transient), but two in a row for the same agent means it's\n644: \t\t// genuinely stuck, regardless of whether the NEEDS-HUMAN-INPUT\n645: \t\t// marker was present.\n646: \t\tstageAfter := j.Stage()\n647: \t\t// Fresh context for the post-agent probe: the pre-agent probeCtx\n648: \t\t// above was cancelled before runner.Run and would in any case be\n649: \t\t// long expired after a minutes-long invocation — reusing it would\n650: \t\t// make this probe fail instantly and read \"\", which noChange would\n651: \t\t// take as a HEAD change and the stall backstop would never fire.\n652: \t\tprobeAfterCtx, cancelProbeAfter := context.WithTimeout(context.Background(), jdiProbeTimeout)\n653: \t\theadAfter, _ := git.HeadCommitWithContext(probeAfterCtx, root, j.Branch)\n654: \t\tcancelProbeAfter()\n655: \t\tnoChange := stageAfter == stageBefore && headAfter == headBefore\n656: \n657: \t\tif noChange && lastNoChange && decision.Agent == lastAgent {\n658: \t\t\treturn finish(orchestrate.StopNeedsHuman, fmt.Sprintf(\"%s made no progress on two consecutive runs (Stage and branch HEAD unchanged both times) — stopping rather than looping indefinitely\", decision.Agent), true)\n659: \t\t}\n\n(Showing lines 540-659 of 664. Use offset=660 to continue.)\n</content>","metadata":{"preview":"\t\t// One bounded context for this iteration's pre-agent git probes\n\t\t// (see jdiProbeTimeout): a hung git must stall one probe, not the\n\t\t// whole run. Each context is cancelled at its point of use — never\n\t\t// deferred, which would accumulate up to maxIterations timers until\n\t\t// Run returns. The post-agent stall probe gets its own fresh context\n\t\t// below: runner.Run is a minutes-long d